{"raw": {"https://apply.interfolio.com/143792": {"title": "Faculty Fellow", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus Location: Norman, OK, United States Reporting to the Associate Director of Residence Life in the Residential Colleges, Faculty Fellows will be responsible for providing students with exceptional residential academic experiences and enhancing OU\u2019s residential community with an emphasis in student development, retention, and sense of belonging among residents of the Residential Colleges, Couch Center, Walker Center, Cross Village, and David L Boren Hall. The Department of Housing and Residence Life at The University of Oklahoma (OU) invites applications for Faculty Fellow positions beginning July 2024. **The successful candidate must be an active Faculty member at the University of Oklahoma** Of particular interest are candidates who are committed to making a difference in society through academic excellence. Responsibilities include: Plan, organize, and facilitate at least 1 major event per semester geared towards the academic and/or personal development mission of the Residential Life team. These would generally take place in in the evening, M-Th, and would run approximately 1-1.5 hours.\u00a0These are flexible - you could be the main presenter (e.g., about your work/discipline) or you could be a part of a panel.\u00a0 You could bring in someone else to give a presentation.\u00a0 There is a myriad of possibilities. Attend and support the events put on by the other Faculty Fellows and major events planned by the Faculty in Residence team. (About 1-2 events per semester) Attend planning lunches every other week with the Faculty in Residence team and other Faculty Fellows. Have a presence in assigned residential community, including dining facilities. ***There will be a $1250 stipend a semester for the Fall and Spring Semesters*** Tools/Technical Skills: To perform this job successfully an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. Incumbents will be evaluated, in part, based on performance of each essential function. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. Technology Requirements: Faculty Fellows are required to have an adequate computer to handle an electronic learning platform, keyboard, pointing device, and computer monitor. All other tools are knowledge-based or training-based through the University of Oklahoma. The Faculty Member must be able to use Microsoft Office products (MS Word, MS Excel, and MS PowerPoint), zip software, and Adobe Acrobat and must have reliable Internet access as well as alternate access in cases where primary access becomes unavailable. The use of Canvas will be required. Training is available through the University. Licensing/Certification: Faculty Members must be appropriately credentialed, possess an earned degree degree from an accredited institution or recognized by a country's ministry of education. Faculty Fellows must maintain their licenses and any appropriate certifications to continue to teach at OU and serve as a Faculty Fellow. Faculty Fellows are also expected to stay current with research by reviewing articles, journals, and presentations. Faculty Fellows cannot be on sabbatical during their time in the role. EDUCATION AND/OR EXPERIENCE : \u00b7 Earned degree from an accredited program. \u00b7 Been employed as full-time faculty for a minimum of one full academic year before July 1, 2024. \u00b7 Minimum of three years of experience teaching and mentoring/ \u00b7 Interest in establishing a community of students. \u00b7 Ability to provide leadership and support in program assessment. \u00b7 Ability to assume a significant role in addressing student questions and concerns from the community. \u00b7 Commitment to the goals of diversity and social change. \u00b7 Ability to work with non-traditional students. Required Skills : \u00b7 Strong interpersonal skills \u00b7 Good writing skills \u00b7 Dedication to students\u2019 success and high standards of teaching \u00b7 Evidence of effective teaching skills and online communication \u00b7 Demonstrated ability to collaborate with colleagues. Required Applicant Documents: 1. Curriculum Vita 2. Cover Letter Sample Instruction to Apply: **The successful candidate must be an active Faculty member at the University of Oklahoma** Consideration of applications will begin immediately.\u00a0 The position will remain open until filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/165436": {"title": "Faculty Fellow", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus Location: Norman, Oklahoma Position Title: Faculty Fellow Location: Norman, Oklahoma Position Type: Supplemental pay Position Position Description: Reporting to the Associate Director of Residence Life in the Residential Colleges, Faculty Fellows will be responsible for providing students with exceptional residential academic experiences and enhancing OU\u2019s residential community with an emphasis in student development, retention, and sense of belonging among residents of the Residential Colleges, McCasland Hall, Couch Center, Walker Center, Cross Village, and David L Boren Hall. The Department of Housing and Residence Life at The University of Oklahoma (OU) invites applications for Faculty Fellow positions beginning July 2025. Of particular interest are candidates who are committed to making a difference in society through academic excellence. Responsibilities include: 1. Plan, organize, and facilitate at least 1 major event per semester geared towards the academic and/or personal development mission of the Residential Life team. a. These would generally take place in in the evening, M-Th, and would run approximately 1-1.5 hours. These are flexible - you could be the main presenter (e.g., about your work/discipline) or you could be a part of a panel. You could bring in someone else to give a presentation. There is a myriad of possibilities. 2. Attend and support the events put on by the other Faculty Fellows and major events planned by the Faculty in Residence team. (About 1-2 events per semester) 3. Attend planning lunches with the Faculty in Residence team and other Faculty Fellows. 4. Have a presence in assigned residential community, including dining facilities. Tools/Technical Skills: To perform this job successfully an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. Incumbents will be evaluated, in part, based on performance of each essential function. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. Technology Requirements: Faculty Fellows are required to have an adequate computer to handle an electronic learning platform, keyboard, pointing device, and computer monitor. All other tools are knowledge-based or training-based through the University of Oklahoma. The Faculty Member must be able to use Microsoft Office products (MS Word, MS Excel, and MS PowerPoint), zip software, and Adobe Acrobat and must have reliable Internet access as well as alternate access in cases where primary access becomes unavailable. The use of Canvas will be required. Training is available through the University. Licensing/Certification: Faculty Members must be appropriately credentialed, possess an earned degree from an accredited institution or recognized by a country's ministry of education. Faculty Fellows must maintain their licenses and any appropriate certifications to continue to teach at OU and serve as a Faculty Fellow. Faculty Fellows are also expected to stay current with research by reviewing articles, journals, and presentations. Faculty Fellows cannot be on sabbatical during their time in the role. EDUCATION AND/OR EXPERIENCE: \u00b7 Been employed as full-time faculty for a minimum of one full academic year before July 1, 2025. \u00b7 Minimum of three years of experience teaching and mentoring. \u00b7 Interest in establishing a community of students. \u00b7 Ability to provide leadership and support in program assessment. \u00b7 Ability to assume a significant role in addressing student questions and concerns from the community. \u00b7 Commitment to the goals of diversity and social change. \u00b7 Ability to work with non-traditional students. Minimum Qualifications: 1. Earned degree from an accredited program 2. Strong interpersonal skills 3. Good writing skills 4. Dedication to students\u2019 success and high standards of teaching 5. Evidence of effective teaching skills and online communication 6. Demonstrated ability to collaborate with colleagues Application Instructions Required Applicant Documents: 1. Curriculum Vita 2. Cover Letter Sample Instruction to Apply: Consideration of applications will begin immediately. The position will remain open until filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/174012": {"title": "Endowed Chair \u2013 George Lynn Cross Chair in Microbiome-microbiome interactions", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus Location: University of Oklahoma-Norman Campus The School of Biological Sciences (SBS) at the University of Oklahoma (OU) invites application for a full-time 9-month tenured or tenure-track position Associate or full professor with a start date of August 2026, and will maintain a 1:0 teaching load as a condition of being appointed to the endowed chair position. We seek an innovative, creative, and collaborative colleague with a record of leading scholarship in microbiome-microbiome interactions who will bring a highly productive, cross-disciplinary, and externally funded program of scholarship to OU. The ideal candidate will have demonstrated success in using a combination of empirical and computational approaches to problems in metabolic processes in areas such as regulation of the inflammation, bacterial pathogenesis, and microbiome-immune system interactions. Further, the successful candidate will have: (1) a record of forming and leading productive interdisciplinary or transdisciplinary teams that work on convergent approaches to solving complex and pressing biomedical problems associated with disease; (2) successfully built collaborations within and outside their institution; and (3) mentored research trainees and taught courses related to infectious disease dynamics. The SBS was recently established through the integration of the departments of Biology and Microbiology and Plant Biology to align graduate programs and research with faculty strengths and the OU Norman Research Strategic Verticals and Lead On Strategic Plan . OU invests in its faculty by providing support and resources through the Center for Faculty Excellence and Vice President for Research and Partnerships Office . The Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences supports faculty development through mentoring, new faculty orientation series, and access to research and educational support. We encourage candidates to apply who are seeking to work in a rapidly growing college with collegial interdisciplinary groups and strong academic units. Required and Preferred Qualifications Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in microbiology or a related discipline. Established record of forming and leading research teams. Sustained record of high-quality research and publications. Demonstrated record of sustained nationally competitive extramural funding. Familiarity with effective teaching practices and mentoring approaches thatsupport students from a wide range of backgrounds. Demonstrated experience in convergent and interdisciplinary or transdisciplinaryapproaches to solving complex societal problems. Preferred Qualifications: Record of scholarship sufficient for appointment as an Associate or Full Professor Demonstrated record of excellent in teaching. Evidence of ability to build partnerships within and across institutions Application Instructions Applicants should submit 1) a cover letter describing their interest in the position and their fit in the School of Biological Sciences The cover letter may address examples of strategies for broadening access.; 2) a full curriculum vitae; 3) a research portfolio describing your current and future scholarship, consisting of up to a three-page narrative and up to three representative publications; 4) a teaching portfolio detailing your experience and approach to classroom teaching and student mentorship, including your approach to classroom teaching and advising undergraduate and graduate students, with up to a two-page narrative and optional syllabus for a course you are interested in teaching; 5) names and contact information for three confidential letters of recommendation.; 6)Additional materials may be requested at a later date. Applications must be submitted online via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/XXXXX The search committee will begin reviewing applications on October 30, 2024 and will continue until the position is filled. Inquiries should be directed to: Dr. Jeff Kelly, Director, School of Biological Sciences University of Oklahoma jkelly@ou.edu University of Oklahoma The University of Oklahoma (OU) is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement, serving the educational, cultural, economic, and healthcare needs of the state, region, and nation from three campuses: Norman, Health Sciences Center in Oklahoma City and the Schusterman Center in Tulsa. OU enrolls over 32,000 students and has more than 2,700 full-time faculty members in 21 colleges. The Norman Community Norman is a vibrant university town of more than 130,000 inhabitants with a growing entertainment and art scene. With outstanding schools, amenities, and a low cost of living, Norman is a perennial contender in \u201cbest place to live\u201d rankings. Visit http://soonerway.ou.edu for more information. Within an easy commute, Oklahoma City features a dynamic economy and outstanding cultural venues adding to the region\u2019s growing appeal."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/182493": {"title": "General Manager of Student Broadcast Media & Journalism Instructor", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus Location: Norman The Gaylord College of Journalism and Mass Communication at the University of Oklahoma seeks to fill a renewable-term instructor position who will also serve as General Manager of Student Broadcast Media to begin in Fall 2026.\u00a0\u00a0The initial appointment is for one year. Gaylord College, the number one media program in the nation as ranked by the Broadcast Education Association, is looking for an individual with high professional and ethical standards who can serve as general manager of the College\u2019s fast growing broadcast area that helps prepare students (in and out of the classroom) for careers in broadcast news and related content generation and streaming opportunities. This is a teaching position with administrative duties. In this role, the general manager will provide a faculty bridge by supervising and coordinating all the operations staff in editorial, production, engineering and sports related to the creation of our award-winning student newscast and other broadcast programming.\u00a0\u00a0This position will also be the key liaison with and part of the Journalism faculty. The person we hire will be prepared to help the program evolve with digital and other technological changes unfolding in the broadcast industry, create new programming/streaming opportunities for the growing number of students, as well as forge new partnerships with industry. In sum, this position will creatively push the boundaries of media innovation to help make sure our students are future-proof and industry-ready. Candidates will be able to teach basic and advanced courses dealing with newsgathering and storytelling for television, TV news producing and management, and to oversee participation in student award competitions. A Master\u2019s degree and significant professional experience in broadcast journalism are required. Successful experience in network or local television news operations, news management roles, and a track record of adopting and innovating new media concepts are preferred. Experience teaching broadcast journalism is a plus. Applications for the search will be reviewed beginning March 20, 2026 and will be accepted until the position is filled. Applicants should provide 1) a cover letter describing their qualifications and their vision and plans for this position, 2) a curriculum vitae, and 3) names, addresses, phone numbers, and email of at least three references. For specific questions about the position and application process, please contact search committee chair Dr. Robert Kerr at rkerr@ou.edu. Applications should be submitted online at apply.interfolio.com/182493"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193903": {"title": "STRATEGIC COMMUNICATION STUDENT AGENCY ADVISOR", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus Location: Norman The Gaylord College of Journalism and Mass Communication at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for a full-time strategic communication student agency advisor to provide hands-on instruction, mentorship, and senior leadership for Lindsey + Asp - a student-managed, professionally led agency. The appointment will begin in January 2027. Founded in 2009, Lindsey + Asp has provided hundreds of students with hands-on, real-world agency experience helping clients solve their strategic communication needs. Over the years, the student agency has developed creative work for national clients including American Airlines, the U.S. Navy, Carnival Corporation, Spotify, Dress for Success. The agency is a nationally recognized part of Gaylord\u2019s top-ranked strategic communication program and is helping students translate their portfolio of Lindsey + Asp work and experience into jobs throughout the U.S. The successful candidate will have a track record of building and leading teams with demonstrated oversight responsibility and/or experience in these areas on the agency and/or client side: strategic communication, marketing, research and insights, digital and social marketing, branding, advertising, public relations, video content creation, new business acquisition and client management. The student agency advisor will serve as the overall leader of the agency and will be responsible for business development, mentoring, coaching, and supervising student leaders and student members. The successful candidate should have a minimum of 10 years of progressively responsible professional experience. The agency faculty advisor will be responsible for building an agency strategic plan, including a comprehensive new business plan, and for working with the students to develop and maintain a strong, differentiated agency brand. Two of the most critical responsibilities of the agency faculty advisor will be building and sustaining a strong client roster and developing the students who serve those clients. The advisor will formulate and execute an ongoing business development strategy to identify, pursue, and secure new client engagements while cultivating relationships with existing clients. Equally important will be the recruitment, onboarding, training, mentorship, and professional development of agency members, utilizing college, alumni, and external industry resources. The advisor will be expected to build a robust network of Gaylord alumni and other industry professionals to support student development and create internship and post-graduation employment opportunities. The advisor will also facilitate connections between students and external resources in the development and activation of ongoing research and insights initiatives. The agency faculty advisor will be expected to forge alliances with other colleges and programs on campus and throughout the U.S., build strong relationships with relevant industry trade organizations like the AAF, PRSA, AMA, and the strategic communication community at-large to further advance relations between academia and industry. Service may also include activities such as student recruitment, student introduction to industry contacts and career placement, student co-curricular activities (Ad Club, PRSSA, NSAC), college and university committees, and service to the profession and public. The agency advisor will also be expected to teach a minimum of two classes per semester beyond agency faculty advisor responsibilities. Key performance indicators (KPIs) may include but not be limited to: \u00b7 Creating a well differentiated brand and unique selling proposition for the agency . \u2022 Agency Brand & Market Positioning: Develop and maintain a differentiated agency brand, positioning, and value proposition that strengthens Lindsey + Asp\u2019s visibility \u2022 New Business Development & Client Acquisition: Proactively identify, solicit, pitch, and secure new client engagements that build and strengthen the agency\u2019s client roster. \u2022 Client Retention & Relationship Development: Cultivate and maintain strong relationships with existing clients to encourage retention, repeat engagements, expanded opportunities, and positive long-term partnerships. \u2022 Student Recruitment & Agency Participation: Build awareness of the agency and successfully recruit talented students across relevant disciplines to maintain a strong, engaged agency workforce. \u2022 Student Onboarding, Training & Professional Development: Develop and maintain a structured onboarding and ongoing training program that prepares agency members to meet professional standards and grow their skills throughout their agency experience. \u2022 Research Activation & Industry Thought Leadership: Leverage the agency\u2019s proprietary research and insights across reports, presentations, digital and social content, industry outreach, media opportunities, and other channels to build the agency\u2019s visibility, credibility, and reputation as a source of youth-culture intelligence. \u2022 Financial Management & Agency Sustainability: Effectively manage agency revenue, expenses, client billing, and financial performance to support responsible operations and long-term sustainability. A master\u2019s degree and a minimum of 10 years of progressively responsible professional experience are required. While not required, candidates with a Ph.D. or other terminal degree are encouraged to apply. The position requires scheduling flexibility, including evening and other non-traditional hours, to provide effective oversight, mentorship, and support for student agency operations. Applications for the search will be reviewed beginning October 15, 2026, and will be accepted until the position is filled. Applicants should provide 1) a letter describing their qualifications and their vision and plans for this position, 2) a complete curriculum vitae, 3) access to an online portfolio of work if applicable, and 4) names, addresses, and telephone numbers of at least three references. Applications should be uploaded to Interfolio ( apply.interfolio.com/193903 ). Applicants with questions can reach out to the search committee chair, Debbie Yount (debbie.yount@ou.edu)."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/112293": {"title": "Lecturer or Instructor in Finance", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Michael F. Price College of Business: Division of Finance  Location: Norman The Finance Division of the Price College of Business, University of Oklahoma, invites applications for a full-time position as Lecturer or Instructor in Finance. To be considered for appointment as Lecturer, candidates must have the Ph.D. in Finance or a closely related discipline and an extensive track record of teaching excellence. Candidates who do not have the Ph.D. must have the M.B.A. or other graduate degree in Finance or a closely related discipline and will be considered for appointment as Instructor. We invite applications from candidates in all areas of finance. Candidates who have teaching experience in real estate, wealth management, asset management, and/or FinTech, are especially encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will be expected to teach courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Senior-level candidates may be considered for appointment as Senior Lecturer/Instructor or Distinguished Lecturer/Instructor. Salary and benefits will be highly competitive. To be considered for appointment as Lecturer, candidates must have the Ph.D. in Finance or a closely related discipline and an extensive track record of teaching excellence. Candidates who do not have the Ph.D. must have the M.B.A. or other graduate degree in Finance or a closely related discipline and will be considered for appointment as Instructor. We invite applications from candidates in all areas of finance. Candidates who have teaching experience in finanical modeling, wealth management, and/or real estate topics are especially encouraged to apply. Applications should be submitted online via Interfolio at http://apply.interfolio.com/112293 . Applications will be accepted until the position is filled and should be received as early as possible to maximize chances of selection. Applicants should upload a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, evidence of teaching effectiveness, and names and contact information of three references to. Inquiries should be addressed to Patricia Saltus, Lead Administrative Support Specialist, Finance Division, Michael F. Price College of Business, Suite 3250 Adams Hall, University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK 73019; finance@ou.edu . Tel. 405-325-5591."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/171834": {"title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Finance", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Michael F. Price College of Business: Division of Finance  Location: Norman, OK The Finance Division of the Price College of Business, University of Oklahoma, invites applications for two full-time tenure-track positions as Assistant or Associate Professor of Finance beginning August 2027. To be considered for these positions, candidates must have, or reasonably expect to have by August 15, 2027, the Ph.D. in Finance or a closely related discipline. Candidates who have served a minimum of three years in a tenure-track position at an AACSB-accredited business school prior to the expected appointment date and have publications in top-tier journals are especially encouraged to apply. The successful candidates will have strong research credentials and excellent teaching skills. While we will accept applications from qualified candidates with interests in all areas of finance, we especially invite applicants specializing in corporate finance, banking, household finance and/or real estate. Candidates with demonstrated excellence in research, as evidenced by a strong track record of publications in top-tier journals, could be considered for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor. Salary and benefits will be highly competitive. To be considered for these positions, candidates must have, or reasonably expect to have by August 15, 2027, the Ph.D. in Finance or a closely related discipline. Candidates who have served a minimum of three years in a tenure-track position at an AACSB-accredited business school prior to the expected appointment date and have publications in top-tier journals are especially encouraged to apply. The successful candidates will have strong research credentials and excellent teaching skills. While we will accept applications from qualified candidates with interests in all areas of finance, we especially invite applicants specializing in corporate finance, banking, household finance, and/or real estate. Candidates with demonstrated excellence in research, as evidenced by a strong track record of publications in top-tier journals, could be considered for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor. Applications will be accepted until the positions are filled and should be received as early as possible to maximize chances of selection. Applicants should upload a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, up to three working papers and/or publications, and evidence of teaching effectiveness to https://apply.interfolio.com/171834 and arrange for three letters of recommendation to be confidentially submitted to this website. Inquiries should be addressed to Patricia Yeo Saltus, Finance Division, Michael F. Price College of Business, Suite 3250 Adams Hall, University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK 73019; finance@ou.edu . Tel. 405-325-5591."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195272": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Visual Arts - European Art History Before 1800", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Weitzenhoffer Family College of Fine Arts: School of Visual Arts Location: Norman, OK The OU School of Visual Arts at the University of Oklahoma extends an opportunity for a dynamic scholar to launch a significant career in European Art History. This job call is open to candidates in any area of European Art before 1800, including but not limited to Medieval, Renaissance, Baroque, and Early Modern Art. We are especially interested in candidates whose research and teaching complements our program\u2019s strengths in European Art History, Native American Art History, and Art of the American West. The 2/2 course load will encompass teaching at all levels in the program, including seminars supporting the MA program and PhD track in European Art History, advanced undergraduate courses of the candidate\u2019s choosing, Introduction to Art History and Introduction to European Art, and the possibility to teach on our Museum Studies certificate if they wish. The tenure-track position, starting on August 16, 2027, comes with a competitive salary, generous start-up funding, and strong research support. OU\u2019s Fred Jones Jr. Museum of Art and Sam Noble Museum of Natural History provide world-class resources for teaching and research in art history, and the Special Research Collections at OU\u2019s Bizzell Library contain important medieval and early modern materials including the History of Science Collections. Important research collections nearby include the Oklahoma City Museum of Art (OKC), the Philbrook Art Museum (Tulsa), the Nelson-Atkins Museum of Art (Kansas City), the Museum of Fine Arts (Houston), and the Kimbell Art Museum (Fort Worth). \u25aa Ph.D. in Art History or related field in hand by July 1, 2027 \u25aa university-level teaching experience \u25aa record of innovative research A complete application must include: a curriculum vitae ; a letter of application (cover letter); one or more writing samples (article, book chapter, dissertation chapter); and the names of three references with current contact information. Review of completed applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries should be directed to the chair of the search committee, Erin Duncan-O\u2019Neill, at erinduncanoneill@ou.edu. Applications can be submitted electronically through Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/195272 . For additional information, please call (405) 325-2691 or visit the OU SoVA home page, https://www.ou.edu/finearts/visual-arts ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192986": {"title": "Research Scientist at the Laboratories of Molecular Anthropology and Microbiome Research (LMAMR)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Anthropology Location: University of Oklahoma We are seeking a highly motivated, PhD-level scientist to serve as a Research Scientist at the Laboratories of Molecular Anthropology and Microbiome Research. This role is ideal for a researcher who enjoys balancing hands-on experimental work with data analysis, academic writing, and mentoring trainees, and who thrives in a collaborative, research environment. The Laboratories of Molecular Anthropology and Microbiome Research (LMAMR- lmamr.org ) represent an interdisciplinary partnership that seeks to understand biocultural, ecological and evolutionary dimensions of life. We study biomolecules retrieved from the following: extant, forensic, historical, archaeological, and environmental materials, with special attention to humans and human-associated organisms including commensals and pathogens. Our mission includes innovation in research and commitment to training in molecular and systems biology approaches, with a collaborative effort towards conservation, cultural respect, community engagement and outreach, both regional and international. Duties: Collaborate with PIs and project technicians on study design and data generation. Conduct bioinformatic analyses on ancient and modern genomic datasets Write manuscripts in support of the LMAMR projects Mentor undergraduate, graduate and visiting students Work closely with the PIs and project stakeholders on collaborative activities with tribal communities and resource managers Contribute to the LMAMR community Co-write grants for additional funding and support Participate in science communication endeavors Travel for project meetings/sample collection as needed Education: Requir ed Ph.D in Life Sciences or Computer Science (preferably Genomics, Evolutionary Biology, Computational Biology and/or Conservation Biology) ** or Anthropology with genomics experience Skills: * Strong background in population genetics and bioinformatics * Experience analyzing whole genome datasets * Experience with ancient DNA or degraded DNA (not required but useful) The ideal Research Scientist will be interested in strengthening their skills in informatics, student mentorship, and paleogenomics. We encourage you to apply even if you do not meet all the preferred qualifications in the job posting. We value the skills and perspectives that scientists from a variety of backgrounds bring, as they can drive our lab\u2019s research in innovative and exciting directions. Interested candidates should reach out to Courtney Hofman, courtney.hofman@ou.edu with questions and submit their CV and cover letter via Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com/192986"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193521": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate of Anthropology", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Anthropology Location: University of Oklahoma, Norman Campus   The Department of Anthropology and the Laboratories of Molecular Anthropology and Microbiome Research (LMAMR) at the University of Oklahoma invite applications for a Postdoctoral Researcher to join the research group of Dr. Lilly Parker. This position is ideal for a researcher who enjoys balancing hands-on experimental work with data analysis, academic writing, and mentoring trainees, and who thrives in a collaborative research environment. The successful candidate will contribute to research in evolutionary, conservation, and ancient/anthropological genomics. The initial work will focus primarily on cuscuses, including the following: questions involving diversification, systematics, biogeography, conservation, and the evolutionary consequences of intentional translocations. The researcher will also have opportunities to contribute to projects involving shrews and other mammalian systems and to develop research questions that align with their interests. LMAMR is an interdisciplinary research and training environment focused on the biocultural, ecological, and evolutionary dimensions of life. Researchers in the laboratories study biomolecules recovered from modern, forensic, historical, archaeological, and environmental materials. Our mission includes innovation in research and commitment to training in molecular and systems biology approaches, with a collaborative effort towards conservation, cultural respect, community engagement and outreach, both regional and international. Research in the Parker group examines how environmental change and human activities have shaped animal populations through time. This is a combined wet-laboratory and computational position. The successful candidate will be expected to conduct wet-lab work, including work in the ancient DNA laboratory. Candidates must either have relevant laboratory experience or be willing and motivated to learn these methods. Training will be provided as needed. Duties: Conduct wet-lab and computational genomic research, with an initial focus on cuscuses Generate and analyze genomic data from modern, museum, historical, archaeological, and other degraded biological materials Work in LMAMR\u2019s ancient DNA laboratory Prepare manuscripts and contribute to collaborative publications Mentor undergraduate, graduate, and visiting students Collaborate with Dr. Parker, other LMAMR researchers, museum researchers, conservation partners, and project stakeholders Develop grant proposals jointly with Dr. Parker and help shape new research directions Participate in science communication and outreach Travel for project meetings, sample collection, and conferences as needed Research Environment and Mentoring: The postdoctoral researcher will be based in LMAMR at the University of Oklahoma in Norman and will have access to laboratory and computational resources for genomic research. The postdoc will work closely with Dr. Parker in developing a mentoring plan tailored to their experience and career goals. Mentoring may include laboratory and computational training, manuscript development, student mentorship, grant writing, project development, and preparation for careers in academia, museums, conservation, government, or other research settings. The position offers substantial opportunities for intellectual independence. Existing datasets and projects will support immediate research and publication, while the postdoctoral researcher will also be encouraged to help develop new projects and collaborative grant proposals. Appointment: This is a full-time postdoctoral appointment at the University of Oklahoma. Salary will be commensurate with experience and will include University benefits. The initial appointment will be for one year, with the possibility of renewal based on satisfactory progress and the availability of funding. The anticipated start date is flexible. Education: Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Life Sciences or Computer Science (preferably Evolutionary Biology, Molecular Ecology, Genomics, Bioinformatics, and/or Conservation Biology) or Anthropology with genomics experience. Because the position includes both laboratory and computational research, applicants must be willing to conduct wet-lab work and have some experience with laboratory protocols (e.g. DNA extraction, QC procedures, and library preparation). Prior ancient-DNA experience is welcome but is not required. Applicants without this experience must be interested in learning relevant laboratory methods, including procedures for working with ancient and degraded DNA. Training will be provided as needed. Competitive applicants will have experience in one or more of the following areas: Strong background in bioinformatics workflows Population, evolutionary, or conservation genomics Analysis of high-throughput sequencing data Linux or Unix computing environments R, Python, or other computational tools Molecular laboratory methods Museum genomics, ancient DNA, degraded DNA, or low-coverage genomic data Phylogenetics, phylogenomics, systematics, or biogeographic modeling Mammalian evolutionary biology or conservation High-performance computing Applicants are not expected to have experience in every method or research area listed above. Candidates with complementary backgrounds who want to develop additional laboratory, genomic, computational, or museum-based research skills are strongly encouraged to apply. We value the skills and perspectives that scientists from a variety of backgrounds bring, as they can drive our lab\u2019s research in innovative and exciting directions. Questions about the position should be directed to Dr. Lilly Parker at lilly.parker@ou.edu . Applicants should submit the following materials via Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com/193521 A curriculum vitae A cover letter describing their research background, interests, and interest in the position Names and contact information for two professional references The cover letter should briefly describe the applicant\u2019s previous research experience, relevant laboratory and/or computational experience, and the questions or skills they would like to develop during the appointment. Applicants are not expected to propose a fully developed independent project. Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026 and continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195695": {"title": "Research Coordinator of Anthropology", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Anthropology Location: Laboratories of Molecular Anthropology and Microbiome Reseach We are seeking a Research Coordinator at the Laboratories of Molecular Anthropology and Microbiome Research. The ideal candidate for this position will have laboratory experience in sample preparation for high-throughput DNA sequencing and be interested in contributing to funded research projects on mammal and sedimentary DNA. The Laboratories of Molecular Anthropology and Microbiome Research (LMAMR- lmamr.org) represent an interdisciplinary partnership that seeks to understand biocultural, ecological and evolutionary dimensions of life. We study biomolecules retrieved from the following: extant, forensic, historical, archaeological, and environmental materials, with special attention to humans and human-associated organisms including commensals and pathogens. Our mission includes innovation in research and commitment to training in molecular and systems biology approaches, with a collaborative effort towards conservation, cultural respect, community engagement and outreach, both regional and international. Duties: Collaborate with PIs, lab manager and postdocs on study design and implementation Perform standardized laboratory protocols for sample processing and analysis Manage sample inventories and associated metadata throughout sample collection, laboratory processing, and long-term storage Collect, organize, manage, and analyze research data and metadata Prepare reports summarizing results of experiments or conclusions of research projects Help maintain the laboratories Keep detailed records of all work-related activities Travel for project meetings/sample collection as needed Identify supplies and reagents that need to be purchased Perform various duties as needed to successfully complete research project objectives Contribute to the collaborative and supportive LMAMR community Participate in science communication and outreach endeavors Education: Required Bachelor\u2019s degree. MA/MS in Life Sciences or Anthropology with genomics experience preferred. Two years of experience in lieu of Master\u2019s degree. Skills: * Experience with DNA extraction, library preparation & high throughput sequencing. * Experience with ancient DNA, eDNA, SedaDNA or degraded DNA (not required but useful). * Experience with project management. The ideal Research Coordinator will be interested in contributing to ongoing research projects in collaborative teams. We encourage you to apply even if you do not meet all the preferred qualifications in the job posting. We value the skills and perspectives that scientists from a variety of backgrounds bring, as they can drive our lab\u2019s research in innovative and exciting directions. Interested candidates should reach out to Courtney Hofman, courtney.hofman@ou.edu with questions and submit their CV and cover letter via Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com/195695"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/177567": {"title": "Professor of Classics and Letters- Sanders Chair in Law and Liberty (Tenured)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Classics and Letters Location: Department of Classics and Letters The Institute for the American Constitutional Heritage (IACH) and Department of Classics and Letters at the University of Oklahoma seek to recruit a dynamic leader and accomplished scholar for the endowed Sanders\u00a0Chair in Law and Liberty, who will also serve as the Director of the IACH. Established in 2009, the IACH has become a premier civic education program, whose mission is focused on teaching undergraduates, nurturing outstanding scholarship, and engaging the public with a variety of enriching programs. The next Sanders\u00a0Chair will have substantial opportunity to develop the IACH\u2019s vision,\u00a0advance its mission, and increase its impact. Subfields of interest include but are not limited to: American law and constitutionalism from the eighteenth-century to present; the historical and ideological background of the American founding; the history of republicanism and democratic theory, etc. Applications are welcome from scholars in fields such as History, Political Science, Law, and Philosophy. Experience in building interdisciplinary academic programs, developing impactful public-facing events, and fundraising are desirable. The successful candidate will be hired at the\u00a0Full Professor rank in the Department of Classics and Letters starting August 2026 or January 2027. This position will have a 1:1 teaching load while holding the Endowed Chair and Director position. For questions or inquiries, please contact Kyle Harper at kyleharper@ou.edu . Advanced degree in relevant field Experience and accomplishment commensurate with full professor rank Candidates should submit a letter of interest, CV, sample publication, and contact information for three confidential letters of recommendation, via Interfolio (https://apply.interfolio.com/177567). Review of applications will begin on January 15, 2026, and continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192547": {"title": "Director of Institute for the Constitutional Heritage and Sanders Chair in Law and Liberty in Classics and Letters (Professor)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Classics and Letters Location: Norman, OK The Institute for the American Constitutional Heritage (IACH) and Department of Classics and Letters at the University of Oklahoma seek to recruit a dynamic leader and accomplished scholar for the endowed Sanders\u00a0Chair in Law and Liberty, who will also serve as the Director of the IACH. Established in 2009, the IACH has become a premier civic education program, whose mission is focused on teaching undergraduates, nurturing outstanding scholarship, and engaging the public with a variety of enriching programs. The next Sanders Chair will have substantial opportunity to develop the IACH\u2019s vision, advance its mission, and increase its impact. Subfields of interest include but are not limited to: American law and constitutionalism from the eighteenth-century to present; the historical and ideological background of the American founding; the history of republicanism and democratic theory, etc. Applications are welcome from scholars in fields such as History, Political Science, Law, and Philosophy. Experience in building the following: interdisciplinary academic programs, developing impactful public-facing events, and fundraising are desirable. The successful candidate will be hired at the Full Professor rank in the Department of Classics and Letters starting August 2027 or January 2028. This position will have a 1:1 teaching load while holding the Endowed Chair and Director position. For questions or inquiries, please contact Kyle Harper at kyleharper@ou.edu . Advanced degree in relevant field Experience and accomplishment commensurate with full professor rank Candidates should submit a letter of interest, CV, sample publication, and contact information for three confidential letters of recommendation, via Interfolio ( https://apply.interfolio.com/192547 ). Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194380": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Communication- Organizational Communication (Tenure Track)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Communication Location: Norman, OK The Department of Communication in the Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences at The University of Oklahoma invites applications for a full-time Tenure-Track Assistant Professor position in Organizational Communication. We seek an outstanding scholar who will contribute to the department\u2019s national reputation in Organizational Communication and who has methodological expertise in quantitative, qualitative, or mixed research methods. We take a social-scientific approach to Human Communication rather than rhetorical, critical, professional, or performative ones. The successful candidate should be an active scholar with a clearly defined program of research that can make significant theoretical contributions to the organizational communication sub-field and a demonstrated record of or strong potential for publication in refereed journals and/or books. Furthermore, the scholar\u2019s research should help enhance the department\u2019s program and contribute to the broader strategic vision of the Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences and The University of Oklahoma. Interdisciplinary interests that could lead to collaborations across other departments and centers at the University are desirable, particularly if such initiatives lead to external funding that can enhance the university\u2019s national profile. Departments and Colleges in which potential for collaboration may exist include the following: Industrial/Organizational Psychology, Price College of Business, Engineering Leadership, Women\u2019s and Gender Studies, Sociology, Political Science, or a discipline where the scholar applies their work. Furthermore, opportunities for collaboration beyond the Norman campus include the Harold Hamm Health Campus in Oklahoma City and the University of Oklahoma-Tulsa. The successful candidate will teach in the department\u2019s required undergraduate course sequence and in upper-level and graduate courses in their area of interest as well as mentor graduate students. Normal duties consist of a 2-2 teaching load each academic year. The candidate will teach undergraduate and graduate courses in organizational communication, small group communication, and related classes. The candidate will also provide service to the department, university, and discipline. The department currently includes 14 T/TT faculty, 2 RT faculty, and offers B.A., M.A., and Ph.D. degrees to roughly 350 undergraduate students and 45 graduate students on the Norman campus. Additionally, the department supports an online master\u2019s degree in organizational leadership with another 150 master\u2019s students. The Department of Communication is part of the Division of Social Sciences within the Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences, the largest and oldest college at the University. As Oklahoma\u2019s flagship research university, the University of Oklahoma continues to strengthen its research impact and capacity. The University recently set a record for sponsored research, and research expenditures are expected to exceed $500 million this year. Fueled by more than 70% growth in research activity over the past six years, OU now ranks among the nation\u2019s fastest-growing research universities. Moreover, for the sixth consecutive year, this year\u2019s incoming freshman class was the largest in state history, consisting of 6,437 students. Importantly, 37% of the incoming class are first in their families to attend college. Norman, OK (population approx. 131,000) is located in the heart of the state. The city has been consistently rated as one of the top 100 best places to live by various magazines. The city offers a wide variety of cultural, educational, leisure, and recreational opportunities and a lower cost of living than the national average. It is conveniently located less than 20 miles from a large airport and the opportunities of a major metropolitan area, Oklahoma City (over 1.5 million). Candidates must have an earned doctorate in Communication or a related field at the time of appointment (August 2027) and will be appointed at the rank of assistant professor (tenure-track). Salary will be competitive and commensurate with experience. Start-up funds and relocation expenses may be available. Initial screening will begin no later than November 12, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. A complete application will include a letter of interest not to exceed three pages, the candidate\u2019s curriculum vitae, evidence of teaching effectiveness, a representative academic writing sample, and contact information for three confidential letters of recommendation . https://apply.interfolio.com/194380 For additional information, contact Dr. Ryan S. Bisel, Search Committee Chair ( ryanbisel@ou.edu )."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194855": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Communication- Health Communication (Tenure Track)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Communication Location: Norman, OK The Department of Communication in the Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences at The University of\u202fOklahoma\u202finvites applications for a full-time tenure-track assistant professor position in H ealth Communication. We seek an outstanding scholar who will contribute to the department\u2019s national reputation in H ealth C ommunication and who has methodological expertise in quantitative, qualitative, or mixed research methods. We take a social-scientific approach to human communication rather than rhetorical, critical, professional, or performative ones. The successful candidate should be an active scholar with a clearly defined program of research that can make significant theoretical contributions to the health communication sub-field and a demonstrated record of or strong potential for publication in refereed journals. Furthermore, the scholar\u2019s research should help enhance the department\u2019s program and contribute to the broader strategic vision of the Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences and The University of\u202fOklahoma. Interdisciplinary interests that could lead to collaborations across other departments and centers at the University are desirable, particularly if such initiatives lead to external funding that can enhance the university\u2019s national profile. Departments and Colleges in which potential for collaboration may exist include the following: College of Allied Health, OU\u2019s Harold Hamm Health Campus, Gaylord College of Journalism and Mass Communication, the Department of Women \u2019s and Gender Studies, Department of Sociology, or a discipline where the scholar applies their work. The successful candidate will teach in the department\u2019s required undergraduate course sequence and in upper-level and graduate courses in their area of interest as well as mentor graduate students. Normal duties consist of a 2-2 teaching load each academic year. The candidate will teach undergraduate and graduate courses in health communicatio n a nd related classes. The candidate will also provide service to the department, university, and discipline. The department currently includes 1 4 T/TT faculty , 2 RT faculty, and offers B.A., M.A., and Ph.D. degrees to roughly 350 undergraduate students and 45 graduate students on the Norman campus . The Department of Communication is part of the Division of Social Sciences within the Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences, the largest and oldest college at the University. As Oklahoma\u2019s flagship research university, the University of Oklahoma continues to strengthen its research impact and capacity. The U niversity recently set a record for sponsored research, and research expenditures are expected to exceed $500 million this year. Fueled by more than 70% growth in research activity over the past six years, OU now ranks among the nation\u2019s fastest-growing research universities. Moreover, for the sixth consecutive year, this year\u2019s incoming freshman class was the largest in state history, consisting of 6,437 students. Importantly, 37% of the incoming class are first in their families to attend college. Norman, OK (population approx. 1 31,000 ) is located in the heart of the state. The city has been consistently rated as one of the top 100 best places to live by various magazines. The city offers a wide variety of cultural, educational, leisure, and recreational opportunities and a lower cost of living than the national average. It is conveniently located less than 20 miles from a large airport and the opportunities of a major metropolitan area,\u202fOklahoma\u202fCity (over 1. 5 million). Candidates must have an earned doctorate in Communication or a related field at the time of appointment (August 202 7 ) and will be appointed at the rank of assistant professor (tenure-track). Salary will be competitive and commensurate with experience. Start-up funds and relocation expenses may be available. Initial screening will begin no later than January 12 , 202 6 , and will continue until the position is filled. A complete application will include a letter of interest not to exceed three pages, the candidate\u2019s curriculum vitae, evidence of teaching effectiveness, a representative academic writing sample, and contact information for three confidential letters of recommendation. https://apply.interfolio.com/194855 For additional information contact Dr. Elena Bessarabova , Search Committee Chair ( ebess@ou.edu )."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185168": {"title": "Lecturer of Economics", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Economics Location: The University of Oklahoma Department of Economics  The Department of Economics at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for a Lecturer of Economics Teaching Specialist position.\u00a0 The successful candidate will have demonstrated teaching excellence and is capable of effectively leading large Macroeconomics classes (lectures are 400-500 students each) and a variety of other undergraduate courses. The regular teaching load is 4:4 or equivalent. Completion of a Ph.D. in Economics prior to start date, August 15, 2026 is required.\u00a0 Contributions to our service missions are also expected.\u00a0 Salary and benefits are competitive. A background check is required for employment. Norman is a vibrant university town with outstanding schools, amenities, and a low cost of living, http://soonerway.ou.edu .\u00a0 Norman is part of the Oklahoma City metropolitan area, located less than 20 minutes from downtown, offering the charm of a college town with the amenities of a larger city. The University of Oklahoma (OU) is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement. OU enrolls over 32,000 students and has more than 2,700 full-time faculty members in 21 colleges. A Ph.D. in Economics is required by appointment date. ABD candidates may apply but must have a Ph.D. in Economics degree in hand by start date. Initial screening will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applicants must upload a cover letter, curriculum vitae, job market paper, teaching evaluations, teaching portfolio, and contact information for three confidential letters of recommendation. Applicants may also upload an optional 15 minutes long teaching demonstration. Submit your application via Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com/185168 For any search related questions: Search Committee Chair, Dr. M.J. Kim, mjkim@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193544": {"title": "Associate or Full Professor of English- Paul and Carol Daube Sutton Chair in Rhetoric and Writing Studies (Tenured)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: English Location: University of Oklahoma The Department of English in the Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for a tenured scholar of Rhetoric and/or Writing Studies of national distinction and promise at the rank of Associate or Full Professor to hold the Paul and Carol Daube Sutton Chair in English with a target start date of August 16, 202 7 . We seek a highly productive scholar of rhetoric, writing, or literacy studies with a national/international reputation. We are especially interested in a scholar whose work will attract undergraduate and graduate students and foster a culture of collaboration between faculty and students. The field of specialization is open , though we especially welcome applicants who conduct theoretical and/or historical researc h ; study cultural, environmental, or civic rhetorics ; or work in any area of writing and literacy studies. The successful candidate will contribute to our vibrant and growing Rhetoric and Writing Studies Program, teaching both undergraduate and graduate courses in rhetoric and writing and mentoring emerging scholars in a welcoming academic environment. The position is a full-time, nine-month appointment with a 1 :1 teaching load while holding the Endowed Chair . Salary is commensurate with expertise and experience. The endowment provides annual research and travel funding. Requirements: A PhD in Rhetoric and Writing Studies , Communication Studies, or equivalent field . T enure at current institution and significant academic experience. T wo scholarly books published by nationally recognized press es or a negotiable equivalent of two books (e.g., approximately 20 or more peer-reviewed articles in reputable journals or sustained evidence of peer-review ed , high-impact community-engaged or digital scholarship) . The ideal candidate will have a record of national awards or fellowships and demonstrate successful , ongoing student mentorship . To apply, please submit a curriculum vitae, a writing sample, a letter of interest indicating how the candidate anticipates contributing to our growing undergraduate and graduate program, and contact information for three ( 3 ) confidential letters of recommendation to https://apply.interfolio.com/193544 . Review of applications will begin November 1 , 202 6 , and will continue until the position is filled. Direct inquiries to Professor Roxanne Mountford , Search Committee Chair ( mountford @ou.edu )."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/179390": {"title": "Postdoctoral Fellowship - American History", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: History Location: The University of Oklahoma - Department of History  The Department of History at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for a two-year postdoctoral fellowship in American history, beginning August 2026. We welcome applications from scholars working in any period of American history. The department has areas of specialization in environmental history, Native American history, the history of the American West, and transnational women\u2019s and gender history, but all fields will be considered. The Fellow will teach two courses per semester. Among those courses will be both halves of the US history survey. The Fellow will also have the opportunity to teach upper-level courses that align with their research interests. T he Fellow will also teach other courses as needed by the department, including upper-level courses that may align with their research interests. The Department of History promotes the professional development and career advancement of all its members. The Fellow will be paired with a faculty mentor in the Department of History for the duration of their time at OU. The department, working with the OU Arts and Humanities Forum, will sponsor a manuscript development workshop to assist the Fellow in preparing a book manuscript for publication. The fellow will be eligible to apply for funds for research and conference travel. The University of Oklahoma (OU) is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement. The University of Oklahoma is a Carnegie-R1comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement. OU enrolls over 32,000 students and has more than 2,700 full-time faculty members in 21 colleges. The University is home to a 277-acre Research Campus in Norman, a culturally rich and vibrant town with outstanding schools, amenities, and a low cost of living. Norman is located just south of Oklahoma City, the capital and economic hub of the state of Oklahoma, which offers all the amenities of a major metropolis. See http://soonerway.ou.edu A PhD in History or a related field is required by August 2026, the time of the appointment. Candidates who do not have a PhD in hand at the time of application must include a letter from their committee chair stating that the candidate will have completed all degree requirements before the start date of the postdoc position. Applicants must be no more than four years beyond conferral of the PhD. Please submit a c over letter, CV, a one-page proposal for an upper-level course, including illustrative readings, a letter from your committee chair attesting to anticipated degree completion date (if required), and three letters of reference to https://apply.interfolio.com/179390 . Review of applications will begin on February 15, 2026. We encourage applicants to submit all materials by that date, although applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Please address any questions regarding this position to Prof. Adam Malka at: acmalka@ou.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/110960": {"title": "Clinical Mental Health Counseling, RT - Lecturer, Online", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Human Relations Location: Norman, Oklahoma The University of Oklahoma is an accredited graduate institution with a mission for social change, dedicated to high academic standards and helping students make a difference in their professions, organizations and communities. The Department of Human Relations in the College of Arts and Sciences at The University of Oklahoma (OU) invites applications for a Renewable Term Lecturer for its Clinical Mental Health Counseling Online program, beginning Fall 2024. Our mission for the Clinical Mental Health Counseling online program is to promote excellence in counseling through evidence-based clinical training, democratic values, and advocacy through the provision of clinical services. Of particular interest at the Department of Human Relations are candidates who are committed to making a difference in society through social change. Responsibilities include teaching masters-level counseling courses offered online. Duties will also include advising students in the online program. The new online program aims to reach a wide student population across the nation who are interested in becoming Licensed Professional Counselors through an exclusively online format. The targeted online student population will be seeking a dynamic, rigorous online master program to improve their careers and livelihoods. The CMHC program is currently in the process of seeking accreditation by the Council for Accreditation of Counseling and Related Educational Programs (CACREP), an independent accrediting agency recognized by the Council for Higher Education Accreditation that accredits counselor preparation master's degree programs. Faculty may be expected to assist with some accreditation activities. Course Teaching/Availability: The teaching expectation is three (3) courses in the Fall, Spring, and Summer semesters (3:3:3). All courses will be taught in the online format through CANVAS. Travel Requirements: Some travel could be required (minimally once per quarter). Tools/Technical Skills: To perform this job successfully an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. The requirements listed are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. Incumbents will be evaluated, in part, based on performance of each essential function. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. Technology Requirements: Faculty members are required to have an adequate computer to handle an electronic learning platform, keyboard, pointing device, and computer monitor. All other tools are knowledge-based or training-based through the University of Oklahoma. The Faculty Member must be able to use Microsoft Office products (MS Word, MS Excel, and MS PowerPoint), zip software, and Adobe Acrobat and must have reliable Internet access as well as alternate access in cases where primary access becomes unavailable. The use of Canvas will be required. Training is available through the University. Licensing/Certification: Faculty Members must be appropriately credentialed, possess an earned degree doctoral degree from an accredited institution or recognized by a country's ministry of education in the discipline being taught, and may be expected to be licensed or license-eligible in order to teach in specific programs. Faculty Members must maintain their licenses and any appropriate certifications in order to continue to teach at OU. Faculty Members are also expected to maintain current with research by reviewing articles, journals, and presentations. If participating in research, the Faculty Member may publish and acknowledge OU though there is not an expectation of research with this hire. EDUCATION AND/OR EXPERIENCE: Earned doctorate in Counseling or Counselor Education from a CACREP accredited program at an accredited institution Have related doctoral degrees and have been employed as full-time faculty members in a counselor education program for a minimum of one full academic year before July 1, 2013 Doctoral student who will graduate from a CACREP accredited program no later than May 2024 Current state licensure as a mental health professional Minimum of three years of experience teaching and mentoring master\u2019s level students. Interest in establishing a community of mental health students. Ability to provide leadership and support in program assessment. Ability to assume a significant role in addressing student questions and concerns about the program. Experience supervising students in their field training experience. Commitment to the goals of diversity and social change. Ability to work with non-traditional students. Ability to participate in residencies, national faculty meetings and other work-related travel. Minimum Qualifications: Earned doctorate from a CACREP accredited program or a seasoned doctoral level faculty member who was previously certified as a core CMHC faculty member OR Doctoral student in a CACREP program with a graduation date no later than May 2024 LPC License Strong interpersonal skills Good written skills Dedication to students\u2019 success and high standards of teaching Evidence of effective teaching skills and online communication Demonstrated ability to collaborate with colleagues Teaching online experience at least 3 years \u2013 Preferred Member of a counseling organization. Required Applicant Documents: Curriculum Vita Cover Letter Sample Three Letters of Reference with contact information of references Unofficial Academic Transcripts (official transcripts due upon hire) Sample Syllabus Instruction to Apply: Consideration of applications will begin immediately.\u00a0 The position will remain open until filled. Applications should be submitted online via Interfolio at http://apply.interfolio.com/110960 ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/176618": {"title": "Renewable Term Lecturer in Applied Information Technology", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Library and Information Studies Location: The University of Oklahoma, Norman Campus The School of Library and Information Studies (SLIS) at the University of Oklahoma (OU) invites applications for one full-time, Renewable Term Lecturer positions (9-month academic year contract with 3 months summer required) to teach in the Bachelor of Science in Information Science and Technolog y program. Th is faculty position will begin in August 202 6 (fall semester). Th is position is located in Norman, Oklahoma and faculty members will be expected to move to the area (this is not a remote position). The Bach el or of Science in Information Science and Technology is delivered in an online and asynchronous format. The standard distribution of effort for RT positions is 80% teaching and 20% service with a 4:4 (9-mo.) or 4:4:2 (12-mo.) teaching load, or equivalency. This position is currently 80% teaching with a load of 8 classes per year, typically on a 3 fall, 3 spring, and 2 summer schedule. The remaining 20% is service within the department and university. Successful candidates will have a specialization in Information Technology and the ability to teach two or more of the following specializations: Data analytics (introductory and advanced levels) Website design and development Information visualization Database design a nd implementation User-centered design and evaluat ion Machine learning and natural language processing Applied Artificial intelligence models, evaluation, and application s Cybersecurity Cloud computing Trus t and security Faculty responsibilities include: Instruction at the undergraduate and graduate level s Course development Mentoring undergraduate and graduate students Supervising student independent courses and projects Contributing to systematic planning and development within SLIS, including attending faculty meetings SLIS committee membership, other SLIS service assignments Qualifications Minimum Qualifications: Earned Ph.D. with a focus on information science , data science, or related discipline . Demonstrated online teaching experience Preferred Qualifications Experience teaching undergraduate and graduate students Experience teaching in an online, asynchronous format Application Instructions Applications must be submitted through the online application system https://apply.interfolio.com/176618 and include the following: A letter of interest detailing the individual\u2019s qualifications for the position A current curriculum vitae An unofficial graduate transcript showing the earned graduate degree Names and contact information for three professional references Position will remain open until filled. If you have any questions, please email the chair of the search committee, Dr. June Abbas, at jmabbas@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194006": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Library and Information Studies - Human Information Behavior (Tenure Track or Tenured)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Library and Information Studies Location: Norman, OK The School of Library and Information Studies (SLIS) in the Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences at the University of Oklahoma (OU) invites applications for a full-time tenured/tenure track faculty position in Human Information Behavior . This position will begin on August 16, 2027. The position is located in Norman, Oklahoma and the successful candidate will be required to move to the area (this is not a remote position). Our mission statement and other information can be found at https://www.ou.edu/cas/slis/about . The University of Oklahoma offers a comprehensive benefits package, including health insurance, retirement, and leave benefits. For additional information about benefits, please visit the https://hr.ou.edu/ . We are seeking a dynamic candidate with a demonstrated research agenda, commitment to service, and the ability to teach courses at the undergraduate, master\u2019s, and/or doctoral levels. This is a 9-month position with a teaching load of 2 courses in the fall semester and 2 courses in the spring semester. Faculty distribution of effort is typically 40% teaching, 40% research, and 20% service and faculty members are expected to be successful in all three categories. A successful hire for this faculty position will have the research experience and knowledge to engage deeply in user-centered design practices, system design, and user studies across many disciplines, industries, and grant projects, working collaboratively with faculty within and outside of SLIS. Courses taught by this faculty member could include topics such as Information Seeking and Use, Information Sources and Services, Information Behavior, Information and Society, Online Information Retrieval, Health Information Behavior, Digital Information Ethics, and User-Centered Design. Faculty responsibilities include: Instruction including course development, mentoring graduate students, and supervising student independent courses and projects Research and publication at a level appropriate for a Carnegie R1 research university Departmental service including contributing to systematic planning and development within SLIS, attending faculty meetings, SLIS committee membership, other SLIS service assignments Service at the college and university, and professional service at the regional, national, and international levels Required: Earned Ph.D. or equivalent in LIS or related discipline with a specialization in Human Information Behavior. The candidate can be ABD at the time of application but is expected to have Ph.D. in-hand by August 2027. Demonstrated teaching experience Demonstrated research ability Preferred (not required): Experience teaching at the graduate level Experience teaching in an online, asynchronous format Applicants are invited to submit a comprehensive cover letter addressing how their credentials make them a good fit for this faculty position, including a description of their research agenda, and career goals. The cover letter may address examples of the candidate's approach to teaching. In addition to the cover letter, applicants will submit a current C.V. and names with contact information for three confidential letters of reference. Materials should be submitted to https://apply.interfolio.com/194006 . The position will remain open until filled. Contact Person and Application Review Date: The search committee will begin reviewing\u00a0applications on November 15, 2026. Inquiries should be directed to the search committee co-chair: Dr. Susan K. Burke, Associate Professor The University of Oklahoma. School of Library and Information Studies sburke@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/186883": {"title": "Instructor/Lecturer in Modern Language, Literature and Linguistics - Spanish", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Modern Languages, Literatures and Linguistics Location: Norman, Oklahoma The Department of Modern Languages, Literatures, and Linguistics at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for one full-time, Renewable Term position in Spanish for the 2026-27 academic year, with a possibility of reappointment contingent upon excellent performance and adequate enrollments. Start date is August 16, 2026. The teaching load is 4:4 or equivalent. Current equivalency is 22-26 credit hours per academic year, or 3-4 courses per semester, primarily from Beginning to Intermediate Spanish. Salary is based upon rank. The position includes benefits. Instructors will attend all meetings and workshops related to lower-division Spanish and complete tasks (such as participation in preparing some course materials and holding office hours) for the section as required. Candidates must hold at least an M.A. (Instructor) or Ph.D./terminal degree (Lecturer) in Spanish language, literature, linguistics, applied linguistics, or a related field at the time of appointment. Applicants must have native or near-native proficiency in Spanish. Preference will be given to candidates with asynchronous teaching experience in an American university setting. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Submit applications via Interfolio https://apply.interfolio.com/186883 . Required Materials: 1.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 A cover letter detailing how the candidate meets the minimum requirements above; 2.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Curriculum vitae; 3.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Evidence of successful teaching experience, including complete sets of teaching evaluations. 4.\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0Contact information for three confidential letters of recommendation. For questions please contact Daniel Anderson at daniel.anderson@ou.edu \u201cSpanish Instructor/Lecturer\u201d in the subject line."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185961": {"title": "Instructor of Native American Studies - Cherokee Language (RT)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Native American Studies Location: Norman, Oklahoma Cherokee Language Instructor (Renewable Term, Unranked) The Department of Native American Studies invites applications for a Renewable Term Cherokee Language Instructor to teach a three\u2011course Cherokee language sequence (introductory through intermediate/advanced levels). This unranked position emphasizes high\u2011quality undergraduate instruction, language revitalization, and service contributions that support Cherokee language learning and Indigenous language initiatives at an R1 institution. The successful candidate will have an in-load of 4:4 or equivalent - currently the equivalency is 13 credit hours per semester , typically across the three\u2011course sequence, using communicative, culturally grounded, and learner\u2011centered pedagogical approaches. Courses may include in\u2011person instruction and may incorporate instructional technologies consistent with university and departmental practices. Expected start date of Fall 26 or January 27. Primary responsibilities include course preparation and instruction, assessment of student learning, timely grading, maintenance of syllabi aligned with program outcomes, and regular student advising related to language courses. The instructor is also expected to contribute to departmental and program service, which may include curriculum development, coordination with language programs, participation in language\u2011related events, and collaboration with tribal and community partners as appropriate. This position is a nine\u2011month appointment with a five\u2011year renewable term , subject to annual review in accordance with college and university policy. The role is primarily teaching\u2011focused, with service responsibilities aligned to the mission of Indigenous language education and stewardship. Demonstrated high proficiency in Cherokee (spoken and written), sufficient to teach a full three\u2011course college\u2011level language sequence. Experience teaching Cherokee or another Indigenous language at the postsecondary level or comparable community\u2011based, immersion, or revitalization program experience appropriate to college instruction. Demonstrated knowledge of communicative and culturally grounded language\u2011teaching methodologies , including learner\u2011centered and proficiency\u2011based instruction. Ability to develop and deliver college\u2011level curricula , including syllabi, assessments, and course materials aligned with departmental and program learning outcomes. Evidence of effectiveness in teaching , such as teaching evaluations, lesson plans, teaching philosophy, or supervisor/community references. Commitment to Indigenous language revitalization , cultural stewardship, and ethical engagement with language communities. Ability to contribute to departmental and program service , including curriculum coordination and participation in language\u2011related initiatives. Eligibility to work in the United States by the start date of the appointment. Education Masters degree in related field and/or equivalent language expertise, teaching experience, and community\u2011recognized knowledge are demonstrated. community\u2011recognized speaker of Cherokee , or long\u2011term advanced second\u2011language speaker with demonstrated instructional proficiency. Prior experience teaching at an R1 or AAU institution , or familiarity with the expectations of research\u2011intensive universities. Experience teaching a coordinated language sequence (multi\u2011semester progression) and collaborating on shared curricula. Experience developing instructional materials for Cherokee language teaching, including original lesson plans, textbooks, open educational resources, or digital tools. Familiarity with language instruction technologies , such as learning management systems (e.g., Canvas), multimedia materials, hybrid/online tools, or language\u2011learning platforms. Experience working with tribal governments, educational programs, or community partners in language education or revitalization initiatives. Ability to contribute to interdisciplinary or community\u2011engaged language programming , such as events, workshops, or language\u2011culture initiatives. Experience mentoring students in language learning or supporting co\u2011curricular language activities. Applications must be submitted via Interfolio - https://apply.interfolio.com/185961 , and include: Cover letter detailing qualifications for the position. Current CV Syllabus Contact information for three confidential letters of recommendation, or three confidential letters of recommendation. The position will remain open until filled. For any questions regarding this position, please contact Dr. Laura Harjo, Department Chair - harjo@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190539": {"title": "Research Associate of Native American Studies", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Native American Studies Location: Norman, Oklahoma The Research Associate provides professional-level research support for interdisciplinary community-engaged research initiatives affiliated with the Indigenous Futures Research Lab (IFRL) at the University of Oklahoma. The Lab serves as a hub for future-building projects grounded in kinship-based approaches to community engagement. The Research Associate supports faculty-led and community driven research integrating the following: immersive technologies, Indigenous media, performance, archaeology, and digital humanities. The position combines research coordination, relational engagement with community partners, and research operations support in alignment with Indigenous research methodologies and university requirements. Job Duties: Provides professional research coordination support for faculty-led interdisciplinary, and grant funded research projects affiliated with the Indigenous Futures Research Lab (IFRL). Assists in planning and implementing research activities, timelines, and deliverables across multiple concurrent projects. Supports research initiatives involving immersive technologies, Indigenous films, sound, podcasting, performance, archaeology (including drones, GPR, and LiDAR), and digital humanities. Applies Indigenous research methodologies and principles of kinship-based engagement and relational accountability in support of research activities. Coordinates participation of faculty, research staff, students, consultants and community collaborators. Collects, organizes, and maintains research-related documentations, records, and output. Assists with preparation of research materials and documentation for internal review, external sponsors, and grant reporting. Research Operations & Financial Support: Supports research-related purchasing, reimbursements, and payments using Peoplesoft and Concur, in coordination with Shared Business Services Center (SBSC). Assists with tracking research expenditures to support alignment with approved grant budgets and allowable costs. Maintains organized documentation to support audit, compliance, and institutional accountability requirements. Research Personnel & Onboarding: Assist with onboarding and orientation of research faculty, research students, artists, elders, and project affiliates. Supports coordination of research student appointments and participation in lab projects. Maintains onboarding materials and participation documentation. Communications & Research Dissemination: Supports dissemination of research activities and outcomes through events, public programming, and digital platforms. Assists with marketing, social media, and public-facing communications that support lab research visibility. Coordinates ordering, logistics, and operational support for research-related events and activities. Required Education and Experience: Master's degree in related field or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience supporting research, creative projects, or community-engaged initiatives. Preferred Education and Experience: PhD in Indigenous/Native American Studies, humanities, social services, media studies, archaeology, or related field. Experience working with Indigenous communities or cultural institutions. Experience supporting grant-funded research environments. Familiarity with creative, digital humanities, or interdisciplinary research projects. To apply for the Research Associate position, submit a Cover letter, Curriculum Vitae (CV) and Transcripts via Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com/190539"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193462": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Oklahoma Biological Survey and Biological Sciences- Vector Biology and One Health (Tenure-Track)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Oklahoma Biological Survey Location: University of Oklahoma, Norman Campus The Oklahoma Biological Survey (OBS) and the School of Biological Sciences (SBS) at the University of Oklahoma (OU) invite applications for a full-time tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Vector Biology and One Health science with a starting date of August 16, 2027, teaching 1 course per academic year. We seek an innovative, collaborative, and forward-looking colleague with a record of research in vector biology, whose research addresses the biological foundations of One Health and the effects of environmental change on arthropods of medical/veterinary importance and/or vector-borne diseases; who will establish a nationally recognized, student-engaged, interdisciplinary, and externally funded research program; and who will build collaborations across the University and with public, agricultural, wildlife, and health partners, and contribute to the University\u2019s efforts to understand and respond to emerging health challenges associated with environmental and land-use as part of OU\u2019s Lead On Strategic Plan . Areas of interest for the ideal candidate include, but are not limited to the following: vector biodiversity and population and range dynamics, vector-host-pathogen interactions, medical and veterinary entomology, and disease ecology. We are especially interested in candidates who integrate field-based investigation with molecular, genomic, geospatial, quantitative, or predictive approaches to address questions related to vectors and vector-borne disease in the context of One Health. The successful candidate will be expected to: (1) develop and sustain an extramurally-funded research program; (2) contribute to statewide biodiversity monitoring and environmental health surveillance through the Oklahoma Biological Survey; (3) foster interdisciplinary collaborations across OU and with governmental, tribal, agricultural, wildlife, and public-health partners; (4) mentor undergraduate and graduate students in research and experiential learning; and (5) contribute to undergraduate and graduate teaching in areas such as vector biology, vector\u2013host\u2013pathogen interactions, parasitology, medical and veterinary entomology and/or microbiology, microbial genetics, and One Health science. The OBS is both a state agency and a research unit in the Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences affiliated with the School of Biological Sciences at OU, and is home to the Robert Bebb Herbarium , the Oklahoma Natural Heritage Inventory , and the Oklahoma Natural Areas Registry program. The SBS was recently established through the integration of the departments of Biology and Microbiology and Plant Biology to align graduate programs and research with faculty strengths and OU\u2019s Lead On Strategic Plan . OU invests in its faculty by providing support and resources through the Center for Faculty Excellence and Vice President for Research and Partnerships Office . The Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences supports faculty development through senior faculty mentoring, new faculty orientation series, and access to research and educational support. We encourage candidates to apply who seek to contribute to collegial and interdisciplinary research environments. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in biological sciences, vector biology, or other related fields. Established record of high-quality research and publications. Demonstrated record of, or clear potential for, strong extramural funding. Demonstrated or explicit commitment to outreach, community engagement, and service to the OBS, SBS, university, and discipline. Familiarity with effective teaching practices and student mentoring approaches Preferred Qualifications: Experience leading collaborative projects and working with interdisciplinary teams. Demonstrated teaching and mentoring record, evidenced by list of courses taught, description of innovative pedagogy, effective student mentoring and engagement, and/or a history of positive student and peer evaluations. Demonstrated potential for coordination and collaboration development with state offices, tribal organizations, private landowners, and the communities of OBS, SBS, and OU. Applicants are required to submit: 1) a cover letter (limited to 2 pages) describing your motivations and qualifications for applying and fit with the OBS and one or more of the SBS research themes; 2) a full curriculum vitae; and 3) a statement of interest (limited to 4 pages) that includes a (i) brief summary of your current and future scholarship, (ii) brief summary of your experience with classroom teaching and mentoring, and advising undergraduate and graduate students, and (iii) brief discussion of your taxonomic expertise as it relates to your research program. Applications must be submitted online via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/193462 . Additionally, applicants must submit contact information within Interfolio to initiate requests for three (3) confidential letters of recommendation; all letters must be on file for an application to be reviewed for the position. Please limit the cover letter and statement of interest to a maximum of 2 and 4 pages, respectively. Additional materials may be requested. Screening of candidates will begin October 26, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Inquiries should be directed to the search committee chair: Chair: Dr. Cameron Siler, Director Oklahoma Biological Survey University of Oklahoma camsiler@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/175334": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Philosophy \u2013 Socially Engaged Philosophy (Tenure Track)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Philosophy Location: University of Oklahoma, Norman Campus The Shyam Dev Patwardhan Department of Philosophy in the Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences at the University of Oklahoma is seeking an Assistant Professor (tenure track) specializing in an area of Socially Engaged Philosophy. We conceive of socially engaged Philosophy as concerned with matters of societal challenge and opportunity. Examples include practical, applied, and professional ethics (business, health, military, etc.); Philosophy of Technology; and approaches to Ethics, Political Philosophy, Epistemology, Philosophy of Science, and related fields that address issues in this domain. Areas of competence are open. The typical teaching load is 2 courses per semester and involves teaching B.A., M.A., and PhD students. The anticipated start date is August 16, 2026. The successful candidate will become a central member of a vibrant intellectual community that prizes the ability to converse across philosophical traditions and sub-disciplines and values interdisciplinary engagement. We are especially interested in applications from qualified candidates who are committed to practicing philosophy in a cooperative mode and to achieving rigor and excellence in their research and teaching. For more information about our department, please see https://www.ou.edu/cas/philosophy . The University of Oklahoma (OU) is a growing Carnegie R1 public research university located in the city of Norman, Oklahoma. The university and the surrounding community create an environment where our faculty can integrate work with family, recreation, and a high quality of life. OU enrolls over 30,000 students and has more than 2,700 full-time faculty members in 21 colleges. Required qualifications: The candidate must have a PhD in philosophy or a related discipline at the time of application or be ABD with evidence that the PhD will have been defended prior to the fall 2026 start date. (If you do not already have your PhD in hand, please specifically discuss how you meet this qualification in your letter of interest. Describe how much of your dissertation is already complete, and explain your plan for completion by the August 16, 2026, start date.) A record of research in Socially Engaged Philosophy. Preferred qualifications: Demonstrated excellence of research in Socially Engaged Philosophy. Ability to contribute to the department\u2019s curriculum beyond the candidate\u2019s core research area. Having served as the instructor of record in courses at the university level. Demonstrated commitment to pedagogical approaches that foster the success of students with varied levels of preparation. Submit applications (letter of interest, CV, writing sample, and evidence of teaching excellence) at https://apply.interfolio.com/175334 . The letter of interest may elaborate on the candidate\u2019s qualifications for the position in ways that go beyond the information contained in the other documents, such as providing examples of the candidate\u2019s approach to teaching, classroom management, strategies for broadening access, graduate student mentorship, research, service, or the like. Additional materials may be requested at a later date. **Please upload an anonymized version of your writing sample(s), with your name and affiliation removed.** If the paper has been published, it is fine to submit an anonymized penultimate draft. Three letters of recommendation will be required for all candidates. During the application process, you will be prompted for the contact information for references. Each letter writer will receive an e-mail with instructions about how to upload the letter. Contact Person and Application Review Date: To be guaranteed full consideration, please submit all application materials by Dec.5, 2025. The search committee will begin reviewing applications on Dec.6 and the search will remain open until filled. Inquiries should be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. Zev Trachtenberg, at ztrachtenberg@ou.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192938": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Philosophy - Philosophy of Technology or Philosophy of Health (Tenure Track)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Philosophy Location: University of Oklahoma, Norman Campus The Shyam Dev Patwardhan Department of Philosophy in the Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences at the University of Oklahoma is seeking up to two assistant professors (tenure track) specializing in either Philosophy of Technology or Philosophy of Health. Areas of competence are open. The typical teaching load is 2 courses per semester and involves teaching B.A., M.A., and PhD students. The anticipated start date is August 16, 2027. The successful candidate will become a central member of a vibrant intellectual community that prizes the ability to converse across philosophical traditions and sub-disciplines and values interdisciplinary engagement. We are especially interested in applications from qualified candidates who are committed to practicing philosophy in a cooperative mode and to achieving rigor and excellence in their research and teaching. For more information about our department, please see https://www.ou.edu/cas/philosophy . The University of Oklahoma (OU) is a growing Carnegie R1 public research university located in the city of Norman, Oklahoma. The university and the surrounding community create an environment where our faculty can integrate work with family, recreation, and a high quality of life. OU enrolls over 30,000 students and has more than 2700 full-time faculty members in 21 colleges. Required qualifications: The candidate must have a PhD in Philosophy or a related discipline at the time of application or be ABD with evidence that the PhD will have been defended prior to the fall 2027 start date. (If you do not already have your PhD in hand, please specifically discuss how you meet this qualification in your letter of interest. Describe how much of your dissertation is already complete, and explain your plan for completion by the August 16, 2027 start date.) A record of research in Philosophy of Technology or Philosophy of Health. Preferred qualifications: Demonstrated excellence of research in Philosophy of Technology or Philosophy of Health. Ability to contribute to the department\u2019s curriculum beyond the candidate\u2019s core research area. Having served as the instructor of record in courses at the university level. Demonstrated commitment to pedagogical approaches that foster the success of students with varied levels of preparation. Submit applications (letter of interest, CV, writing sample, evidence of teaching excellence and three confidential letters of recommendation) at https://apply.interfolio.com/192938 . The letter of interest may elaborate on the candidate\u2019s qualifications for the position in ways that go beyond the information contained in the other documents, such as providing examples of the candidate\u2019s approach to teaching, classroom management, strategies for broadening access, graduate student mentorship, research, service, or the like. Additional materials may be requested at a later date. **Please upload an anonymized version of your writing sample(s), with your name and affiliation removed.** If the paper has been published, it is fine to submit an anonymized penultimate draft. Three confidential letters of recommendation will be required for all candidates. During the application process you will be prompted for the contact information for references. Each letter writer will receive an e-mail with instructions about how to upload the letter. Contact Person and Application Review Date: To be guaranteed full consideration, please submit all application materials by Nov. 9, 2026. The search committee will begin reviewing applications on Nov. 10 and the search will remain open until filled. Inquiries should be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. Adam Green, at greenab3@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/159986": {"title": "Research Scientist of Physics and Astronomy-Instrumentation", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Physics and Astronomy Location: University of Oklahoma, Homer L. Dodge Department of Physics and Astronomy The Homer L Dodge Physics and Astronomy Department at the University of Oklahoma (OU) in Norman, Oklahoma, is seeking a candidate to fill an Instrumentation Research Scientist position in the Condensed Matter Physics group which is\u00a0 part of the multi-disciplinary Center for Quantum Research and Technology (CQRT). The successful candidate will join a dynamic group of experimental faculty,\u00a0 postdoctoral scientists, and graduate students with expertise ranging from ultrafast spectroscopy to pulsed laser deposition and molecular beam epitaxy, offering instrumentation and experimental design support. DUTIES \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Provide maintenance to cryogenics, vacuum and laser systems \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0Assist research groups with new equipment installation \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Contribute to the development of dedicated electronics and circuitry for power systems, equipment control and monitoring, cryogenics and vacuum systems \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Coordinate with the machine shop and electronics shop on new installations that require upgrades of existing infrastructure. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Troubleshooting-house as well as externally developed equipment \u00b7 Salary range: 50,000-75,000 depending on qualifications and experience Required Education \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Bachelor's degree and at least four years of experience Equivalency Substitution \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 a combination of post high school education and experience equal to six years. Department Preferences \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0MS or PhD in Physics, Materials Science or Engineering \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Evidence of hands-on experience with equipment maintenance Skills \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Background in electrical engineering, physics, vacuum technology, or similar disciplines. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 significant hands-on technical experience, preferably related to machine shop, clean room or high vacuum technology repair \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 experience with LabvieW and SolidWorks or similar CAD software. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Experience in project planning and implementation of a proactive maintenance program is highly preferred. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Must be willing and able to work in a culturally diverse atmosphere and to communicate effectively, both orally and in writing. \u00b7 Salary range: 50,000-75,000 depending on qualifications and experience Interested candidates should apply to: http://apply.interfolio.com/159986 Please submit a cover letter and your resume."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/176646": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate of Physics and Astronomy- Astrophysics", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Physics and Astronomy Location: University of Oklahoma, Homer L. Dodge Department of Physics and Astronomy The Astrophysics group at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for up to two postdoctoral research positions to work on studies of Sun-like and low-mass stars, within the research group led by Professor Sean Matt.\u00a0\u00a0Our research team is dynamic and collaborative and currently includes one postdoc, five PhD students, and several undergraduate research students.\u00a0\u00a0The overarching goal of our research is to develop a theoretical understanding of rotation, magnetic activity, and mass loss of solar-like and low-mass stars, including interactions with their environments (e.g., planets, accretion disks), from the pre-main-sequence phase to the end of the main sequence.\u00a0\u00a0We encourage applications from all who have interests, skills, or experience in any of these areas and who will collaborate with the team. There is ample funding for computing resources and travel, and there will also be the opportunity to gain experience in mentoring, outreach, and other departmental activities. Start date: flexible, starting as soon as 2026 April. Term Length: initial 1-year, renewable up to 2 more years (3 years total) There are 2 positions for postdoctoral research associates open. Applicants should have completed requirements for a relevant PhD before the start date. Each full-time position is for up to 3 years (1-year initial appointment + 2-year renewal with satisfactory performance). The start date for the appointment is flexible (starting as early as 2026 April), so please indicate a potential start date in your application. Applications should include: (1) a cover letter (optional, max 2 pages), (2) a curriculum vitae, (3) a brief research plan (max 3 pages) describing past work and future interests, and (4) having 3 reference letters sent by the application deadline.\u00a0\u00a0All materials must be uploaded via the application system at https://apply.interfolio.com/176646 Applications received by 2026 January 10 will receive full consideration. The positions will remain open until filled. You will submit your application via Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com/176646 Inquiries should be directed to the search committee chair: Professor Sean Matt Subject: Inquiry about postdoc in your group Email: sean@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/179570": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate-Center for Quantum Research and Technology-Condensed Matter Physics", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Physics and Astronomy Location: University of Oklahoma, Homer L. Dodge Department of Physics and Astronomy The Tischler group from the Homer L. Dodge Department of Physics and Astronomy and the Center for Quantum Research and Technology at the University of Oklahoma (OU) invites applications for a postdoctoral researcher position in experimental condensed matter physics (CMP). Topics of interest include optical properties of semiconductors and metamaterials. Topics of interest include metasurfaces of surface phonon polaritons and surface phonon plasmons in heavily doped semiconductors as well as excitons in colloidal nanostructures and other semiconductor heterostructures. The research will focus on physics of these material systems with and without external magnetic fields and temperatures ranging from 4K to high temperatures. The initial appointment is for two years with the possibility of an extension for a total of three years, contingent on satisfactory performance and availability of funds. The start date of the appointment is flexible and can be as early as February 7, 2026. Compensation for the position is competitive and travel support is available. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Physics or a related area. Background on metamaterial fabrication techniques, finite element modelling and optical measurements will be considered favorably. These include experience with high resolution 3D printing through two-photon polymerization (Nanoscribe), modelling using COMSOL, photoluminescence and Raman spectroscopy using grating spectrometers, Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) measurements, cryogen cryostats and high magnetic fields. To apply, please upload your application materials [cover letter, curriculum vitae (including a list of publications), statement of research interests and plans (up to two pages), and contact information for three letters of reference] to INSERT INTERFOLIO WEBSITE . Please also arrange for three letters of reference to be sent directly to tischler@ou.edu. Applications will be given full consideration till the position is filled. For questions, please contact Prof. Joe Tischler at tischler@ou.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/184320": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Experimental Atomic Physics", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Physics and Astronomy Location: Norman, OK Available immediately. Experimental postdoc position in atomic physics pursuing implementations of quantum information science (QIS) in neutral atom systems within the contexts of quantum sensing with atom interferometers, and/or multi-atom entanglement using interactions between ultra-cold Rydberg atoms in optical tweezers.\u00a0 These research directions build upon our prior, foundational work with entangling interactions using ultra-cold Rydberg atoms controlled in optical tweezers, as well as novel techniques for matter wave interferometry.\u00a0 Our current investigations cover aspects of matter wave interferometry, quantum synchronization, and an exploration of computational complexity versus robustness with entangled atomic spins. Main duties include leading, developing, and managing QIS experiments that advance these research directions. The initial appointment is for two years, with the possibility of renewal for a third year, contingent on satisfactory performance and continued funding. The stipend will be $65,000 per year plus benefits along with an annual $4,000 travel budget for the Fellow. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Physics or a related area. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae (including publications), a statement of research interests and plans limited to two pages to interfolio . Applicants will be asked to provide contact information of three confidential letter writers, who will be subsequently contacted. Applicants are asked to indicate their potential start date in the cover letter. For more information please visit the group website (biedermann-group.com) and contact Grant Biedermann: biedermann@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188587": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate of Physics and Astronomy - Theoretical Condensed Matter Physics", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Physics and Astronomy Location: 440 W. Brooks St., Norman, OK 73019 The Center for Quantum Research and Technology (CQRT) at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for two Postdoctoral Associate positions in Theoretical Condensed Matter Physics. The first postdoctoral research associate position, under the direction of Prof. Bruno Uchoa, will broadly collaborate with Center theorists on topics related to topological matter, electronic interactions, quantum simulation, quantum information, many-body quantum entanglement and out-of-equilibrium physics. This position is made possible by the generous support of the Dodge Family Postdoctoral Research Fellows Fund. The second postdoctoral position is in Prof. Tsung-Cheng Lu's research group, which would also involve research in the above areas. CQRT is housed in Lin Hall, a state-of-the-art experimental research facility.\u00a0 Its researchers span the departments of Physics and Astronomy, Electrical Engineering and Chemistry. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Physics or related field. The initial appointment for each position is for two years, with the possibility of renewal for a third year, contingent on satisfactory performance and continued funding. The start date for these appointments is flexible, with appointments starting as early as August 10, 2026. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae (including publications), a statement of research interests and plans limited to two pages to https://apply.interfolio.com/188587 . Applicants will be asked to provide contact information for three confidential letters of recommendation, who will be subsequently contacted through Interfolio. Any inquiries regarding the two positions should be sent to Bruno Uchoa (uchoa@ou.edu) or Tsung-Cheng Lu (tclu@ou.edu). Screening of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the positions are filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192488": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Physics and Astronomy-Theoretical Atomic, Molecular, and Optical Physics", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Physics and Astronomy Location: Norman, OK  The Homer L. Dodge Department of Physics and Astronomy at the University of Oklahoma (OU) invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in theoretical Atomic, Molecular, and Optical (AMO) physics with a starting date of August 16, 2027. This position in fundamental research is associated with the Center for Quantum Research and Technology (CQRT) at OU. The CQRT ( https://ou.edu/cqrt ) is hosted in a state-of-the-art physics building with 18,000 square feet of laboratory space that meets NIST-A specifications on environmental control. Campus resources include supercomputer access. Successful candidates are expected to teach effectively at both the undergraduate and graduate levels (one class per semester), maintain an outstanding record of original published research, and develop an internationally recognized, externally funded research program that complements and expands existing research efforts at OU. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in physics or a closely related field. Demonstrated record of research accomplishments in theoretical AMO physics or closely related field. Preferred Qualifications: Post Ph.D. experience. Demonstrated evidence to teach effectively and to effectively mentor students. Applicants must apply for this position online at the quick link https://apply.interfolio.com/192488 Applications must include: Cover letter (3 pages max). The letter may address examples of the candidate\u2019s approach to teaching, research, service, mentorship, outreach, etc. Curriculum vitae. List of publications Description of past research and future research plans (3-5 pages), demonstrating potential to attract external funding Description of past teaching and/or future plans (2 pages max). Confidential letters of recommendation (3) arranged and uploaded through Interfolio The search committee will begin reviewing applications on Wednesday, November 18, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Inquiries should be directed to the search committee chair: Professor Doerte Blume - doerte.blume-1@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193146": {"title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Physics and Astronomy- Experimental High Energy Physics", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Physics and Astronomy Location: Norman, OK  The Homer L. Dodge Department of Physics and Astronomy in the Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences at the University of Oklahoma (OU) invites applications for a full-time tenure/tenure-track faculty position in experimental High-Energy Physics (HEP). Qualified applicants in all areas of experimental HEP are encouraged to apply. The appointment is expected to begin in August 2027, at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor. Salary will be competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience. The teaching load in the department is one course per semester. Our department has active research groups in astronomy and the following: astrophysics; atomic, molecular, and optical physics; high-energy physics; and condensed matter physics. The department hosts an active NSF REU program, maintains both a machine shop and electronics shop, and enjoys access to the Oklahoma Supercomputing Center for Education and Research (OSCER). The University of Oklahoma HEP group contributes to the ATLAS experiment, has extensive experience in silicon strip and pixel detector technology, and is participating in the high-luminosity upgrade of the ATLAS detector and muon collider R&D. In addition, we host one of the ATLAS tier 2 computing centers. The group has experience in SM and BSM physics, specifically Higgs, top, supersymmetry, and unconventional signatures. Separate from this search, a recent generous gift to the department has endowed the experimental HEP group with the funds to support two full-time research faculty in perpetuity, as well as sustained funds for a seminar series and graduate student support. For further information about our department and the experimental HEP group, see https://ou.edu/cas/physics-astronomy or contact the search committee Chair, John Stupak ( john.stupak@ou.edu ). OU is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement. OU enrolls over 30,000 students and has more than 2,700 full-time faculty members in 21 colleges. Norman is a culturally rich and vibrant town located just outside Oklahoma City. With outstanding schools, amenities, and a low cost of living, Norman is a perennial contender on \u201cbest place to live\u201d lists. For more information, visit http://www.ou.edu/content/dam/provost/documents/facultyflipbook.pdf and https://ou.edu/news/articles/2023/july/forbes-names-ou-the-nations-number-1-employer-for-women . Required Qualifications: Applicants must have a Ph.D. (or equivalent) in physics, or a related area, and a record of research accomplishments in experimental high energy physics. Preferred Qualifications: Applicants should also possess the potential to teach effectively at both undergraduate and graduate levels and a willingness to contribute to a friendly and supportive work environment. Applicants must apply for this position online at https://apply.interfolio.com/193146 . Applications should include: a curriculum vitae (including a list of publications) a description of past research and future plans (4 pages maximum) a description of past teaching and/or future plans (2 pages maximum) a cover letter (2 pages maximum) confidential letters of recommendation (3) The review of complete applications (including reference letters) will begin after November 14, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. For further inquiries, please contact the search committee chair, John Stupak: john.stupak@ou.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/174534": {"title": "Associate or Full Professor of Psychology - Cognitive Psychology (Neuroscience)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Psychology Location: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus As part of a multi-year strategic hiring and growth initiative in Applied Psychological Science, the Department of Psychology at The University of Oklahoma is seeking applications for a tenure track or tenured faculty position in Cognitive Neuroscience at the Associate or Full Professor level with a focus on attention, memory, or language. This position aligns with the strategic plans in Psychology and in the Office of the Vice President for Research and Partnerships ( https://www.ou.edu/research-norman ). The anticipated start date for this position will be August 16, 2026. The Psychology department offers the PhD in Cognitive, Industrial/Organizational, Applied Social and Developmental, and Quantitative Psychology (Norman campus), and an MA in Organizational Dynamics (Tulsa campus). Additionally, some faculty members also work in a multidisciplinary degree program in cellular and behavioral neurobiology. For the tenure track or tenured Associate or Full Professor position in Cognitive Neuroscience, the successful candidate will hold a Ph.D. in Psychology or a related field, have an established record of productivity, including a record of external funding, with work focused on aspects of attention, memory, or language. This search is motivated in part by the recent revision of the University's strategic plan, which includes an emphasis on growing neuroscience research. With respect to enhancing the research portfolio of the department, the successful candidate will ideally have a record of both basic and applied research, including expertise in developing and applying mathematical or computational models. Although all methodologies in human cognitive neuroscience are welcome, we are particularly interested in scholars capable of participating in the recently announced research agreement with the Siemens Corporation, which will bring a high-field (7 Tesla) research-dedicated magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) instrument to the University of Oklahoma Health Campus in Oklahoma City. The successful candidate will also have an established record of effective mentoring at both the undergraduate and graduate levels and should be able to contribute to the teaching of core courses in cognitive neuroscience, as well as to the development of novel course offerings at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. The teaching load will be 2:2. About The University of Oklahoma: Established in 1890, The University of Oklahoma is the flagship public research university in the state of Oklahoma (Carnegie Classification R1: highest research activity), with over 30,000 students and more than 2,700 full-time faculty members offering nearly 500 different graduate and undergraduate degree programs on three campuses (Norman, Oklahoma City, and Tulsa). OU has one of the oldest comprehensive colleges of fine arts in the Great Plains states, with highly regarded schools of Music, Musical Theatre, Drama, Art and Dance, and programs in opera and sculpture. The OU College of Law is Oklahoma\u2019s premier law school, and the Michael F. Price College is ranked as one of the nation\u2019s top business schools. The OU Health Campus is one of only four comprehensive academic health centers in the nation with seven professional schools, including more than 660 doctors practicing in nearly every adult and child specialty. OU\u2019s Department of Psychology is one of the university\u2019s largest departments by enrollment, with 30+ faculty and affiliates serving more than 1,600 undergraduate majors and minors, and 50+ fully funded PhD students. OU Research & Innovation: Adjacent to the beautiful and historic Main campus in Norman, with 277 acres and over 1 million square feet of high-tech facilities located along the Oklahoma Technology Corridor, the research campus hosts the National Weather Center and affiliated organizations. Some of these include the Data Institute for Societal Challenges, the Institute for Community and Society Transformation, the Oklahoma Aerospace and Defense Innovation Institute, the Institute for Resilient Environment and Energy Systems, the Institute for Public Policy Research and Analysis, and many others. By synergistically co-locating State and Federal agency research and operational organizations, along with more than a dozen private companies and several University centers and academic programs, the Norman research campus creates a uniquely rich environment for collaboration, innovation, and commercialization. Over the last seven years, OU\u2019s Office of Technology Commercialization has evaluated over 500 innovations, filed for 350 patents, and returned $23 million in royalty/license income and reimbursement back to OU. Local Community & Culture: The city of Norman, Oklahoma, is a perennial contender on \u201cbest place to live\u201d rankings with outstanding schools, amenities, a low cost of living, small town charm, a variety of recreational opportunities, four distinct seasons, and more than 225 days of sunshine each year. \u00a0Norman is located 20 miles south of Oklahoma City, the Capital and economic hub of the state of Oklahoma. The Oklahoma City Metroplex, including Norman and several other suburban cities, is home to about 1.5 million residents, offering all the amenities of a major modern metropolitan community, including rich cultural heritage, an abundance of the arts, superb dining and nightlife, major sporting and concert events, family entertainment, recreational adventure activities, and much more. The University of Oklahoma is committed to investing in the success of its faculty at all stages of their careers. The department and college offer unique and essential research and professional development resources characteristic of a Flagship Public Research University (e.g., well-equipped, dedicated and shared laboratory space; shared supercomputer access; considerable graduate student funding and recruiting support; large research participant pools; flexible and innovative instructional, service, and leadership opportunities). \u00a0By mutual agreement, the successful candidate may also affiliate with strategic research organizations and centers (e.g., the Center for Applied Social Research, Institute for Community and Society Transformation, multiple centers on the OU Health Campus). Salary, benefits, and start-up packages are highly competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience. Required: Ph.D. in Psychology or a related field Established record of securing external funding, with work focused on the cognitive neuroscience of attention, memory, or language. Ability to develop novel cognitive neuroscience course offerings at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. An established record of effective mentoring at both the undergraduate and graduate levels Preferred: A record of both basic and applied research, including expertise in developing and applying mathematical or computational models. Capable of participating in research utilizing a high-field (7 Tesla) magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) instrument. Ability to contribute to the teaching of core courses in cognitive neuroscience. We encourage applicants to apply by November 1, 2025, for full consideration, although applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Application review will begin immediately, and the position will remain open until filled. All application materials must be submitted electronically via Interfolio. A cover letter addressing position fit, qualifications, student mentorship, and background. Candidates may elaborate on their approach to teaching. A curriculum vita Contact information for three confidential letters of recommendation Up to 3 reprints/preprints Evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., teaching evaluation materials, peer evaluations, example syllabi, student comments/course evaluations) A brief statement of research interests (e.g., 300-2000 words) To apply to the Cognitive Psychology position, please submit the above materials at https://apply.interfolio.com/174534 Please send inquiries to Dr. Michael Wenger ( michael.j.wenger@ou.edu )."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/174543": {"title": "Associate or Full Professor of Psychology - Quantitative Psychology", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Psychology Location: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus As part of a multi-year strategic hiring and growth initiative in Applied Psychological Science, the Department of Psychology at The University of Oklahoma is seeking applications for a tenure track or tenured faculty position in Quantitative Psychology with a focus on Health Outcomes at the Associate or Full Professor level. This position aligns with the strategic plans in Psychology and in the Office of the Vice President for Research and Partnerships ( https://www.ou.edu/research-norman ).\u00a0The anticipated start date for this position will be August 16, 2026. The Psychology department offers the PhD in Cognitive, Industrial/Organizational, Applied Social and Developmental, and Quantitative Psychology (Norman campus), and an MA in Organizational Dynamics (Tulsa campus). Additionally, some faculty members also work in a multidisciplinary degree program in cellular and behavioral neurobiology. For the tenure track or tenured Associate or Full Professor position in Quantitative Psychology, the department seeks a leader in developing and applying advanced quantitative methods to pressing questions in health and/or related outcomes. Current quantitative faculty are known for their interdisciplinary scholarship in areas including (intensive) longitudinal modeling, dynamic modeling, psychometrics, and structural equation modeling. We are eager to welcome a colleague whose expertise will complement and extend this core, fostering new collaborations within the department and across the university. A record of securing external research funding is essential, and a commitment to multidisciplinary collaboration is highly valued. \u00a0The successful candidate will be an established investigator with a commitment to excellence in mentoring graduate students and teaching in our quantitative psychology curriculum. Must hold a Ph.D. in Quantitative Psychology, Psychology, Statistics, Data Science, or a closely related field. Teaching load of 2:2. About The University of Oklahoma: Established in 1890, The University of Oklahoma is the flagship public research university in the state of Oklahoma (Carnegie Classification R1: highest research activity), with over 30,000 students and more than 2,700 full-time faculty members offering nearly 500 different graduate and undergraduate degree programs on three campuses (Norman, Oklahoma City, and Tulsa). OU has one of the oldest comprehensive colleges of fine arts in the Great Plains states, with highly regarded schools of Music, Musical Theatre, Drama, Art and Dance, and programs in opera and sculpture. The OU College of Law is Oklahoma\u2019s premier law school, and the Michael F. Price College is ranked as one of the nation\u2019s top business schools. The OU Health Sciences Center is one of only four comprehensive academic health centers in the nation with seven professional schools, including more than 660 doctors practicing in nearly every adult and child specialty. OU\u2019s Department of Psychology is one of the university\u2019s largest departments by enrollment, with 30+ faculty and affiliates serving more than 1,600 undergraduate majors and minors, and 50+ fully funded PhD students. OU Research & Innovation: Adjacent to the beautiful and historic Main campus in Norman, with 277 acres and over 1 million square feet of high-tech facilities located along the Oklahoma Technology Corridor, the research campus hosts the National Weather Center and affiliated organizations. Some of these include the Data Institute for Societal Challenges, the Institute for Community and Society Transformation, the Oklahoma Aerospace and Defense Innovation Institute, the Institute for Resilient Environment and Energy Systems, the Institute for Public Policy Research and Analysis, and many others. By synergistically co-locating State and Federal agency research and operational organizations, along with more than a dozen private companies and several University centers and academic programs, the Norman research campus creates a uniquely rich environment for collaboration, innovation, and commercialization. Over the last seven years, OU\u2019s Office of Technology Commercialization has evaluated over 500 innovations, filed for 350 patents, and returned $23 million in royalty/license income and reimbursement back to OU. Local Community & Culture: The city of Norman, Oklahoma, is a perennial contender on \u201cbest place to live\u201d rankings with outstanding schools, amenities, a low cost of living, small town charm, a variety of recreational opportunities, four distinct seasons, and more than 225 days of sunshine each year. \u00a0Norman is located 20 miles south of Oklahoma City, the Capital and economic hub of the state of Oklahoma. The Oklahoma City Metroplex, including Norman and several other suburban cities, is home to about 1.5 million residents, offering all the amenities of a major modern metropolitan community, including rich cultural heritage, an abundance of the arts, superb dining and nightlife, major sporting and concert events, family entertainment, recreational adventure activities, and much more. The University of Oklahoma is committed to investing in the success of its faculty at all stages of their careers. The department and college offer unique and essential research and professional development resources characteristic of a Flagship Public Research University (e.g., well-equipped, dedicated and shared laboratory space; shared supercomputer access; considerable graduate student funding and recruiting support; large research participant pools; flexible and innovative instructional, service, and leadership opportunities). \u00a0By mutual agreement, the successful candidate may also affiliate with strategic research organizations and centers (e.g., the Center for Applied Social Research, Institute for Community and Society Transformation, multiple centers on the OU Health Sciences Campus). Salary, benefits, and start-up packages are highly competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience. Required: A Ph.D. in Quantitative Psychology, Psychology, Statistics, Data Science, or a closely related field Strong record of securing external funding, with a\u00a0focus on developing and applying advanced quantitative methods to pressing questions in health and/or related outcomes An established record\u00a0of effective mentoring and teaching at both the undergraduate and graduate levels Preferred: Ability to develop novel course\u00a0offerings at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. A strong\u00a0record of research\u00a0on cutting-edge data analytics, such as\u00a0(intensive) longitudinal modeling, dynamic modeling, psychometrics, and structural equation modeling Capable of participating in research utilizing multidisciplinary collaboration. We encourage applicants to apply by December 1, 2025 for full consideration, although applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Application review will begin immediately and the position will remain open until filled. All application materials must be submitted electronically via Interfolio. A cover letter addressing position fit, qualifications, student mentorship and background. Candidates may elaborate on their approach to teaching. A curriculum vita Contact information for three confidential letters of recommendation Up to 3 reprints/preprints Evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., teaching evaluation materials, peer evaluations, example syllabi, student comments/course evaluations) A brief statement of research interests (e.g., 300-2000 words) To apply to the Quantitative Psychology position, please submit the above materials at https://apply.interfolio.com/174543 Please send inquiries to Dr. Hairong Song ( hsong@ou.edu )."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/174556": {"title": "Lecturer in Psychology", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Psychology Location: University of Oklahoma, Norman Campus The Department of Psychology in the Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for a full-time, benefits-eligible Lecturer position for a five-year renewable term, non-tenure track. The workload is eight courses per year (four per semester). Ideal candidates will be able to teach a variety of core undergraduate courses such as the following: Introductory Psychology, Statistics, Research Methods, Lifespan Development, Cognitive Psychology, and Social Psychology. Strong teaching evaluations are a must, and experience with large sections is desired. The position begins August 16, 2026. Eligibility for annual reappointment is contingent upon satisfactory teaching performance evaluations. Renewal of the five-year term is contingent upon department needs and future funding approval. The University provides a pathway to promotion to Senior Lecturer and Distinguished Lecturer for renewable-term faculty. About The University of Oklahoma Established in 1890, The University of Oklahoma is the flagship public research university in the state of Oklahoma (Carnegie Classification R1: highest research activity), with over 30,000 students and more than 2,700 full-time faculty members offering nearly 500 different graduate and undergraduate degree programs on three campuses (Norman, Oklahoma City, and Tulsa). OU has one of the oldest comprehensive colleges of fine arts in the Great Plains states, with highly regarded schools of Music, Musical Theatre, Drama, Art and Dance, and programs in opera and sculpture. The OU College of Law is Oklahoma\u2019s premiere law school, and The Michael F. Price College is ranked as one of the nation\u2019s top business schools. The OU Health Campus is one of only four comprehensive academic health centers in the nation with seven professional schools, including more than 660 doctors practicing in nearly every adult and child specialty. OU\u2019s Department of Psychology is one of the university\u2019s largest departments by enrollment, with 30+ faculty and affiliates serving more than 1,600 diverse and engaged undergraduate majors and minors, and 50+ fully funded PhD students. Local Community & Culture The city of Norman, Oklahoma, is a perennial contender on \u201cbest place to live\u201d rankings with outstanding schools, amenities, a low cost of living, small town charm, a variety of recreational opportunities, four distinct seasons, and more than 225 days of sunshine each year. Norman is located 20 miles south of Oklahoma City, the Capital and economic hub of the state of Oklahoma. The Oklahoma City Metroplex, including Norman and several other suburban cities, is home to about 1.5 million residents, offering all the amenities of a major modern metropolitan community, including rich cultural heritage, an abundance of the arts, superb dining and nightlife, major sporting and concert events, family entertainment, recreational adventure activities, and much more. To be considered for this position, candidates must have a Ph.D. in Psychology or a closely-related discipline by the time of appointment. Candidates must have evidence of university teaching effectiveness in psychology courses. Preference will be given to candidates who have experience teaching large sections (100+ students). This institution is using Faculty Search in Interfolio to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. To apply, please submit the following materials at https://apply.interfolio.com/174556 Application Instructions We encourage applicants to apply by November 1, 2025 for full consideration, although applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Application review will begin immediately, and the position will remain open until filled. All application materials must be submitted electronically via Interfolio. \u2022 A cover letter specifically addressing position fit, qualifications, and background. The cover letter may address examples of the candidate\u2019s approach to teaching. \u2022 A curriculum vita \u2022 Evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., teaching evaluation materials, peer evaluations, example syllabi, student comments/course evaluations) \u2022 Contact information for three confidential references Please send inquiries, informal candidate nominations, and other suggestions or comments to the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Nicole Campbell ( njudice@ou.edu )."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192219": {"title": "Open Rank Professor of Psychology -- Cognitive Psychology (Neuroscience) (Tenure Track or Tenured)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Psychology Location: University of Oklahoma, Norman Campus As part of a multi-year strategic hiring and growth initiative in Applied Psychological Science, the Department of Psychology in the Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences at The University of Oklahoma is seeking applications for two tenured/tenure-track faculty openings, rank open, one in Quantitative Psychology with a focus on Health Outcomes, and one in Cognitive Neuroscience with a focus on attention, memory, or language. These positions align with the strategic plans in Psychology and in the Office of the Vice President for Research and Partnerships ( https://www.ou.edu/research-norman ). Expected start date of August 2027 with a 2:2 teaching load. The Psychology department offers the PhD in Cognitive, Industrial/Organizational, Applied Social and Developmental, and Quantitative Psychology (Norman campus), and an MA in Organizational Dynamics (Tulsa campus). Additionally, some faculty members also work in one or more integrative, crosscutting specialties: Neuroscience, Personality/Individual Differences and a multidisciplinary degree program in Cellular and Behavioral Neurobiology. For the tenure track/tenured position in Cognitive Neuroscience , the successful candidate will have an established record of productivity, including a record of external funding or the potential to secure funding , with work focused on aspects of attention, memory, or language. This search is motivated in part by the recent revision of the University's strategic plan, which includes an emphasis on growing neuroscience research. With respect to enhancing the research portfolio of the department, the successful candidate will ideally have a record of both basic and applied research, including expertise developing and applying mathematical or computational models. Although all methodologies in human cognitive neuroscience are welcome, we are particularly interested in scholars capable of participating in the recently announced research agreement with the Siemens Corporation, which will bring a high-field (7 Tesla) research-dedicated magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) instrument to the University of Oklahoma Health Campus in Oklahoma City. The successful candidate will also have a commitment to effective mentoring at both the undergraduate and graduate levels and should be able to contribute to the teaching of core courses in cognitive neuroscience, as well as to the development of novel course offerings at both the undergraduate and graduate level. About The University of Oklahoma: Established in 1890, The University of Oklahoma is the flagship public research university in the state of Oklahoma (Carnegie Classification R1: highest research activity), with over 30,000 students and more than 2,700 full-time faculty members offering nearly 500 different graduate and undergraduate degree programs on three campuses (Norman, Oklahoma City, and Tulsa). The University of Oklahoma has one of the oldest comprehensive colleges of fine arts in the Great Plains states, with highly regarded schools of Music, Musical Theatre, Drama, Art and Dance, and programs in opera and sculpture. The OU College of Law is Oklahoma\u2019s premiere law school and The Michael F. Price College is ranked as one of the nation\u2019s top business schools. The OU Health Campus is one of only four comprehensive academic health centers in the nation with seven professional schools, including more than 660 doctors practicing in nearly every adult and child specialty. The University of Oklahoma's Department of Psychology is one of the university\u2019s largest departments by enrollment, with 30 faculty and affiliates serving more than 1,600 undergraduate majors and minors, and 50 fully funded PhD students. OU Research & Innovation: The Norman Research campus was named the No.1 research campus in the nation in 2013 by the Association of Research Parks. Adjacent to the beautiful and historic Main campus in Norman, with 277 acres and over 1 million square feet of high-tech facilities located along the Oklahoma Technology Corridor, the research campus hosts the National Weather Center and affiliated organizations. Some of these include the Data Institute for Societal Challenges, the Institute for Community and Society Transformation, the Oklahoma Aerospace and Defense Innovation Institute, the Institute for Resilient Environment and Energy Systems, and the Institute for Public Policy Research and Analysis, and many others. By synergistically co-locating State and Federal agency research and operational organizations, along with more than a dozen private companies and several University centers and academic programs, the Norman research campus creates a uniquely rich environment for collaboration, innovation, and commercialization. Over the last seven years, OU\u2019s Office of Technology Commercialization has evaluated over 500 innovations, filed for 350 patents, and returned $23 million in royalty/license income and reimbursement back to OU. Local Community & Culture: The city of Norman, Oklahoma, is a perennial contender on \u201cbest place to live\u201d rankings with outstanding schools, amenities, a low cost of living, small town charm, a variety of recreational opportunities, four distinct seasons, and more than 225 days of sunshine each year. \u00a0Norman is located 20 miles south of Oklahoma City, the Capital and economic hub of the state of Oklahoma. The Oklahoma City Metroplex, including Norman and several other suburban cities, is home to about 1.5 million residents, offering all the amenities of a major modern metropolitan community, including rich cultural diversity and heritage, an abundance of the arts, superb dining and nightlife, major sporting and concert events, family entertainment, recreational adventure activities, and much more. The University of Oklahoma is committed to investing in the success of its faculty at all stages of their careers. The department and college offer unique and essential research and professional development resources characteristic of a Flagship Public Research University (e.g., well-equipped dedicated and shared laboratory space; shared supercomputer access; considerable graduate student funding and recruiting support; large and varied research participant pools; flexible and innovative instructional, service, and leadership opportunities). \u00a0By mutual agreement, the successful candidate may also affiliate with strategic research organizations and centers (e.g., the Center for Applied Social Research, Institute for Community and Society Transformation, multiple centers on the OU Health Sciences Campus). Salary, benefits, and start-up packages are highly competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience. Candidates should have evidence of high-quality scholarship in Cognitive Neuroscience through published research and should have a record of, or demonstrate potential to secure, external funding. The successful candidate will also have an established record of effective mentoring at both the undergraduate and graduate levels and should be able to contribute to the teaching of core courses in cognitive neuroscience, as well as to the development of novel course offerings at both the undergraduate and graduate level. Must hold a Ph.D. in Psychology or a related field by the time of appointment (mid-August, 2027). This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. We encourage applicants to apply by October 1, 2026, for full consideration, although applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Application review will begin immediately and the position will remain open until filled. All application materials must be submitted electronically via Interfolio. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 A cover letter addressing position fit, qualifications, student mentorship and background. The cover letter may address examples of the candidate\u2019s approach to teaching. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 A curriculum vita \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Contact information for three confidential letters of recommendation \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Up to 3 reprints/preprints \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., teaching evaluation materials, peer evaluations, example syllabi, student comments/course evaluations) \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 A brief statement of research interests (e.g., 300-2000 words) To apply to the Cogntive Neuroscience position, please submit the above materials at https://apply.interfolio.com/192219 P lease send inquiries to Dr Michael Wenger ( michael.j.wenger@ou.edu )."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185734": {"title": "Instructor or Lecturer of Social Work - Online RT", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Anne and Henry Zarrow School of Social Work Location: Norman, Oklahoma Instructor/Lecturer Position, Anne & Henry Zarrow School of Social Work. Online BASW Program , 5-year Renewable Term Position. Instructor or Lecturer, 4:4:2 or equivalent teaching load. This position requires Oklahoma residency and the ability to attend on\u2011campus meetings. The Anne and Henry Zarrow School of Social Work at the University of Oklahoma seeks an innovative instructor to join our dynamic fac ulty team beginning August 2026 or January 2027 . This position is within our new Online BASW Program and will involve teaching students in a virtual environment. We are especially interested in candidates who can build collaborative relationships across the School, University, and broader community through innovative online teaching, virtual engagement, and service activities that foster connection and partnership in digital learning environments. The S chool is looking to find\u00a0social work educators who will be responsible for teaching online BASW students, possibly providing supervision to students in field practicum, mentoring students and participating in service activities within the S chool .\u00a0\u00a0Ideal candidates will demonstrate strong online teaching skills, including the ability to foster student connection and engagement, facilitate interactive and accessible online learning experiences, communicate effectively in virtual settings, provide timely feedback and support, and utilize educational technology to enhance student learning and success. The S chool seeks applicants with expertise in one or more of the following areas: micro practice, community practice, policy practice, health, clinical social work practice, mental health, substance abuse, or other clincial experience. We also welcome candidates with social work training experience, including the following: simulation, supervising social workers and social work students, and continuing education. The Online BASW Program is launching Fall 2026 and is prepped to reach students across the nation and internationally. The Anne and Henry Zarrow School of Social Work is h ome to the largest MSW program in Oklahoma. The School offers fully accredited Master and Bachelor Social Work Programs to 9 00 students annually, and is dedicated to the enhancement of human well-being through high quality education and research . Visit socialwork.ou.edu for more information. Oklahoma is a state with geographic diversity including urban, metropolitan, rural and frontier areas, and considerable socio-demographic and cultural diversity. Oklahoma ranks low in performance on almost all health and well-being measures, and this indication of high need places the Anne and Henry Zarrow School of Social Work in a position of responding to considerable social problems. With two physical campuses (Norman and Tulsa), and an Online BASW and MSW Program, the School delivers the largest undergraduate and graduate programs of social work in the state and has the responsibility for educating a diverse array of graduate students for a variety of professional Social Work practice fields. The School thrives on a collegial, engaged, and supportive environment. The University of Oklahoma (OU) is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement, serving the educational, cultural, economic and healthcare needs of the state, region, and nation from three campuses: Norman, Health Sciences Center in Oklahoma City, the Schusterman Center in Tulsa , and rapidly expanding online delivery programs . OU enrolls over 30,000 students and has more than 2700 full-time faculty members in 21 colleges. In 2014, OU became the first public institution ever to rank #1 nationally in the recruitment of National Merit Scholars, with 311 scholars. Position requirements : MSW degree and a minimum of 2 years of post-MSW practice experience required . Applicants must reside in, or be willing to relocate to, the Oklahoma City Metro area prior to the start date. Preferred : Licensed Clinical Social Worker (LCSW) . Previous experience teaching, serving as a field instructor, and/or faculty field liaison is preferred . How to apply: Application materials are reviewed until the position is filled and must include (1) a letter of interest, (2) a curriculum vitae, and (3) a minimum of three confidential letters of reference. Position will remain open until filled. Reports to: Director and Associate Director Questions: Jennifer Dell, BASW Program Director at jenniferdell@ou.edu Materials are uploaded to: https://apply.interfolio.com/185734"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189450": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Management and International Business - Organizational Behavior", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Michael F. Price College of Business: Division of Management and International Business Location: Norman, Oklahoma The Division of Management & International Business at the University of Oklahoma, Price College of Business is pleased to announce that applications are being accepted for a tenure-track position in Organizational Behavior at the rank of Assistant/Associate Professor. The candidate must have an earned doctorate in a relevant field. Evidence of high-level scholarly contributions, or the potential to create such contributions, to a relevant literature is required. We are particularly interested in applicants who may have an interest in teaching negotiations and leadership. Candidates must be committed to excellence in research and teaching, to collegiality, and to fulfilling the mission of the Price College ( https://www.ou.edu/price ). This position offers a competitive salary along with research and travel support. Teaching responsibilities may include in-person or online undergraduate courses and/or courses in our MBA program, which has various formats, including online and in-person at our 27,000 square-foot flagship facility for the Gene Rainbolt Graduate School of Business in the heart of Oklahoma City at OU's University Research Park on the Health Sciences Center campus ( https://www.ou.edu/price/mba ). Applications will be reviewed starting September 4, and the process will continue until the position is filled. The University of Oklahoma is a comprehensive research university offering a wide range of undergraduate, masters, and Ph.D. programs plus an extensive continuing education and public service program. The Michael F. Price College of Business is AACSB accredited at all levels with over 7,000 students in bachelors, masters, and Ph.D. programs. In addition, the Price College of Business has recently launched the Price Executive Academy, providing continuing Executive Education for business leaders. The Division of Management & International Business in the Michael F. Price College of Business is composed of faculty with interests in organizational behavior, human resource management, international business, strategic management, sports business, leadership, and legal studies. We currently offer undergraduate degrees in Management, Energy Management, International Business, and Sports Business. We also offer certificates in Leadership, Legal Studies, and Human Resource Management. We deliver required courses for multiple master\u2019s programs. In addition, we provide Ph.D. concentrations in Organizational Behavior and Strategic Management. Currently, the division has 25 full-time faculty members, including the following management scholars: Ronald Anderson, Nick Bartkoski, Ana Bolino, Mark Bolino (Division Director), Nathan Black, Bret Bradley, Becky Burink, Jaron Harvey, Inn Hee Gee, Christina Li, Shawn McClean, Brandi McManus, Oleg Petrenko, Troy Smith, Trevor Watkins, and Zhenyu Yuan. Our faculty is an active group of collaboratively minded scholars whose work has appeared regularly in premier outlets such as the Academy of Management Annals, Academy of Management Journal, Academy of Management Review, Journal of Applied Psychology, Journal of Management, Journal of International Business Studies, Leadership Quarterly, Organizational Behavior and Human Decision Processes,\u00a0Personnel Psychology, and Strategic Management Journal. Several faculty also fulfill major editorial roles in these and other premier outlets. The city of Norman, Oklahoma, is a vibrant community of approximately 130,000 residents and is often ranked among the lowest in the nation in terms of cost of living. The OU Campus is home to the Fred Jones Museum of Art, featuring a collection of impressionistic art valued at over $100 million, including works of Degas, Monet, Cassatt, Van Gogh, Pissarro, Renoir, and others. The campus also houses the Sam Noble Oklahoma Museum of Natural History, which won the National Medal for Museum and Library Science in 2014. In 2015, CBS Sports named the University of Oklahoma as having the best collegiate athletics program in the country, and in 2024, OU joined the Southeastern Conference (SEC). Located less than twenty miles from Norman, Oklahoma City is home to an international airport, numerous restaurants, retailers, the 2025 NBA Champions (Oklahoma City Thunder), AAA Baseball, USL Soccer, a world-class symphony, ballet, a traveling Broadway Series, and the Oklahoma City Museum of Art which boasts one of the world's most comprehensive collections from famed glass artist Dale Chihuly. Oklahoma City's Boathouse District is a training site for Olympians and offers recreational boating and cycling along the Oklahoma River. Oklahoma City has also been chosen to host two 2028 Olympic sports (canoe slalom and softball) and was named by U.S. News and World Report as the #1 Best Big City to Live in the United States for 2025-2026. Norman is also less than three hours away from the Dallas-Fort Worth Metroplex, one of the fastest growing regions of the United States. In sum, the city of Norman offers all of the benefits of living in a college town, while affording easy access to the amenities of larger cities. Required Criteria: \u2022 Earned doctorate in management or related field. \u2022 Research record, teaching experience, and other qualifications commensurate with the Assistant/Associate Professor level. Preferred Criteria: \u2022 Interest and expertise in teaching leadership and negotiations \u2022 Interest and ability to collaborate with Division faculty \u2022 Record of teaching success To apply, please upload a cover letter, current vita, names and contact information for three references, plus a summary of teaching evaluations at Interfolio . For additional information, please contact Mark Bolino (mbolino@ou.edu) or call (405) 325-2651 ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190712": {"title": "Renewable Term Instructor/Lecturer of Management and International Business - Sports Business", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Michael F. Price College of Business: Division of Management and International Business Location: Norman Center for Sports and Entertainment Business | Price College of Business | University of Oklahoma Division of Management & International Business Location: Norman, Oklahoma Anticipated start date: January or August 2027 Position Overview The Division of Management & International Business at the University of Oklahoma's Price College of Business invites applications for a renewable term faculty position in Sports Business at the rank of Instructor or Lecturer. The position sits within the Undergraduate and Graduate Education programs of the Center for Sports and Entertainment Business \u2014 a college-wide initiative built on four pillars: Executive Programs, Undergraduate and Graduate Education, Experiential Programs, and Thought Leadership. The Price College Sports Business program is, first and foremost, a business program. Our students are business students preparing for careers in the sports industry, and our faculty teach sports business through the core disciplines of business \u2014 finance, marketing, strategy, analytics, management, and economics. As a program within an AACSB-accredited college of business, we are distinct from sport management programs housed in other academic units, and we approach the industry with the rigor and a focus on AACSB standards. We seek an educator who brings a strong background in \u2014 or a genuine enthusiasm for \u2014 experiential learning, and who embraces mentoring students as a natural part of the teaching role. In our program, experiential learning and student mentorship are woven into the way our faculty teach rather than added on top of it, and the successful candidate will bring both into the classroom and the broader life of the program. The position offers a competitive salary along with annual support for professional development, travel, and research. A Distinctive Sports Market The position is situated within one of the most dynamic sports markets in the country. The Oklahoma City metropolitan area is undergoing significant development, including a new downtown arena for the NBA's Oklahoma City Thunder, a multipurpose stadium that will host professional soccer and a United Football League franchise, and \u2014 for the first time in the state's history \u2014 Olympic competition, as Oklahoma City stages two 2028 Olympic events in partnership with Team OKC. While the position is based at the University of Oklahoma, the surrounding region offers a rich landscape of organizations and events that can serve as the foundation for meaningful experiential learning. As these venues come online and the 2028 Games approach, the successful candidate will help prepare students to pursue the opportunities this growth creates. Teaching Responsibilities The successful candidate will primarily teach undergraduate courses in a 4/4 format. However, there will be future opportunities to teach graduate courses as well as online courses. We place a high value on experiential learning and expect faculty to bring applied, hands-on approaches \u2014 industry-connected projects, site visits, consulting engagements, and undergraduate research \u2014 into the courses they teach. Teaching assignments will be shaped around the candidate's academic and professional strengths and the evolving needs of the program, but there will always be opportunities to propose and develop new courses as the program grows. Mentorship and Student Development Mentorship is central to how our sports business faculty serve students. Alongside their teaching, the successful candidate will advise and mentor students on their academic progress and career development. They will connect classroom learning to contemporary research and professional practice. They will support students as they pursue experiential learning opportunities like internships, research, consulting projects, site visits, and study abroad experiences. This work reflects the shared commitment our faculty make to student success and shapes the way our courses and programs are designed; it is part of what it means to teach in this program rather than a separate assignment. About the University, College, and Division The University of Oklahoma is a comprehensive research university offering a wide range of undergraduate, master's, and Ph.D. programs plus an extensive continuing education and public service program. The Michael F. Price College of Business is AACSB accredited at all levels with approximately 6,000 students in bachelor's, master's, and Ph.D. programs. In addition, the Price College of Business has recently launched the Price Executive Academy, providing continuing executive education for business leaders. The Division of Management & International Business in the Michael F. Price College of Business is composed of faculty with interests in organizational behavior, human resource management, international business, sports business, strategic management, leadership, and legal studies. The Division offers undergraduate degrees in Management, Energy Management, International Business, and Sports Business, as well as certificates in Leadership, Legal Studies, and Human Resource Management. It delivers required courses for multiple master's programs and provides Ph.D. concentrations in Organizational Behavior, International Business, and Strategic Management. About the Community The city of Norman, Oklahoma, is a vibrant community of approximately 130,000 residents and is often ranked among the lowest in the nation in cost of living. The OU campus is home to the Fred Jones Museum of Art, featuring a collection of impressionistic art valued at over $100 million, including works of Degas, Monet, Cassatt, Van Gogh, Pissarro, and Renoir, and the Sam Noble Oklahoma Museum of Natural History, which won the National Medal for Museum and Library Science in 2014. In 2015, CBS Sports named the University of Oklahoma as having the best collegiate athletics program in the country, and in 2024, OU joined the Southeastern Conference (SEC). Located less than twenty miles from Norman, Oklahoma City is home to an international airport, numerous restaurants and retailers, the 2025 NBA Champion Oklahoma City Thunder, AAA baseball, USL soccer, UFL football, two 2028 LA Olympics event venues, a world-class symphony, a ballet, a traveling Broadway series, and the Oklahoma City Museum of Art, which holds one of the world's most comprehensive collections from famed glass artist Dale Chihuly. Oklahoma City's Boathouse District is a training site for Olympians and offers recreational boating and cycling along the Oklahoma River. Oklahoma City was recently named by U.S. News and World Report as the #1 Best Big City to Live in the United States for 2025-2026. Norman is also less than three hours from the Dallas-Fort Worth Metroplex, one of the fastest-growing regions of the United States. In sum, Norman offers the benefits of a college town while affording easy access to the amenities of larger cities. Qualifications Required: \u2022 A master's degree in business, sport management, or a related field \u2022 A grounding in one or more core business disciplines (such as finance, marketing, strategy, analytics, management, or economics) and the ability to teach sports business from that foundation \u2022\u00a0 An outstanding teaching record or, for candidates transitioning from industry, demonstrated experience in professional instruction, training, or mentorship \u2022 A strong background in, or a clear enthusiasm for, experiential and applied approaches to teaching \u2022 A commitment to advising and mentoring students, with excellent communication and interpersonal skills Preferred: \u2022 A Ph.D. or other terminal degree in a related field \u2022 Professional experience (and network) in the sports industry \u2014 including collegiate athletics, professional sport, agencies, brands, national governing bodies, or sports business organizations \u2014 that can enrich experiential learning \u2022 Experience designing or delivering experiential learning activities, internships, or applied student projects, both on and off campus Application Process Applications must include the following materials: \u2022\u00a0 A current curriculum vitae \u2022\u00a0 A cover letter that addresses (1) the applicant's teaching philosophy, including the use of experiential and applied learning in the classroom, and (2) the applicant's approach to mentoring and supporting students in their academic and professional development \u2022\u00a0 Evidence of teaching effectiveness or, for candidates transitioning from industry, evidence of professional instruction, presentation, or mentorship experience \u2022\u00a0 The names and contact information of three professional references Review of applications will begin September 12 and will continue until the position is filled. Questions regarding the position may be directed to Dr. Sean Daly: seanfdaly@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/171749": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Management Information Systems - Tenure Track", "description": "Posted: Jul 28, 2026 Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Michael F. Price College of Business: Division of Management Information Systems Location: Norman, OK The University of Oklahoma (OU), invites applications to fill one tenure-track Assistant Professor position in the MIS division starting in the Fall of 2027.\u202f Our strong programs in the MIS division build on a history of excellence, distinguished faculty, and active industry partnerships.\u202f The division offers an undergraduate major and minor in MIS, graduate programs in Business Analytics, both in-person and online, and a Ph.D. degree.\u202f It operates the Center for MIS Studies (CMISS), an industry-academic partnership that brings together faculty and business partners to collaborate on research and curriculum development. The division has an active MIS Student Association, provides sizable student scholarships annually, and enjoys strong support from the university and industry partners.\u202f Faculty members are world-renowned, engaged in thriving research programs, generate external research funding, hold leadership positions in leading journals and conferences, and are outstanding teachers. Many opportunities exist for professional growth and recognition as an MIS faculty member and a member of OU\u2019s Michael F. Price College of Business. The University of Oklahoma is classified as an R1, Very High Research University by the Carnegie Foundation, generates substantial research funding, and has a growing number of endowed faculty chairs and professorships. Norman, where the main campus is located, is a culturally rich and vibrant town just outside Oklahoma City and close to other major cities. For more information about our MIS Division, please visit http://price.ou.edu/mis Candidates must have a PhD in Management Information Systems or a closely related discipline, or be on track to complete their PhD by the position start date. ABD candidates and faculty currently in an assistant professor position at another institution are encouraged to apply. Applicants should demonstrate strong potential for excellence in both research and teaching. Preference will be given to candidates who are prepared to teach general MIS classes (e.g., Databases, Intro to MIS), classes on cybersecurity, AI, and data analytics at the undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral levels. Salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications. Interested candidates should submit their application materials \u2013 CV, research plan, teaching plan including teaching evaluations, copies of 1-2 papers, and names of three references at https://apply.interfolio.com/171749 . The cover letter may address examples of the candidate\u2019s approach to research, teaching, service, and graduate student mentorship. Additional materials may be requested at a later date.\u202f The screening will begin on August 1, 2026 . The search committee will reach out to candidates who best meet the position requirements.\u202f The search will remain open until the position is filled. Please reach out to Kristin Stokes ( wkstokes@ou.edu ) if you have any issues submitting your application."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187568": {"title": "RT Instructor/Lecturer of Management Information Systems", "description": "Posted: Jun 11, 2026 Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Michael F. Price College of Business: Division of Management Information Systems Location: Norman, OK Price College of Business is seeking dynamic educators to contribute to our Division of Management Information Systems (MIS), known for its excellence, distinguished faculty, and robust industry partnerships. This position offers a unique chance to shape future leaders in management information systems through teaching and research in our vibrant academic setting. We provide a collaborative environment that emphasizes practical application and industry relevance across our undergraduate and graduate programs. Our strong connections are highlighted through the Center for MIS Studies, where faculty and business leaders collaborate on research and curriculum development. With opportunities for professional growth in areas like healthcare and business analytics, and resources like our Graduate Education Center in Oklahoma City, we offer a supportive and expanding community for personal and professional development. Master\u2019s degree or PhD in Management Information Systems or a related field, with demonstrated knowledge of SAP and other core MIS topics. Demonstrated ability to teach core MIS courses, including areas such as business processes, Excel-based data analysis, enterprise systems, databases, and introduction to MIS. Experience applying SAP concepts in academic or professional settings, with the ability to connect enterprise systems to real-world business operations. Strong communication and instructional skills, with the ability to explain technical and business concepts in an accessible and engaging manner to a diverse student population. E xperience with online teaching and the ability to effectively engage students in multiple instructional formats. Interested candidates should submit their CV , and cover letter at https://apply.interfolio.com/187568 . The cover letter may address examples of the candidate\u2019s approach to teaching, service, and student mentorship. Additional materials may be requested at a later date. The screening will begin immediately . The search committee will reach out to candidates who best meet the position requirements. The search will remain open until the position is filled. Please reach out to Kristin Stokes ( wkstokes@ou.edu ) if you have any issues submitting your application."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188322": {"title": "Open Rank Professor of Management Information Systems - Ranked Renewable Term", "description": "Posted: Jun 23, 2026 Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Michael F. Price College of Business: Division of Management Information Systems Location: Norman, OK The MIS Division of the Price College of Business, University of Oklahoma (OU), invites applications for a full-time Ranked Renewable Term Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor of M anagement Information Systems ( non-tenure track ). Initial appointments are for three years , and ranked renewable term faculty who meet the appropriate qualification requirements may be eligible for promotion in rank.\u202f RRT Faculty are expected to cover 3 to 4 courses per semester, be active in research , and perform assigned administrative duties.\u202f Preferred applicants will have experience covering courses in cybersecurity , artificial intelligence, ERP systems, and data analytics at both undergraduate and graduate levels. The appointment is expected to start in the Fall of 202 6 .\u202f Our strong programs in the MIS division build on a history of excellence, distinguished faculty, and active industry partnerships .\u202f The division offers an undergraduate major and minor in MIS, graduate programs in Business Analytics, both in-person and online , and a\u202fPhD degree.\u202f It operates the Center for MIS Studies (CMISS),\u202fan industry-academic partnership, through which faculty and business partners collaborate on research and curriculum development. Candidates with demonstrated leadership experience and a strong record of industry engagement may be considered for the role of Director of CMISS, though this is not a requirement of the position. The division has an active MIS Student Association, provides sizable student scholarships annually, and enjoys strong support from the university and industry partners.\u202f Faculty members are world-renowned, engaged in thriving research programs, generate external research funding, hold leadership positions in leading journals and conferences, and are outstanding teachers. Many opportunities exist for professional growth\u202fand recognition as a MIS faculty member and member\u202fof\u202fOU\u2019s Michael F. Price College of Business. For more information about our MIS Division, please visit http://price.ou.edu/mis Candidates must have earned a PhD in M anagement Information Systems or related disciplines by the position start date .\u202f Desirable qualifications include an established record of excellence in teaching and the ability to conduct and publish articles in peer-reviewed journals. Preference will be given to applicants whose teaching and research capabilities align well with the division\u2019s needs. Candidates with prior experience leading industry-academic partnerships or research centers are especially encouraged to apply . Salary is competitive and will depend on qualifications. Interested candidates should submit their application materials \u2013 CV, teaching plan , including teaching evaluations, research plan, copies of 1-2 peer reviewed papers, and names of three professional references at apply.interfolio.com/188322 . The cover letter may address examples of the candidate\u2019s approach to research, teaching, service, and graduate student mentorship. Additional materials may be requested at a later date. The screening will begin immediately . The search committee will reach out to candidates who best meet the position requirements. The search will remain open until the position is filled. Please reach out to Kristin Stokes ( wkstokes@ou.edu ) if you have any issues submitting your application."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193774": {"title": "Open Rank Professor of Management Information Systems", "description": "Posted: Sep 21, 2026 Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Michael F. Price College of Business: Division of Management Information Systems Location: Norman Open Rank Professor of MIS (Tenured or Tenure-Track) The University of Oklahoma (OU) invites applications for one open-rank tenured or tenure-track (Assistant, Associate, Full) Professor position in the Management Information Systems (MIS) Division of the Michael F. Price College of Business, beginning in Fall 2027.\u00a0 Our strong programs in the MIS division build on a history of excellence, distinguished faculty, and active industry partnerships.\u00a0 The division offers an undergraduate major and minor in MIS, graduate programs in Business Analytics, both in-person and online, and a Ph.D. degree.\u00a0 It operates the Center for MIS Studies (CMISS), an industry-academic partnership that brings together faculty and business partners to collaborate on research and curriculum development. The division has an active MIS Student Association, provides sizable student scholarships annually, and enjoys strong support from the university and industry partners.\u00a0 Faculty members are world-renowned, engaged in thriving research programs, generate external research funding, hold leadership positions in leading journals and conferences, and are outstanding teachers. Many opportunities exist for professional growth and recognition as an MIS faculty member and a member of OU\u2019s Michael F. Price College of Business. The University of Oklahoma is classified as an R1, Very High Research University by the Carnegie Foundation, generates substantial research funding, and has a growing number of endowed faculty chairs and professorships. Norman, where the main campus is located, is a culturally rich and vibrant town just outside Oklahoma City and close to other major cities. For more information about our MIS program, please visit http://price.ou.edu/mis . Candidates must have a PhD in Management Information Systems or a closely related discipline, or be on track to complete their PhD by the position start date. ABD candidates and faculty currently in an assistant, associate, or full professor position at another institution are encouraged to apply. Applicants should demonstrate strong potential for excellence in both research and teaching. Preference will be given to candidates who are prepared to teach general MIS classes (e.g., Databases, Intro to MIS), classes on cybersecurity, AI, and data analytics at the undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral levels. Salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications. Interested candidates should submit their application materials \u2013 CV, research plan, teaching plan including teaching evaluations, copies of 1-2 papers, and names of three references at apply.interfolio.com/193774. The cover letter may address examples of the candidate\u2019s approach to research, teaching, and service. For applicants at the associate or full professor level, the letter may also address leadership and mentorship in the academic community. Additional materials may be requested at a later date.\u00a0 The screening will begin in mid October, 2026.\u00a0The search committee will reach out to candidates who best meet the position requirements.\u00a0 The search will remain open until the position is filled. Please reach out to Kristin Stokes ( wkstokes@ou.edu ) if you have any issues submitting your application."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/174690": {"title": "Associate or Full Professor of Marketing - Michael F. Price Chair with Tenure", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Michael F. Price College of Business: Division of Marketing and Supply Chain Management Location: Norman, OK The Marketing & Supply Chain Management Division in the Michael F. Price College of Business at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for a tenured appointment at the rank of Associate or Full Professor to hold the Michael F. Price Chair in Marketing. We seek a scholar of national or international distinction who will sustain a visible, high-impact research agenda with publications in premier journals\u2014such as those recognized by the UT Dallas Top 24 (UTD 24) and Financial Times 50 (FT50) lists, or equivalent leading journals appropriate to the candidate\u2019s area. The successful candidate will be expected to maintain an active research pipeline that advances knowledge in marketing and related fields. Responsibilities also include teaching and innovating across undergraduate, master\u2019s, doctoral, and executive levels, in multiple delivery formats (in-person, online, and hybrid). A strong commitment to doctoral education is essential, encompassing mentoring, advising, and supporting the placement of doctoral students, as well as contributing to the development of doctoral programs. In addition, the successful candidate will provide leadership and service to the Division, College, University, and profession through activities such as editorial board service, conference leadership, and academic governance. Engagement with industry and executive education is strongly valued, as is a demonstrated ability to build partnerships that connect scholarship with practice. While the area of specialization is open, candidates whose expertise lies in marketing strategy, digital and data-driven marketing, including AI-enabled marketing and analytics, are especially encouraged to apply. The successful candidate should show evidence of sustained impact in the field, a history of scholarly leadership, and the ability to catalyze cross-disciplinary or cross-unit initiatives . About the Division of Marketing & Supply Chain Management The MSCM Division houses undergraduate programs in Supply Chain Management, Marketing, and Healthcare Business, supports multiple graduate programs, and hosts the Center for the Business of Healthcare. Price College and OU are AACSB-accredited, R1 research institutions, with strong undergraduate, master\u2019s, and doctoral programs. Faculty members are world-renowned, engaged in thriving research programs, generate external research funding, hold leadership positions in leading journals and conferences, and are outstanding teachers. Many opportunities exist for professional growth and recognition as an MSCM faculty member and a member of OU\u2019s Michael F. Price College of Business. About OU\u2019s Price College of Business The Price College of Business at OU continues to flourish in a period of growth and expansion. The Price College of Business is AACSB-accredited, with approximately 7,000 business majors, nearly 1,000 master\u2019s students, and 50 doctoral students. Price College is also growing its online programs, executive education, and study abroad activities. Several Price undergraduate and master\u2019s programs have been ranked among the nation's top programs. About OU The University of Oklahoma (OU) is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement, serving the educational, cultural, economic, and healthcare needs of the state, region, and nation from three campuses: Norman, Health Campus in Oklahoma City and the Schusterman Center in Tulsa. OU enrolls over 36,000 students and has more than 2700 full-time faculty members in 21 colleges. The mission of the University of Oklahoma is to provide the best possible educational experience for our students through excellence in teaching, research, and creative activity, as well as service to the state and society. About Norman, OK The city of Norman, Oklahoma is a vibrant community of approximately 130,000 residents located outside Oklahoma City and less than three hours from Dallas/Ft. Worth. Norman is a perennial contender on \"Best Places to Live\" rankings with outstanding schools, nearby amenities, and is often ranked among the lowest in the nation in terms of cost of living. Norman's exceptional cost of living allows for a quality of life typically associated with much higher salaries, while proximity to major metropolitan areas provides access to corporate partnerships and professional networks. In addition to numerous boutiques, shopping, and restaurants located directly north of campus, Norman is nicknamed the 'City of Festivals' due to its numerous art, music, medieval, and other festivals held annually. The OU Campus is home to the Fred Jones Museum of Art, featuring a collection of impressionistic art valued at over $100 million, including works of Degas, Monet, Cassatt, Van Gogh, Pissarro, Renoir, and others. The campus also houses the Sam Noble Oklahoma Museum of Natural History, which won the National Medal for Museum and Library Science in 2014. For more information, please refer to: \u00b7 Price College of Business Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Marketing or a closely related discipline. A strong record of publishing in premier journals, such as those recognized by the UT Dallas Top 24 (UTD 24) and Financial Times 50 (FT50) lists, or equivalent leading outlets. Demonstrated excellence in teaching at the undergraduate, graduate, or executive levels. Record of professional service to the discipline, such as editorial board membership, conference leadership, or service in academic societies. Preferred Qualifications Strong evidence of an ongoing, high-quality and high-impact research program in theory-driven empirical marketing. Experience mentoring and successfully placing doctoral students and teaching doctoral seminars in quantitative marketing. Active engagement with the business community through applied research, consulting, industry partnerships, or executive education. Salary is highly competitive and will depend upon qualifications. Qualified individuals may apply via apply.interfolio.com/174690 . Application materials should include a cover letter, a current curriculum vita (resume), a research statement, evidence of effective teaching, and the names and contact information of three references. Questions regarding the Interfolio application process may be directed to\u00a0 the search committee Chair, Dr. Qiong Wang at (qiongwang@ou.edu). Screening of applications will begin immediately, but applications will continue to be accepted until the position is filled. Applications should include: 1. Cover Letter \u2013 Clearly state your area of specialization in Marketing and summarize your interest in the position. 2. Curriculum Vitae (CV) \u2013 Include education, professional experience, teaching history, publications, and other scholarly or professional activities. 3. Research Statement \u2013 Outline your current scholarly engagement and describe how it aligns with the mission of the MSCM Division and Price College of Business. 4. Evidence of Teaching Effectiveness \u2013 Such as recent student evaluations, peer reviews, teaching awards, sample syllabi, or summaries of instructional innovations. 5. Record of Professional Service to the Discipline \u2013 Such as editorial board membership, conference leadership, or service in academic societies. 6. Names and Contact Information for Three References \u2013 Include email addresses and phone numbers. Letters of recommendation may be requested at a later stage."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/182535": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Marketing and Supply Chain Management - Marketing (Tenure-Track)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Michael F. Price College of Business: Division of Marketing and Supply Chain Management Location: Norman, OK The Marketing & Supply Chain Management Division in the Michael F. Price College of Business at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for a tenure-track appointment at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor in Marketing. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in Marketing or a closely related field at the start of the appointment. Individuals with an established record of producing high\u2011quality scholarly research in leading Marketing journals\u2014such as the Journal of Marketing , Journal of Marketing Research , Journal of Consumer Research , Marketing Science , and the Journal of the Academy of Marketing Science \u2014are particularly encouraged to apply.\u00a0Our faculty members are expected to pursue an active research program, perform undergraduate and graduate teaching, supervise graduate students, and engage in service activities. Faculty responsibilities include pursuing an active research program, demonstrating excellence in teaching empirical or quantitative marketing courses across undergraduate, MBA, M.S., and Ph.D. levels, supervising graduate students, and contributing to service activities within the department, college, university, and the broader academic profession. About the Division of Marketing & Supply Chain Management The MSCM Division houses undergraduate programs in Supply Chain Management, Marketing, and Healthcare Business, supports multiple graduate programs, and hosts the Center for the Business of Healthcare. Price College and OU are AACSB-accredited, R1 research institutions, with strong undergraduate, master\u2019s, and doctoral programs. Faculty members are world-renowned, engaged in thriving research programs, generate external research funding, hold leadership positions in leading journals and conferences, and are outstanding teachers. Many opportunities exist for professional growth and recognition as an MSCM faculty member and a member of OU\u2019s Michael F. Price College of Business. About OU\u2019s Price College of Business The Price College of Business at OU continues to flourish in a period of growth and expansion. The Price College of Business is AACSB-accredited, with approximately 7,000 business majors, nearly 1,000 master\u2019s students, and 50 doctoral students. Price College is also growing its online programs, executive education, and study abroad activities. Several Price undergraduate and master\u2019s programs have been ranked among the nation's top programs. About OU The University of Oklahoma (OU) is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement, serving the educational, cultural, economic, and healthcare needs of the state, region, and nation from three campuses: Norman, Health Campus in Oklahoma City and the Schusterman Center in Tulsa. OU enrolls over 36,000 students and has more than 2700 full-time faculty members in 21 colleges. The mission of the University of Oklahoma is to provide the best possible educational experience for our students through excellence in teaching, research, and creative activity, as well as service to the state and society. About Norman, OK The city of Norman, Oklahoma is a vibrant community of approximately 130,000 residents located outside Oklahoma City and less than three hours from Dallas/Ft. Worth. Norman is a perennial contender on \"Best Places to Live\" rankings with outstanding schools, nearby amenities, and is often ranked among the lowest in the nation in terms of cost of living. Norman's exceptional cost of living allows for a quality of life typically associated with much higher salaries, while proximity to major metropolitan areas provides access to corporate partnerships and professional networks. In addition to numerous boutiques, shopping, and restaurants located directly north of campus, Norman is nicknamed the 'City of Festivals' due to its numerous art, music, medieval, and other festivals held annually. The OU Campus is home to the Fred Jones Museum of Art, featuring a collection of impressionistic art valued at over $100 million, including works of Degas, Monet, Cassatt, Van Gogh, Pissarro, Renoir, and others. The campus also houses the Sam Noble Oklahoma Museum of Natural History, which won the National Medal for Museum and Library Science in 2014. For more information, please refer to: \u00b7 Price College of Business Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Marketing or a closely related discipline. Preferred Qualifications Successful applicants are expected to: Have an established record of producing high quality, scholarly research published in the top journals in Marketing (e.g., Journal of Marketing, Journal of Marketing Research, Journal of Consumer Research, Marketing Science, Journal of Academy of Marketing Science). Provide evidence of excellence in teaching empirical or quantitative marketing courses across the undergraduate, MBA, M.S., and Ph.D. levels. If applicable, provide evidence of excellence in service activities within the department, college, university, and profession. Salary is highly competitive and will depend upon qualifications. Qualified individuals may apply via apply.interfolio.com/182535 . Application materials should include a cover letter, a current curriculum vita (resume), a research portfolio/plan, evidence of effective teaching, and the names and contact information of three references. Questions regarding the Interfolio application process may be directed to\u00a0 the search committee Chair, Dr. Qiong Wang at (qiongwang@ou.edu). Screening of applications will begin immediately, but applications will continue to be accepted until the position is filled. Applications should include: 1. Cover Letter \u2013 Clearly state your area of specialization in Marketing and summarize your interest in the position. 2. Curriculum Vitae (CV) \u2013 Include education, professional experience, teaching history, publications, and other scholarly or professional activities. 3. Research Portfolio/Plan \u2013 Outline your current scholarly engagement and describe how it aligns with the mission of the MSCM Division and Price College of Business. 4. Evidence of Teaching Effectiveness \u2013 Such as recent student evaluations, peer reviews, teaching awards, sample syllabi, or summaries of instructional innovations. 5. Record of Professional Service to the Discipline \u2013 Such as editorial board membership, conference leadership, or service in academic societies. 6. Names and Contact Information for Three References \u2013 Include email addresses and phone numbers. Letters of recommendation may be requested at a later stage."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193265": {"title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Marketing and Supply Chain Management", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Michael F. Price College of Business: Division of Marketing and Supply Chain Management Location: Norman, OK The Division of Marketing and Supply Chain Management (MSCM) in the Michael F. Price College of Business at the University of Oklahoma (OU) is experiencing an exciting period of growth and expansion. To support this growth, we invite applications for a tenure-track position in supply chain management at the rank of assistant professor or untenured associate professor. We seek candidates who conduct empirical research , with a preference for senior assistant professors and untenured associate professors. Doctoral candidates who expect to complete their degrees by August 2027 are also encouraged to apply. The appointment will begin in August 2027. Our Supply Chain Management (SCM) group supports research across a broad range of supply chain topics. Faculty members publish in premier journals, including Management Science , Production and Operations Management, J ournal of Operations Management , Manufacturing & Service Operations Management, and Journal of Supply Chain Management . The MSCM Division houses undergraduate programs in supply chain management, marketing, and healthcare business; supports many of the college\u2019s graduate programs; and recently launched an online Master of Science in Supply Chain Management program. This position is in the Supply Chain Management area . In addition to conducting and publishing high-quality research, the successful candidate will teach undergraduate and graduate courses. Primary teaching responsibilities will include supply chain management topics across our academic programs. The Michael F. Price College of Business continues to flourish. The college is accredited by AACSB International and enrolled more than 6,600 degree-seeking students in Spring 2026. Price College offers nationally ranked undergraduate and graduate business programs, including an undergraduate business program ranked No. 64 and a graduate business school ranked No. 85 by U.S. News & World Report . OU is\u00a0a comprehensive public research university designated by the Carnegie Classification as an R1 institution. Known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement, OU serves the educational, cultural, economic, and healthcare needs of the state, region, and nation through campuses in Norman, Oklahoma City, and Tulsa. The university enrolled more than 34,000 students in spring 2026. Norman is a culturally vibrant city located approximately 30 minutes south of Oklahoma City and less than three hours from the Dallas\u2013Fort Worth area. The city offers highly regarded schools, convenient amenities, and a relatively low cost of living and was named one of Livability\u2019s Top 100 Best Places to Live in 2025. For more information, please refer to these websites: http://www.ou.edu/price https://www.ou.edu/price/marketing-supply-chain-management Required qualifications: A doctoral degree (or ABD status) in supply chain management , operations management , or a closely related field. Commensurate with the candidate\u2019s experience, evidence of, or potential for conducting and publishing empirical research in premier supply chain management journals. Evidence of high-quality teaching. Preferred qualifications: Professional experience in supply chain management or a closely related field and experience engaging with the supply chain management business community. Experience teaching at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. Experience teaching online courses at the undergraduate or graduate level. Qualified individuals may apply at https://apply.interfolio.com/193265 . Application materials should include a cover letter, a current curriculum vitae, a research statement, a teaching statement, evidence of effective teaching and/or student evaluations, sample research publications or works in progress, and three confidential letters of recommendation. Questions about the Interfolio application process may be directed to Ms. Judy Stockdale (jstockdale@ou.edu). Questions about the position or search may be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. Mei Li (mei.li@ou.edu). Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Application Process The University of Oklahoma uses Interfolio Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants receive a free Dossier account and may submit all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, at no charge."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194112": {"title": "Open-Rank Professor of Marketing (Ranked Renewable Term, non-tenure track)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Michael F. Price College of Business: Division of Marketing and Supply Chain Management Location: Norman, OK The Marketing & Supply Chain Management Division (MSCM) of the Price College of Business, University of Oklahoma, is strategically positioning itself at the forefront of AI-driven marketing education and research. As part of a targeted expansion to build one of the nation's leading marketing programs, we seek a dynamic marketing scholar to help shape the future of marketing pedagogy and research. As part of this expansion, applications are invited for a full-time, open-rank, ranked-renewable term (RRT) faculty position in Marketing. Appointment rank will depend on experience and qualifications. This position will start in August 2027. This is a non-tenure track position with academic rank suitable for scholars ranging from new PhDs to advanced Assistant, Associate or full Professors. Duties will include teaching to both undergraduate and graduate students in the MSCM Division. Faculty are expected to maintain Scholarly Academic (SA) status, with an emphasis on pedagogical or practice-based research. Preference will be given to candidates with experience in teaching areas such as professional selling, sales analytics, digital marketing, social media marketing, marketing analytics, or marketing strategy. Familiarity with emerging technologies, including artificial intelligence (AI) applications in marketing, is considered an asset. RRT faculty typically teach four courses per semester. The division is providing substantial resources to support research productivity and industry engagement. Course assignments align with faculty expertise and research interests, with opportunities to develop signature courses in emerging areas. RRT faculty are also expected to contribute meaningfully to departmental and college service activities. Compensation will be competitive. Faculty also receives generous professional development funding. About the Division of Marketing & Supply Chain Management The MSCM Division houses undergraduate programs in Supply Chain Management, Marketing, and Healthcare Business, supports multiple graduate programs, and hosts the Center for the Business of Healthcare. Price College and OU are AACSB-accredited, R1 research institutions, with strong undergraduate, master\u2019s, and doctoral programs. Faculty members are world-renowned, engaged in thriving research programs, generate external research funding, hold leadership positions in leading journals and conferences, and are outstanding teachers. Many opportunities exist for professional growth and recognition as an MSCM faculty member and a member of OU\u2019s Michael F. Price College of Business. About OU\u2019s Price College of Business The Price College of Business at OU continues to flourish in a period of growth and expansion. The Price College of Business is AACSB-accredited, with over 7,000 business majors, nearly 1,000 master\u2019s students, and 50 doctoral students. Price College is also growing its online programs, executive education, and study abroad activities. Several Price undergraduate and master\u2019s programs have been ranked among the nation's top programs. About OU The University of Oklahoma (OU) is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement, serving the educational, cultural, economic, and healthcare needs of the state, region, and nation from three campuses: Norman, Harold Hamm Health Campus in Oklahoma City and the Schusterman Center in Tulsa. OU enrolls over 36,000 students and has more than 2700 full-time faculty members in 21 colleges. About Norman, OK The city of Norman, Oklahoma is a vibrant community of approximately 130,000 residents located outside Oklahoma City and less than three hours from Dallas/Ft. Worth. Norman is a perennial contender on \"Best Places to Live\" rankings with outstanding schools, nearby amenities, and is often ranked among the lowest in the nation in terms of cost of living. Norman's exceptional cost of living allows for a quality of life typically associated with much higher salaries, while proximity to major metropolitan areas provides access to corporate partnerships and professional networks. In addition to numerous boutiques, shopping, and restaurants located directly north of campus, Norman is nicknamed the 'City of Festivals' due to its numerous art, music, medieval, and other festivals held annually. The OU Campus is home to the Fred Jones Museum of Art, featuring a collection of impressionistic art valued at over $100 million, including works of Degas, Monet, Cassatt, Van Gogh, Pissarro, Renoir, and others. The campus also houses the Sam Noble Oklahoma Museum of Natural History, which won the National Medal for Museum and Library Science in 2014. For more information, please see: \u00b7 Price College of Business Required Qualifications \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Ph.D. in Marketing or a closely related discipline. Preferred Qualifications \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Demonstrated excellence in teaching undergraduate and/or graduate marketing courses, with a strong record of engaging students across various instructional formats, including in-person, online, and hybrid delivery. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Proven track record of scholarly or practice-based research in marketing, with publications in peer-reviewed academic journals or high-quality conference outlets. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Professional experience in marketing or sales, with a focus on AI-enabled marketing practices and data-driven decision-making. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Experience developing innovative curricula and courses aligned with industry needs. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Demonstrated success in curriculum innovation, instructional design, or program development that enhances student learning and career readiness. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Expertise in contemporary marketing areas such as marketing analytics, marketing automation, natural language processing (NLP), and customer data platforms (CDPs). \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Proficiency in data visualization and marketing-related software tools such as STATA, Tableau, R, Python, and similar platforms. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Ability to develop and deliver courses that prepare students for professional certifications (e.g., Google Analytics, Google Ads, HubSpot, Hootsuite). \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Engagement with marketing technology tools such as ChatGPT, Jasper, Salesforce Einstein, Adobe Sensei, and other AI-driven platforms. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Active collaboration with the business community through consulting, applied research, industry partnerships, or executive education. Salary is competitive and will depend upon qualifications. Qualified individuals may apply at apply.interfolio.com/194112 . Applications must include the following materials: 1. Cover Letter \u2013 Clearly state your area of specialization in Marketing and summarize your interest in the position. 2. Curriculum Vitae (CV) \u2013 Include education, professional experience, teaching history, publications, and other scholarly or professional activities. 3. Teaching Portf olio - Provide materials that demonstrate your teaching, instructional approach, course development, and commitment to student learning. 4. Student Evaluations - Submit recent course evaluation results or other evidence of teaching effectiveness. 5. Research Statement (Research Plan) - Describe your current research agenda, future scholarly goals, and the potential impact of your research program. 6. Names and Contact Information for Three References \u2013 Include email addresses and phone numbers. Letters of recommendation may be requested at a later stage. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. To ensure full consideration, applicants are encouraged to submit all materials as early as possible. Questions regarding the position or the Interfolio application process may be directed to Dr. Qiong Wang, Search Committee Chair, at qiongwang@ou.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/175035": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Dance - Modern", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Weitzenhoffer Family College of Fine Arts: School of Dance Location: Norman Campus The University of Oklahoma\u2019s Weitzenhoffer Family College of Fine Arts, School of Dance, is seeking an Assistant Professor of Modern Dance to start in August 2026. Housed in the Reynolds Performing Arts Center and annually ranked as one of the top institutions in ballet and modern dance, the school offers a BFA in, modern dance performance, ballet performance, ballet pedagogy and an MFA degree in dance. A strong foundation for the development and growth of the modern dance area has been built over the past several years, with enrollment increasing from 12 to 45 since 2005. Our school, consisting of over 80 dance majors, emphasizes performance, with multiple mainstage performance opportunities each year and highly refined training that prepares our graduates to meet the expectations of a professional career in dance and sustain multifaceted careers with both nationally and internationally recognized companies and organizations. Special consideration will be given to candidates who have established a significant career as a performer, teacher, director, and/or choreographer/repetiteur, and whose ranking in the profession is demonstrated by a substantial career in the national/international dance world. Teaching ability and experience in established techniques, such as but not limited to Graham, Dunham, Fagan, Cunningham, and Gaga, is preferred. Candidates with strengths in teaching improvisation, composition, and mentorship of student choreography are also preferred. Qualifications: \u00b7 MFA degree and/or exceptional professional experience in the field, to include but not limited to, employment with established companies (for a minimum of 5 years) that offer annual contracts for a minimum of 35 weeks \u00b7 Experience in teaching undergraduates and the ability to teach graduate-level courses. \u00b7 Ability to choreograph and/or stage works from diverse national and international repertoires. International connections with leading choreographers and companies in the field are preferred. \u00b7 Minimum of 2 years teaching experience and/or professional performance background of at least 5 years with an established dance company. Responsibilities: \u00b7 Teach all levels of Modern Dance Technique to undergraduate majors and non-majors, Dance Composition, Improvisation Technique, and lecture courses on topics such as Dance Appreciation, in addition to subjects unique to the selected candidate\u2019s expertise. \u00b7 Teach graduate-level courses such as Introduction to Graduate Studies and Advanced Teaching Methods. \u00b7 Mentor and academically advise undergraduate and MFA majors. \u00b7 Advise student choreographic projects \u00b7 Participate in faculty governance of the school, college, and university. \u00b7 Contribute to undergraduate and graduate curriculum development. \u00b7 Fulfill administrative duties typical to a tenure track position. \u00b7 Maintain an active research/creative activity practice. \u00b7 Choreograph and/or stage work for School of Dance majors annually. APPLICATION: Please submit a cover letter of application, statement of training and technical approach to teaching, statement of research/creative activity, complete vita, full names of three references along with email and phone contact information and three samples of research and/or creativity. Video links of teaching and/or choreography/works staged may be requested during the search process as well as letters of reference. Screening of applicants will begin January 10, 2026, and the position will remain open until filled. For additional information please contact Dustin Trotter: dance@ou.edu or (405) 325-4051. APPLICATION PROCESS: This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191811": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Drama", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Weitzenhoffer Family College of Fine Arts: Peggy Dow Helmerich School of Drama Location: Norman, OK The Peggy Dow Helmerich School of Drama in the Weitzenhoffer Family College of Fine Arts at The University of Oklahoma seeks applicants for a full-time, tenure-track\u00a0Assistant/Associate\u00a0Professor of Acting and Performance Area Coordinator to start in August 2027. This is a nine-month faculty appointment with a 2/3 course load and an administrative service position component. The salary range is $64,000-$75,000\u00a0dependent\u00a0on the rank of\u00a0the\u00a0initial hire. This position provides\u00a0an\u00a0opportunity to teach at the heart of a nationally respected actor training program and shape the artistic and curricular direction of the Performance Area at an R1 research institution. Responsibilities include: For the service component of the position as the Performance Area Coordinator, the initial appointment is one year with the possibility to renew the service position every 3 years. If not renewed, there will be no salary reduction. This position will lead the Performance Area faculty (BFA program in Drama: Acting or Dramaturgy Emphasis; MFA in Drama: Directing Option). Other responsibilities may include coordinating/organizing area event schedules, including but not limited to monthly area faculty meetings, acting auditions, and student assessments; maintaining class lists; evaluating petitions and distributing student forms; facilitating curriculum review; communicating with the director and guest artists; representing the area in faculty meetings and recruitment events; and handling student concerns and conflict resolution. Teaching foundational courses in the BFA in Theatre: Acting Emphasis curriculum, selected from Introduction to Acting, Character Study for the Actor, Scene Study for the Actor, Performing Shakespeare, and other upper-level acting technique courses. Developing new courses in area(s) of specialty. Mentoring undergraduate students. Contributing to recruitment efforts. Regularly participating in School of Drama productions as a director or in a similar artistic capacity and contributing positively to the training of School of Drama majors. Maintaining an active research/creative activity profile commensurate with the expectations of an R1 institution. Directing a School of Drama production may count toward creative activity or teaching, pending prior negotiation with the director of the school. Providing service to the department, college, university, and profession. The Peggy Dow Helmerich School of Drama is a vibrant community of artists and storytellers. Through rigorous and innovative curricular training and production practice, we empower students to explore the full spectrum of theatrical expression and their work as artists; we develop skilled, risk-taking, and compassionate professionals who contribute to the betterment of society; and we champion interdisciplinary collaboration and creative, critical inquiry to prepare graduates for meaningful careers in theatre and beyond. Housed in an R1 research institution, the School of Drama combines the advantages of a liberal arts education with conservatory-style BFA training in Acting, Costume Design, Dramaturgy, Lighting Design, Scenic Design, Stage Management, and Sound Design, as well as professional MFA training in Directing. More information about our program can be found at drama.ou.edu. Required Qualifications for Assistant Professor rank: An MFA in Acting/Directing and/or a PhD in Theatre/Performance Studies. Experience in the practice and pedagogy of actor training at the university level. Evidence of sustained professional practice in theatre, film, television, audio, content creation, and/or related media. Required Qualification for Associate Professor rank: An MFA in Acting/Directing and/or a PhD in Theatre/Performance Studies. Six years of experience in the practice and pedagogy of actor training at the university level. Evidence of prolific and high caliber professional practice in theatre, film, television, audio, content creation, and/or related media. Preferred Qualifications: Teaching and research/creative experience in any one of the following: (1) global theatre actor training and performance traditions; (2) Shakespeare and heightened text; (3) ensemble-based, devised dramaturgies; (4) voice; and (5) movement. Experience and interest in directing productions at the university and/or professional level. Active union membership and professional affiliations. Record of publications and scholarship. Evidence of administrative leadership relevant to the position. Curriculum development experience. To apply, submit the following materials at apply.interfolio.com/191811 : Cover letter CV Teaching Statement Portfolio of research/creative activity (e.g., website, acting/directing reels, production images, publications, etc.) Contact information for 3 references Completed applications received by October 30, 2026, will be assured full consideration. The position will remain open until filled. Questions regarding the position may be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. Joe Alberti: dj@ou.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192534": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Drama - Costume Technology", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Weitzenhoffer Family College of Fine Arts: Peggy Dow Helmerich School of Drama Location: Norman, OK The University of Oklahoma Weitzenhoffer Family College of Fine Arts Helmerich School of Drama seeks a creative and committed tenure track Assistant Professor of Costume Technology. The Helmerich School of Drama at the University of Oklahoma prepares students for creative and meaningful professional careers in a broad range of employment in professional theatre, dance, opera, television, film, live entertainment, industrial, and theme park venues. All the advantages of a liberal arts education in a renowned national R1 university are combined with intensive conservatory-style training. \u00a0BFA emphases in the School of Drama include Performance, Dramaturgy, Design (scenic, lighting, costume, and sound), and Stage Management. A new MFA program in Directing is being launched this fall. The school is comprised of 26 full-time faculty and staff and 125 majors. We produce 9 mainstage productions each year in collaboration with the School of Drama, School of Musical Theatre, the School of Dance, and the Opera Program in the School of Music in addition to productions in our Studio Theatre. This faculty member will be expected to: Teach existing undergraduate courses including but not limited to Costume Construction, Costume Crafts, Draping and Pattern Drafting 1 and 2, and Advanced Costume Construction Techniques. Develop new courses related to Costume Technology. Teach courses in the newly developed MFA Costume Technology program upon approval. Conduct research/creative activities related to the field or allied fields. Contribute to advising and recruitment of both undergraduate and, eventually, graduate students. Collaborate with the costume studio supervisor to establish efficiency and quality standards for production builds. Contribute to the professional development of the costume technical staff in conjunction with the costume studio supervisor. Assist with production planning in conjunction with the Costume Design professor and costume studio supervisor. Assume responsibilities as Cutter/draper for one University Theatre production per semester. Participate in service activities in the department, college, university, and professional discipline. Required Qualifications: Earned M.F.A. in Costume Technology/Design with teaching experience at the university level. Demonstrated high level of draping and patternmaking ability. Demonstrated experience and ability to teach and mentor students. Experience with digital pattern drafting. Preferred Qualifications: Knowledge of costume crafts (e.g. millinery, dying techniques and wig styling). Previous experience with creation and/or teaching of graduate costume technology classes. Established experience with recruiting at the undergraduate level. Knowledge of industrial sewing equipment including single bed, sergers, and blind hem machines. Ability to apply and teach current and emerging technologies (e.g., digitizing technology) effectively To apply: Submit a cover letter including a CV, statement of teaching and creative \u00a0experience, contact information for three references, and three samples of research or creative activity. All materials must be uploaded to the following website: Interfolio Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applications received by October 30th, 2026 will receive full consideration. Please contact Jon Young, search committee chair, with questions regarding the position: jon.young-1@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/157288": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Music - Music Theory", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Weitzenhoffer Family College of Fine Arts: School of Music Location: Norman GENERAL INFO \u2022 The Weitzenhoffer Family College of Fine Arts School of Music at the University of Oklahoma (Norman, OK) seeks a dedicated teacher and researcher of music theory. \u2022 Rank: Assistant Professor of Music \u2013 Music Theory, Rank: Renewable. \u2022 Start date: August 16, 2025. JOB DESCRIPTION \u2022 Teach undergraduate courses and graduate seminars (3 2 load) to music majors. \u2022 Advise MM theses and serve on MM and doctoral (DMA and PhD) committees, as needed. \u2022 Collaborate with the theory area in all departmental activities, and serve on various committees as assigned by the Director of the OU School of Music. \u2022 Teach courses in the undergraduate core curriculum, Analysis of Tonal Music (a graduate course), and one graduate seminar per year on a topic pertaining to the candidate's expertise. REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS \u2022 Earned Ph.D. in Music Theory or a Ph.D. in Musicology with a theory concentration. DESIRED QUALIFICATIONS \u2022 Record of scholarly accomplishments: publications and presentations at conferences. \u2022 Evidence of teaching experience in music theory. \u2022 We welcome candidates whose research areas complement those of the existing faculty. Examples include, but are not limited to, aural skills pedagogy, global musics, histories of music theory, Indigenous musics, jazz, music and media, music cognition, and popular musics. \u2022 Our music theory area is committed to fostering excellence in teaching and scholarship in both new and existing areas in music theory. APPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS \u2022Applications should be submitted electronically using\u00a0at http://apply.interfolio.com . \u2022Please submit: 1) a letter of application; 2) a complete curriculum vita; 3) a list of references with complete contact information, including phone numbers. APPLICATION DEADLINE Screening begins December 1, 2024 and continues until the position is filled. Inquiries should be directed to the search committee chair: Dr. Jeff Swinkin, Music Theory Area Chair University of Oklahoma jswinkin@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187718": {"title": "Instructor/Lecturer of Music - Violin", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Weitzenhoffer Family College of Fine Arts: School of Music Location: Norman, Oklahoma The University of Oklahoma Weitzenhoffer Family College of Fine Arts School of Music seeks a dedicated Interim Instructor/Lecturer of Violin to join a program long known for the nurturing and development of generations of gifted musicians and teachers. This position is a one-year appointment. Responsibilities Primary teaching responsibilities include teaching undergraduate and graduate applied violin courses as assigned by the School of Music, having the ability to teach and inspire students in string quartet, chamber music, and other courses in the area of violin and string performance and pedagogy as needed by the program. The Instructor/Lecturer will participate in all String Area activities, including recruitment, auditions, juries, and outreach. Position: Interim Instructor/Lecturer of Violin Starting Date: August 16 th , 2026 Required Qualifications Earned Master's Degree in Violin Performance Evidence of effective teaching and performing Desired Qualifications Earned Doctorate in Violin Performance Collegiate teaching experience at undergraduate and graduate levels Creative/scholarly accomplishments demonstrated through national solo, chamber, and orchestral performances Application Materials Please submit 1) a letter of application, 2) a complete curriculum vita, 3) recent live YouTube video performance recordings, 4) video examples of teaching, and 5) contact information for at least three professional references. Applications must be submitted through Interfolio Direct Questions to Dr. Jonathan Ruck Violin Search Committee Chair jruck@ou.edu Screening of applicants will begin immediately, and applications will be accepted until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194776": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Music - Violin", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Weitzenhoffer Family College of Fine Arts: School of Music Location: Norman, Oklahoma The University of Oklahoma Weitzenhofer Family College of Fine Arts School of Music seeks an accomplished teacher of violin. Rank: Assistant/Associate Professor of Violin, tenure-track, full-time (9 month) Start Date: August 16, 2027 Required Qualifications - Earned Master of Music Degree in Violin Performance - Evidence of successful recruiting, mentoring, and teaching at the collegiate level - Creative/scholarly accomplishments demonstrated through national and international performances, master classes, presentations at conferences, recordings, publications, and/or professional leadership Desired Qualifications - Earned doctoral degree in Violin performance or equivalent professional experience - Experience guiding creative activity and research at the graduate level - We are especially interested in candidates who will contribute to the collaborative activities of our string area, with compelling records of experience in areas such as, but not limited to, chamber music, community outreach and engagement, and interdisciplinary projects. - Experience teaching violin pedagogy and/or specific pedagogical training Application Materials Please submit 1) a letter of application, 2) a complete curriculum vitae, 3) four digital links to performances, including at least three recordings from within the last two years (live performances preferred), 4) at least three current letters of reference, and 5) contact information for three additional references. Additional materials, including video-recorded teaching samples, may be requested at a later date. Inquiries should be addressed to: Dr. Jonathan Ruck at jruck@ou.edu Screening will begin November 13, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Applications should be submitted online via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/194776 ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195413": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Music - Assistant Director of Concert Bands", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Weitzenhoffer Family College of Fine Arts: School of Music Location: Norman, Oklahoma Description Rank: Assistant Professor, Full-time (9 month), 5-year Ranked Renewable Term The University of Oklahoma Weitzenhoffer Family College of Fine Arts School of Music seeks an Assistant Director of Concert Bands at the Assistant Professor rank to join a band program long known for its vibrant and comprehensive ensemble offerings and undergraduate and graduate conducting curriculum. Responsibilities \u00b7 Conduct the OU Symphony Band and teach undergraduate conducting courses and other undergraduate courses and ensembles according to the needs of the school and the candidate's abilities. \u00b7 Teach graduate conducting lessons, special topics, and collaborate with the graduate conducting seminar and conducting lab; guide graduate-level research and serve on committees as deemed appropriate by the SOM. \u00b7 Assist in coordinating recruitment and outreach activities. Actively connect with regional secondary school educators. \u00b7 Engage in an active research, creative activity, and conducting agenda. \u00b7 Participate in School, College, University, and professional service. Required Qualifications Doctorate in Music (Instrumental Conducting or Music Education); ABD considered if terminal degree is earned by August 1, 2027. Demonstrated success in artistic conducting, teaching, and performing at the collegiate and/or secondary level. Desired Qualifications \u00b7 Demonstrated experience in all facets of an active university band program, e.g. concert bands, athletic bands, applicable administrative experience \u00b7 Demonstrated successful public school instrumental teaching experience at the secondary level \u00b7 Evidence of success in teaching undergraduate and graduate conducting \u00b7 Ability and demonstrated experience teaching/conducting in another area of the curriculum such as new music ensemble, music education, world music, etc. \u00b7 Demonstrated ability to build and maintain relationships with immediate colleagues as well as secondary school music educators \u00b7 Demonstrated success in recruiting \u00b7 Record of creative/scholarly accomplishments, as demonstrated through regional, national, and/or international presentations at music conferences, performances, master classes, recordings, publications, and/or professional leadership \u00b7 Experience guiding creative activity at the graduate level Please submit the following materials at apply.interfolio.com/195413 : 1) A letter of application addressing how you meet the required and desired qualifications 2) A complete curriculum vita 3) A video portfolio including: a. a video recording or digital link of rehearsal footage with bands and/or chamber ensembles (minimum of 20 minutes in length), b. a video recording or digital link of performance footage with bands and/or chamber ensembles (minimum of 20 minutes in length), 4) 3 sample programs you have conducted 5) A list of 3-5 current references of people who can speak knowledgeably about your teaching and conducting Letters of application should be addressed to: Dr. Caroline Hand, Chair Assistant Director of Concert Bands Search Committee University of Oklahoma School of Music 500 West Boyd Norman, OK 73019 Additional Inquiries can be made be emailing Dr. Caroline Hand at:\u00a0 cehand@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/177644": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Musical Theatre - Dance (Ranked Renewable Term)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Weitzenhoffer Family College of Fine Arts: School of Musical Theatre Location: ACFATHEA This is a critical teaching position for the School of Musical Theatre (SOMT) and upgrades our current lecturer position to a ranked position with opportunities for promotion and comparable salary increases. The SOMT is renowned for its exceptional training and talented graduates working at the highest levels of the entertainment industry. This faculty member is needed to teach multiple levels of jazz dance requirements in the musical theatre curriculum and may teach additional dance styles and techniques including: tap, theatre dance, hip hop, partnering, contemporary, and choreography. This role may also teach a popular general education elective, \u201cUnderstanding Musical Theatre\u201d for the broader university student population. This position contributes to the creative/research activity of the SOMT and the Weitzenhoffer Family College of Fine Arts at OU. They will regularly choreograph (and possibly direct) musical theatre productions for the OU Musical Theatre Downtown Series or OU University Theatre Season. These projects are large collaborations between the SOMT, Helmerich School of Drama, and the OU University Theatre production staff. Full-time faculty members are evaluated on teaching, continued creative activity and research, and service to SOMT, College of Fine Arts, and the University. An important service expectation for all musical theatre faculty is to participate in the recruitment process for the SOMT. For this role, that may involve travel to lead dance calls at national unified auditions, as well as reviewing audition materials from prospective students. Required qualifications: Bachelors Degree OR equivalent professional experience Minimum 5 years professional musical theatre performance experience 3-5 years teaching dance at the undergraduate or professional level Professional experience choreographing musicals Connections to the industry Authorization to work in the United States Additional desired qualifications: Membership in one or all of the following: AEA, SDC, or AGVA College or Professional level experience teaching tap, ballroom, partnering and additional dance styles Professional experience directing musicals Mentoring pre-professional performance students MFA in Theatre, Performance, Dance or other related field. Please submit to interfolio : A one-page cover letter of interest which may address the candidate\u2019s approach to teaching and choreographing. Theatrical Resume Academic Vita Unofficial Transcripts Contact information for 3 industry/academic references A digital portfolio of your performances/choreography. The application review will begin January 12, 2026. Review will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Person of Contact: Ashton Byrum, Director of the School of Musical Theatre and search committee chair. Please visit musicaltheatre.ou.edu and our social media platforms for more information about the SOMT."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192969": {"title": "Open Rank Faculty Position of Architecture - Architectural History and Theory", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Christopher C. Gibbs College of Architecture Location: University of Oklahoma, Norman Campus The University of Oklahoma\u2019s Christopher C. Gibbs College of Architecture (GCA) is expanding to match the scale of its ambitions. Based in Norman, a lively college town within the Oklahoma City metropolitan area, which was recently ranked the \u201cNo. 1 Best Big City to Live In\u201d by U.S. News & World Report, the Division of Architecture (DivA) seeks new faculty members to help shape the future of architectural education and practice. These positions reinforce OU\u2019s university-wide commitment as an R-1 public research institution, fueled by growth in faculty excellence, research innovation, and cross-sector partnerships. We invite applications for a 9-month, full-time, open-rank (Assistant, Associate, and/or Full Professor) tenure-track or tenured position beginning in August 2027. Review of applications will begin on October 12, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Who We\u2019re Looking For We seek an educator and historian whose teaching and research drives architectural ideas forward. Applicants should demonstrate a clear research trajectory that contributes to contemporary architectural discourse within and through a historical context. Candidates will join a faculty known for its experimental spirit and its legacy of the American School of Architecture . They will play an active role in seminars, lecture courses, and curriculum discussions across our Ph.D. and NAAB-accredited B.Arch and M.Arch programs. Possible areas of investigation may include but are not limited to history of 20 th Century architecture; architectural and urban design history since 1880, or similar. The successful applicant shall have access to wide-ranging resources including the incredible body of work produced by the architects of the American School now housed in the OU Libraries American School Archive . Our Community Faculty in the Division of Architecture foster a culture that connects design education with discovery and impact. They create new knowledge in the areas of placemaking and belonging, health and well-being, entrepreneurship and applied practice, and resilience and sustainability (https://ou.edu/gibbs/explore/research). The Institute for Quality Communities, host of the biennial national Placemaking Conference, connects faculty and students with communities across Oklahoma to shape civic life through design. Annual travel study in Rome, complemented with domestic travel programs, broaden students\u2019 perspectives and deepen their design education through experiential learning. An award-winning Design+Build program and strong results in national student competitions, often in partnership with OU Construction Science, underscore our commitment to hands-on learning. The nationally recognized student journal Telesis reflects a tradition of intellectual creativity and rigor that extends beyond the classroom. How Faculty Contribute Faculty in the Division of Architecture play an active role in shaping a culture of creativity and impact. This includes: Infusing energy and innovation through studio, seminar and lecture course teaching Advancing scholarly and creative work through recognized venues Contributing to the life of the division, college, and university through shared governance Engaging with the profession and the public through meaningful service and outreach What We Offer The Division of Architecture and Gibbs College provide robust faculty support through: Mentorship programs, Research and travel funds, and Teaching and research assistants, supported by the Gibbs Endowment and other resources. About the Division of Architecture and the Christopher C. Gibbs College of Architecture The Division of Architecture (DivA), which first began accepting students in 1926, and has a legacy of experimentation that began with Bruce Goff\u2019s leadership in the mid-20th century, continues today in a culture of creativity and purposeful impact. The Christopher C. Gibbs College of Architecture brings together through in-person and online programs more than 1,300 students across seven disciplines: Architecture, Construction Science, Environmental Design, Interior Design, Landscape Architecture, Regional and City Planning, and Urban Design. The College serves as a hub for interdisciplinary collaboration and community engagement. Learn more at architecture.ou.edu/architecture. About the University of Oklahoma Founded in 1890, the University of Oklahoma (OU) is a Carnegie R1 Institution with over $500M in annual research expenditures and more than 34,000 students across online and three in-person campuses in Norman, Oklahoma City, and Tulsa. OU is advancing an ambitious vision to shape the future through discovery, creativity, and innovation as outlined in the Lead On, University Strategic Plan ( https://ou.edu/leadon/strategic-plan ). Norman offers the atmosphere of a vibrant college town within the Oklahoma City metropolitan area. The region combines affordability with cultural vitality, strong K\u201312 schools, and nationally recognized works of architecture and landscape architecture. Home to the 2025 NBA champions and a host site for events during the 2028 Olympics, Oklahoma City reflects a growing international profile. Oklahoma\u2019s 39 Native Nations shape a context where faculty and students engage in collaborative work that links design to place, culture, and community. Visit https://ou.edu/facultyrecruitment for more information about what OU and the local communities have to offer. Salary Range Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Search Committee Chairperson Inquiries shall be directed to Dr. Wanda Liebermann RA, at wkliebermann@ou.edu A PhD (or equivalent) in architectural history and theory, or similar field Evidence of ability to successfully teach architectural history and theory seminars or lecture courses Evidence or emerging research record of peer-reviewed scholarly productivity Demonstrated ability to contribute to a collegial and collaborative working environment, in addition to possessing effective communication and interpersonal skills Applicants shall submit the following materials as PDF documents via Interfolio : Cover letter Statement of interest outlining in two to three pages the applicant\u2019s experience and research agenda Current Curriculum Vitae Full contact information for minimum three academic/professional references Digital portfolio that provides evidence of 1) scholarly activity and 2) efficacy in teaching with examples of student work if applicable. Digital submission size should not exceed 100MB."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193365": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Architecture - AI/Machine Learning", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Christopher C. Gibbs College of Architecture Location: Christopher C. Gibbs College of Architecture, University of Oklahoma (Norman Campus) The University of Oklahoma\u2019s Christopher C. Gibbs College of Architecture (GCA) is expanding to match the scale of its ambitions. Based in Norman, a lively college town within the Oklahoma City metropolitan area which was recently ranked the \u201cNo. 1 Best Big City to Live In\u201d by U.S. News & World Report, the Division of Architecture (DivA) seeks new faculty members to help shape the future of architectural education and practice. This position reinforces OU\u2019s university-wide commitment as an R-1 public research institution, fueled by growth in faculty excellence, research innovation, and cross-sector partnerships. We invite applications for a 9-month, full-time, tenure-track position at the rank of assistant professor beginning in August 2027. Review of applications will begin on October 12, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Who We\u2019re Looking For We seek educators and practitioners whose teaching and research are shaped by AI/Machine Learning. Applicants should demonstrate a clear research trajectory that contributes to contemporary architectural discourse within and through a context of design and public impact and/or a record of exploring how AI reshapes architectural design processes. Candidates will join a faculty recognized for its experimental spirit and for carrying forward the legacy of the American School (https://architecture.ou.edu/the-american-school/), a distinctive approach to architectural pedagogy and design whose remarkable body of work is preserved in the OU Libraries\u2019 American School Archive (https://libraries.ou.edu/find-expert-services/special-research-collections/our-collecting-areas/american-school-architecture-archive). Successful candidates will contribute actively to design studios, seminars, lecture courses, and broader curricular discussions, engaging students and faculty across our Ph.D. and NAAB-accredited B.Arch and M.Arch programs. Possible areas of investigation may include but are not limited to: AI-driven design workflows; machine learning and generative AI models; AI-based performance evaluation and simulation; agentic and multi-agent AI systems; human-AI collaborative design processes; BIM/AI integration, or similar. Our Community Faculty in the Division of Architecture foster a culture that connects design education with discovery and impact. They create new knowledge in the areas of placemaking and belonging, health and well-being, entrepreneurship and applied practice, and resilience and sustainability (https://ou.edu/gibbs/explore/research). The Institute for Quality Communities, host of the biennial national Placemaking Conference, connects faculty and students with communities across Oklahoma to shape civic life through design. A semester-long study abroad program in Rome, complemented by a range of short-term international and domestic travel opportunities, broaden students\u2019 perspectives and deepen their design education through immersive, experiential learning. Our ACSA award-winning Design-Build program, together with a strong record of achievements in national student competitions, often in partnership with other GCA divisions, further underscore our commitment to hands-on and interdisciplinary learning. The nationally recognized student journal Telesis reflects a longstanding tradition of intellectual curiosity, critical inquiry, creativity, and rigor that extends beyond the classroom. How Faculty Contribut e Faculty in the Division of Architecture play an active role in shaping a culture of creativity and impact. This includes: Infusing enthusiasm and innovation through studio, seminar and lecture course teaching, Advancing scholarly and creative work through recognized venues, Contributing to the life of the division, college, and university through shared governance, and Engaging with the profession and the public through meaningful service and outreach. What We Offer The Division of Architecture and Gibbs College provide robust faculty support through: Mentorship programs, Research and travel funds, and Teaching and research assistants, supported by the Gibbs Endowment and other resources. About the Division of Architecture and the Christopher C. Gibbs College of Architecture The Division of Architecture (DivA) first began accepting students in 1926 and enjoys a legacy of experimentation that began with Bruce Goff\u2019s leadership in the mid-20th century and continues today in a culture of creativity and purposeful impact. The Christopher C. Gibbs College of Architecture brings together through in-person and online programs more than 1,400 students across seven disciplines: Architecture, Construction Science, Environmental Design, Interior Design, Landscape Architecture, Regional and City Planning, and Urban Design. The College serves as a hub for interdisciplinary collaboration and community engagement. Learn more at architecture.ou.edu/architecture. About the University of Oklahoma Founded in 1890, the University of Oklahoma (OU) is a Carnegie R1 Institution with over $500M in annual research expenditures and more than 34,000 students across online and three in-person campuses in Norman, Oklahoma City, and Tulsa. OU is advancing an ambitious vision to shape the future through discovery, creativity, and innovation as outlined in the Lead On, University Strategic Plan ( https://ou.edu/leadon/strategic-plan ). Norman offers the atmosphere of a vibrant college town within the Oklahoma City metropolitan area. The region combines affordability with cultural vitality, strong K\u201312 schools, and nationally recognized works of architecture and landscape architecture. Home to the 2025 NBA champions and a host site for events during the 2028 Olympics, Oklahoma City reflects a growing international profile. Oklahoma\u2019s 39 Native Nations shape a context where faculty and students engage in collaborative work that links design to place, culture, and community. Visit https://ou.edu/facultyrecruitment for more information about what OU and the local communities have to offer. Salary Range Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Search Committee Chairperson Inquiries shall be directed to Awilda Rodr\u00edguez, RA, at awilda.rodriguez@ou.edu Required Qualifications: Evidence of machine learning or deep learning applied to design workflows (coursework, research, or professional practice) Knowledge of multimodal/multi-model AI orchestrations or agentic AI systems Evidence of ability to successfully teach architecture studios, seminars, and/or lecture courses Evidence or emerging research record of peer-reviewed creative and/or scholarly productivity Demonstrated ability to contribute to a collegial and collaborative working environment, in addition to possessing effective communication and interpersonal skills Applicants shall submit the following materials as PDF documents via Interfolio : Cover letter Statement of interest outlining in two to three pages the applicant\u2019s experience, teaching approach, and research agenda Current Curriculum Vitae Full contact information for a minimum of three academic and/or professional references Digital portfolio that provides evidence of 1) scholarly activity and 2) efficacy in teaching with examples of student work if applicable. Digital submission size should not exceed 100MB."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193368": {"title": "Open Rank Professor of Architecture - Architectural Design Pedagogy", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Christopher C. Gibbs College of Architecture Location: Christopher C. Gibbs College of Architecture, University of Oklahoma The University of Oklahoma\u2019s Christopher C. Gibbs College of Architecture (GCA) is expanding to match the scale of its ambitions. Based in Norman, a lively college town within the Oklahoma City metropolitan area, which was recently ranked the \u201cNo. 1 Best Big City to Live In\u201d by U.S. News & World Report, the Division of Architecture (DivA) seeks new faculty members to help shape the future of architectural education and practice. These positions reinforce OU\u2019s university-wide commitment as an R-1 public research institution, fueled by growth in faculty excellence, research innovation, and cross-sector partnerships. We invite applications for two 9-month, full-time, open-rank (Assistant, Associate, and/or Full Professor) tenure-track or tenured positions beginning in August 2027. Review of applications will begin on October 12, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Who We\u2019re Looking For We seek design faculty for beginning and/or advanced studios whose teaching and research drives architectural ideas forward. Applicants should demonstrate a clear research trajectory that contributes to contemporary architectural discourse within and through a context of design and public impact. Possible areas of investigation to complement design studio pedagogy may include but are not limited to architectural and urban design theory, landscape architecture, graphic communications, fabrication and making, materials and processes, and/or computation. Candidates will join a faculty recognized for its experimental spirit and for carrying forward the legacy of the American School (https://architecture.ou.edu/the-american-school/), a distinctive approach to architectural pedagogy and design whose remarkable body of work is preserved in the OU Libraries\u2019 American School Archive ( https://libraries.ou.edu/find-expert-services/special-research-collections/our-collecting-areas/american-school-architecture-archive ). Successful candidates will contribute actively to design studios, seminars, lecture courses, and broader curricular discussions, engaging students and faculty across our Ph.D. and NAAB-accredited B.Arch and M.Arch programs. Our Community Faculty in the Division of Architecture actively contribute to a dynamic culture of creativity, inquiry, collaboration, and impact. They generate new knowledge in the areas of placemaking and belonging, health and well-being, entrepreneurship and applied practice, and resilience and sustainability (https://ou.edu/gibbs/explore/research). The Institute for Quality Communities, host of the biennial national Placemaking Conference, connects faculty and students with communities across Oklahoma to shape civic life through design. A semester-long study abroad program in Rome, complemented by a range of short-term international and domestic travel opportunities, broaden students\u2019 perspectives and deepen their design education through immersive, experiential learning. Our ACSA award-winning Design-Build program, together with a strong record of achievements in national student competitions, often in partnership with other GCA divisions, further underscore our commitment to hands-on and interdisciplinary learning. The nationally recognized student journal Telesis reflects a longstanding tradition of intellectual curiosity, critical inquiry, creativity, and rigor that extends beyond the classroom. Faculty have opportunities to: Inspire and innovate through teaching in design studios, seminar and lecture course teaching, Advance scholarly and creative inquiry through research, design, and work disseminated in recognized academic and professional venues, Contribute to the life of the Division, College, and University through shared governance, and Engage with the profession and the public through meaningful service, engagement, and outreach. What We Offer The Division of Architecture and Gibbs College provide robust faculty support through: Mentorship programs Research and travel funds Teaching and research assistants, supported by the Gibbs Endowment and other resources. About the Division of Architecture and the Christopher C. Gibbs College of Architecture The Division of Architecture (DivA) first began accepting students in 1926, and has a legacy of experimentation that began with Bruce Goff\u2019s leadership in the mid-20th century, continues today in a culture of creativity and purposeful impact. The Christopher C. Gibbs College of Architecture brings together through in-person and online programs more than 1,400 students across seven disciplines: Architecture, Construction Science, Environmental Design, Interior Design, Landscape Architecture, Regional and City Planning, and Urban Design. A new B.S. in Real Estate and Community Development is anticipated in fall 2027. The College serves as a hub for interdisciplinary teaching and research, and community engagement. Learn more at architecture.ou.edu/architecture. About the University of Oklahoma Founded in 1890, the University of Oklahoma (OU) is a Carnegie R1 Institution with over $500M in annual research expenditures and more than 34,000 students across online and three in-person campuses in Norman, Oklahoma City, and Tulsa. OU is advancing an ambitious vision to shape the future through discovery, creativity, and innovation as outlined in the Lead On, University Strategic Plan ( https://ou.edu/leadon/strategic-plan ). Norman offers the atmosphere of a vibrant college town within the Oklahoma City metropolitan area. The region combines affordability with cultural vitality, strong K\u201312 schools, and nationally recognized works of architecture and landscape architecture. Home to the 2025 NBA champions and a host site for events during the 2028 Olympics, Oklahoma City reflects a growing international profile. Oklahoma\u2019s 39 Native Nations shape a context where faculty and students engage in collaborative work that links design to place, culture, and community. Visit https://ou.edu/facultyrecruitment for more information about what OU and the local communities have to offer. Salary Range Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Search Committee Chairperson Inquiries shall be directed to Paolo Sanza, RA, at paolo.sanza@ou.edu. Required Qualifications A minimum of a master\u2019s degree (or equivalent) in architecture or related fields Evidence of ability to successfully teach architecture studios, seminars, and/or lecture courses Evidence or emerging research record of peer-reviewed creative and/or scholarly productivity Demonstrated ability to contribute to a collegial and collaborative working environment, in addition to possessing effective communication and interpersonal skills Applicants shall submit the following materials as PDF documents via Interfolio . Cover letter Statement of interest outlining in two to three pages the applicant\u2019s experience, teaching approach, and research agenda Current Curriculum Vitae Full contact information for minimum three academic and/or professional references D igital portfolio that provides evidence of 1) scholarly activity and 2) efficacy in teaching with examples of student work if applicable. Digital submission size should not exceed 100MB."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193378": {"title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Construction Science (RRT)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Christopher C. Gibbs College of Architecture Location: Norman, OK, United States The Haskell & Irene Lemon Construction Science (CNS) Division in the University of Oklahoma\u2019s (OU) Gibbs College of Architecture (GCA) invites applications for a ranked renewable term (RRT) faculty position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor beginning in August 2027. The university considers RRT faculty \u201cregular\u201d faculty alongside tenure-track/tenured faculty, though RRT does not include eligibility for tenure. RRT contracts are typically renewed on a regular 5-year basis following successful annual reviews, and come with significant voting privileges. Applicants should have a demonstrated background in and be prepared to teach in multiple subject areas in a construction management curriculum. The Division and its endowment support strong leadership, a cadre of passionate and impactful faculty, and a pattern of student success in regional, national, and international competitions. The CNS degree was launched in 1983, and the undergraduate program has been accredited by the American Council for Construction Education (ACCE) since 1990. The College\u2019s history of advancing the design-build movement continues today through its approach to hands-on and interdisciplinary collaborations. The Division also houses five other programs: \u00b7 Master of Science in Construction Management (in-person) \u00b7 Master of Construction Business (MCB) (online) \u00b7 Minor in Construction Science (in-Person) \u00b7 Certificate in Entrepreneurship and Real Estate Development (in-person) \u00b7 Bachelor of Science in Interdisciplinary Studies - Construction Management emphasis (online) Go to http://cns.ou.edu for more information about these programs in the Division. The position benefits from the following notable contexts: A tight-knit group of nationally recognized faculty committed to creative and innovative approaches to construction education Highly collaborative interdisciplinary College setting that fosters a student-centric learning environment that impacts local, state, national, and international communities Cultures of innovative teaching, research, and service in support of the GCA strategic plan Funding and professional development support from the Haskell & Irene Lemon Endowment and GCA A program with strong enrollment growth and close ties to the Kansas City-Oklahoma City-Dallas/Fort Worth corridor Our Culture of Excellence The Gibbs College of Architecture at the University of Oklahoma has an enrollment of more than 1,300 students through undergraduate, master\u2019s, and doctoral programs across in-person and online academic programs that include Architecture, Construction Science, Environmental Design, Interior Design, and Regional and City Planning. A new undergraduate degree in Real Estate and Community Development is planned for fall 2027. For more information about college programs, please visit gibbs.ou.edu . About the University of Oklahoma Founded in 1890, the University of Oklahoma (OU) is a Carnegie R1 Institution with over $500M in annual research expenditures and more than 34,000 students across online and three in-person campuses in Norman, Oklahoma City, and Tulsa. OU is advancing an ambitious vision to shape the future through discovery, creativity, and innovation as outlined in the Lead On, University Strategic Plan . Norman is a perennial contender in \u201cBest Places to Live\u201d rankings and offers the atmosphere of a vibrant college town within the Oklahoma City metropolitan area. The region combines affordability with strong K\u201312 schools and nationally recognized works of architecture and construction. Home to the 2025 NBA champions and a host site for events during the 2028 Olympics, Oklahoma City reflects a growing international profile. Oklahoma\u2019s 39 Native Nations contribute to a context where faculty and students engage in collaborative work that links a culture of making to place and community. Visit https://ou.edu/facultyrecruitment for more information about what OU and the local communities have to offer. A Master\u2019s degree in Construction or a related field. At the time employment begins, a minimum of 5 years\u2019 professional work experience in the US construction industry. A demonstrated background to be able to teach in multiple subject areas, such as Construction Estimating, Field Supervision, Heavy Civil Construction, Materials and Methods, Surveying, Planning & Scheduling, Pre-Construction, Project Management, Safety, Structural Systems, and/or Technology All application materials may be submitted online through the Interfolio website at: apply.interfolio.com/193378. Interested candidates are requested to submit the following materials: Cover letter highlighting relevant teaching and professional experiences, attitudes about professional communication and interdisciplinary collaboration, abilities regarding classroom and job-site technology, and insights regarding the future of the construction industry Current curriculum vitae, including significant accomplishments and professional credentials. Contact information for three (3) academic and/or professional references Priority review of applications will take place on 10/09/2026.\u00a0 The position will remain open until filled. The anticipated start is August 2027.\u00a0 Please submit questions by email to Dr. Doyle Phillips ( dtphillips@ou.edu )."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195462": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Meteorology - Social and/or Behavioral Sciences Applied to Weather and Climate Studies", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Atmospheric and Geographic Sciences: School of Meteorology Location: University of Oklahoma- Norman Campus The School of Meteorology ( https://ou.edu/ags/meteorology ) at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position focused on social and/or behavioral sciences applied to weather and climate studies . The School is housed within the College of Atmospheric and Geographic Sciences. The Purpose of the College of Atmospheric and Geographic Sciences is to inspire and prepare individuals for rewarding careers that advance the understanding and responsible stewardship of the land, water, and sky, promoting a more resilient and flourishing future of our dynamic world. We deliver an exceptional interdisciplinary education that fosters discovery and drives innovation through experiential learning, technological advancement, convergence research, and workforce development. Our collaborative environment empowers students, faculty, and staff to tackle significant environmental and humanitarian challenges, facilitating resilience and economic growth. We seek to continue to broaden the expertise in the School of Meteorology in the application of social, behavioral, and economic science (SBES) to weather and climate, a rapidly growing focus within the atmospheric sciences. Specifically, we seek an empirically oriented and methodologically pluralistic scholar who can connect the School's strengths in physical weather and climate research with SBES to better understand how individuals and organizations understand, use, and respond to weather and climate information, hazards, and impacts. Relevant expertise may include risk communication, decision-making, public perceptions and behavior, hazards and disasters, vulnerability and resilience, or the use of weather and climate information in operational and policy settings. We welcome a wide range of empirical approaches, including surveys, interviews, focus groups, experiments, computational methods, and other quantitative, qualitative, or mixed-methods approaches appropriate for studying these questions. Experience in interdisciplinary research and collaboration with atmospheric scientists and external partners is especially valued, as is experience working with emergency managers or other operational partners. The School of Meteorology is currently composed of 35 faculty and has a long and distinguished history of excellence in various sub-fields of the atmospheric sciences. Our vision is to develop the School into a globally-engaged, student-centered atmospheric science program to advance the Nation\u2019s Weather, Water, and Climate Enterprise, by leveraging our strengths in research, education, and community engagement. The University of Oklahoma is a Carnegie-R1, comprehensive, public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement, serving the educational, cultural, economic and health-care needs of the state, region, and nation from three campuses: Norman, Health Sciences Center in Oklahoma City, and the Schusterman Center in Tulsa. The University of Oklahoma enrolls over 30,000 students and has more than 2700 full-time faculty members in 21 colleges. Norman is a vibrant university town of around 130,000 inhabitants with a growing entertainment and art scene. With outstanding schools, amenities, and a low cost of living, Norman is a perennial contender on \u201cbest place to live\u201d rankings. Visit https://www.ou.edu/facultyrecruitment and https://www.ou.edu/dam/provost/documents/facultyflipbook.pdf for more information. Within an easy commute, Oklahoma City features a dynamic economy and outstanding cultural venues adding to the region\u2019s growing appeal. Applicants must possess a PhD in Meteorology, Atmospheric Science, Social or Behavioral Sciences, or a related discipline at the time of appointment. The candidate will be expected to establish a vigorous, independent research program; to develop and teach courses; and to contribute to mentorship and advisement of students in the undergraduate and graduate degree programs of the School of Meteorology. To apply , submit as one single pdf document a comprehensive cover letter (not to exceed 10 pages), current curriculum vitae, and a list with contact information of at least 3 people who can serve as references. Apply at: https://apply.interfolio.com/195462. The cover letter must address the candidate\u2019s approach to: \u00b7 Teaching (past instructional experiences and plans/goals for teaching at the University of Oklahoma, including teaching existing and proposed courses), \u00b7 Research (including prior contributions to research and goals for developing a strong research program at the University of Oklahoma), \u00b7 Service (their vision for internal service to the academic unit, the College and the University, and for external service to our scientific community and other stakeholders) \u00b7 Student mentorship (including past mentorship experiences and/or mentoring approach) For any questions regarding the application process, please contact David Bodine at bodine@ou.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195734": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Meteorology (Renewable Term)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Atmospheric and Geographic Sciences: School of Meteorology Location: University of Oklahoma- Norman Campus The School of Meteorology ( https://ou.edu/ags/meteorology ) at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for a renewable term Assistant Professor position focused on teaching . The School is housed within the College of Atmospheric and Geographic Sciences. The Purpose of the College of Atmospheric and Geographic Sciences is to inspire and prepare individuals for rewarding careers that advance the understanding and responsible stewardship of the land, water, and sky, promoting a more resilient and flourishing future of our dynamic world. We deliver an exceptional interdisciplinary education that fosters discovery and drives innovation through experiential learning, technological advancement, convergence research, and workforce development. Our collaborative environment empowers students, faculty, and staff to tackle significant environmental and humanitarian challenges, facilitating resilience and economic growth. The School of Meteorology seeks to recruit an experienced instructional leader in atmospheric sciences to support the operation, development, and advancement of our undergraduate programs. Specifically, the faculty member will be expected to mentor renewable term lecturers and instructors, lead the training of graduate teaching assistants, support the development of online courses, and teach up to 3 courses per semester. The faculty member will also be expected to participate significantly in service to the unit, College, University, and broader profession. Technical proficiency in Python-based computer programming, licensure for amateur radio, experience training teachers, and experience with community-engaged activities are also valued. The School of Meteorology is currently composed of 35 faculty and has a long and distinguished history of excellence in various sub-fields of the atmospheric sciences. Our vision is to develop the School into a globally-engaged, student-centered atmospheric science program to advance the Nation\u2019s Weather, Water, and Climate Enterprise, by leveraging our strengths in research, education, and community engagement. The University of Oklahoma is a Carnegie-R1, comprehensive, public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement, serving the educational, cultural, economic and health-care needs of the state, region, and nation from three campuses: Norman, Harold Hamm Health Campus in Oklahoma City, and the Schusterman Center in Tulsa. The University of Oklahoma enrolls over 30,000 students and has more than 2700 full-time faculty members in 21 colleges. Norman is a vibrant university town of around 130,000 inhabitants with a growing entertainment and art scene. With outstanding schools, amenities, and a low cost of living, Norman is a perennial contender on \u201cbest place to live\u201d rankings. Visit https://www.ou.edu/facultyrecruitment and https://www.ou.edu/dam/provost/documents/facultyflipbook.pdf for more information. Within an easy commute, Oklahoma City features a dynamic economy and outstanding cultural venues adding to the region\u2019s growing appeal. Applicants must possess a PhD in Meteorology, Atmospheric Science, or a related discipline at the time of appointment. At least 5 years of instructional experience in higher education is also required. To apply, submit as one single pdf document a comprehensive cover letter (not to exceed 10 pages), current curriculum vitae, and a list of at least 3 people who can serve as references to the link here: https://apply.interfolio.com/195734 The cover letter must address the candidate\u2019s approach to: \u00b7 Teaching (past instructional experiences and plans/goals for teaching at the University of Oklahoma, including teaching existing and proposed courses), \u00b7 Service (their vision for internal service to the academic unit, the College and the University, and for external service to our scientific community and other stakeholders) \u00b7 Mentorship (including past mentorship experiences and/or mentoring approach) Screening of applications will begin on 1 November 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. For any questions regarding the application process, please contact Dr. Cameron Homeyer ( chomeyer@ou.edu )."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195231": {"title": "Associate Professor of Educational Leadership and Policy Studies - Director for the Online Ed.D. Program", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Jeannine Rainbolt College of Education: Department of Educational Leadership and Policy Studies Location: Norman, Oklahoma The Department of Educational Leadership and Policy Studies (ELPS) in the Jeannine Rainbolt College of Education (JRCOE) at the University of Oklahoma (OU) invites applications for a rank-renewable position at the Associate Professor level in the Educational Administration, Curriculum and Supervision (EACS) program area, with primary responsibilities in the Ed.D. Online program (higher education leadership concentration), located in Norman. This is a 12-month full-time position with an expected teaching load of 3\u20133 (for fall, spring) and required 2 in summer and administrative responsibilities (see below). This position is based in Norman, Oklahoma. OU is a comprehensive flagship research institution with a student body of approximately 31,000, committed to excellence in teaching, research, and professional service. The ELPS department ( https://ou.edu/education/academics/educational-leadership-and-policy-studies ) includes faculty dedicated to high-quality teaching, scholarly engagement, and service. The department offers M.Ed., Ed.D., and Ph.D. degrees in Education Studies (EDS), EACS, and Adult & Higher Education (EDAH), with most courses offered at the graduate level. The EACS program includes face-to-face graduate programs on the Norman and Tulsa campuses as well as online doctoral programs serving working professionals. The Ed.D. Online program within EACS and EDAH prepares scholar-practitioners for leadership roles across both PK\u201312 and higher education contexts, with this position specifically contributing to the higher education leadership concentration and the preparation of leaders in colleges, universities, and related organizations. Additional information about ELPS and EACS is available at: https://www.ou.edu/education/academics/educational-leadership-and-policy-studies Responsibilities Rank renewable term faculty are expected to engage in teaching and service and administrative activities consistent with the OU Strategic Plan (Lead On, University! https://www.ou.edu/leadon ). This is a non-tenure track position with no expectation of developing an independent research agenda or being eligible for tenure; however, engagement in professional practice, program evaluation, and applied scholarship is valued. This is a rank renewable term position and cannot be transferred into a tenure line position. The successful candidate will teach doctoral-level courses and chair student dissertations within the Ed.D. Online program (including both asynchronous and synchronous online/hybrid formats). Teaching responsibilities will emphasize educational leadership or higher education leadership, including governance, organizational leadership, student success, policy, and innovation in education contexts. Year-round advising, program administration, and teaching are required. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 We seek individuals who can teach in one or more of the following areas within education leadership (P-20, K-20 or higher education) in the Ed.D. context: o\u00a0\u00a0 Education administration, governance, and policy o\u00a0\u00a0 Leadership for student success o\u00a0\u00a0 Technology, innovation, and digital transformation o\u00a0\u00a0 Organizational leadership, change, and strategic decision-making \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 A critical component of the role is serving as Director of the Ed.D. Online program, which enrolls approximately 180\u2013210 doctoral students across multiple active cohorts. In this capacity, the Director will: o\u00a0\u00a0 Provide overall leadership and coordination for the Ed.D. Online Program within ELPS. o Oversee program operations, including course scheduling, interviewing and hiring adjunct faculty, instructional delivery, and program policies. o Ensure program alignment with departmental, college, and university priorities. o Collaborate with department leadership and faculty on program planning, staffing, and resource allocation. o Monitor and communicate budgetary matters, including providing regular updates to the Department Chair on expenditures, funding needs, and long-term financial planning. o Availability to teach during summer terms, including chairing dissertations Required Qualifications \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 An earned doctorate in Educational Leadership, Higher Education, or a closely related field \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Five (5) to ten (10) years of experience in educational leadership, administration, or program director roles. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Demonstrated excellence in graduate-level teaching, particularly in online or hybrid doctoral programs \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Previous experience leading online programs and more specifically Online EdD programs \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Evidence of previous program direction, growth and improvement \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Experience in online graduate student programming and community building \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Contributes to a culture of mutual respect, shared responsibility, and continuous improvement Preferred Qualifications \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Substantial knowledge of and connections to CPED \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Experience teaching graduate students across multiple modalities (online and hybrid), with demonstrated effectiveness in online instruction \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Experience supporting doctoral students, including mentoring, advising, and dissertation supervision \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Experience with student recruitment, retention, and persistence initiatives in graduate programs \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Ability to develop and assess graduate programs, and experience leading accreditation processes \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Demonstrated expertise aligned with one or more of the focus areas listed above Applicants should submit to Interfolio , a letter of application that clearly addresses the required and preferred qualifications, with particular attention to experience in P-20 leadership and doctoral-level teaching. Applicants should explicitly identify their areas of teaching interest and expertise in alignment with the Ed.D. Online program. Applicants should also provide a current curriculum vitae and the names, professional positions, and contact information of at least three individuals who can serve as professional references. Candidates are encouraged to submit additional materials, such as a teaching statement, evidence of online teaching effectiveness, or examples of doctoral program leadership, that demonstrate alignment with the responsibilities and expectations of the position. Please submit all materials by Wednesday, November 11 th for priority consideration of the position, applications accepted until filled. Appointments will begin in either January 2027 or Summer 2027 depending on appointee availability. Applicants should submit application materials to Interfolio: apply.interfolio.com/195231 Contact Information: Dr. Sarah Guthery, Chair, EdD Online Search Committee Department of Educational Leadership and Policy Studies sarahguthery@ou.edu 405-325-4202"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192538": {"title": "Associate Professor of Educational Psychology - Professional Counseling", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Jeannine Rainbolt College of Education: Department of Educational Psychology Location: Norman, Oklahoma The Position The Professional Counseling M.Ed. Program, in the Department of Educational Psychology, Jeannine Rainbolt College of Education, at the University of Oklahoma, seeks an Associate Professor with a demonstrated record of strength in teaching, supervision, and research, along with a primary professional identity as a counselor educator. The successful candidate will serve a nine-month, tenure-track appointment beginning Fall 2027. Salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and background. Summer employment is contingent on student demand and funding. The Program The Professional Counseling Program at OU offers professional preparation at the master\u2019s degree level for qualified persons wishing to become clinical professional counselors, school counselors, and/or drug and alcohol counselors. The program offers (1) a 60-hour CACREP-accredited Clinical Professional Counseling track that meets the state of Oklahoma LPC requirements, (2) a 15-hour Drug and Alcohol counseling track, and (3) a\u00a015-hour\u00a0School Counseling Certificate.\u00a0 The Drug and Alcohol Counseling concentration includes five specialized courses that have been approved by the Oklahoma Board of Mental Health and Substance Abuse Services. Our primary training goals are to facilitate student integration of counseling theory, research, and practice; and to foster the development and application of holistic counseling skills. We seek to integrate varied multicultural perspectives into our students\u2019 professional training and development.\u00a0A foundational value of the program is to foster understanding, critical thinking, and ethical decision-making. Information About the University of Oklahoma and the City of Norman OU is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement. OU serves the educational, cultural, economic, and health-care needs of the state, region, and nation from three campuses: Norman, Health Campus in Oklahoma City, and the Schusterman Center in Tulsa. OU enrolls over 30,000 students and has more than 2,700 full-time faculty members across 21 colleges. In 2014, OU became the first public institution ever to rank #1 nationally in the recruitment of National Merit Scholars. The 277-acre Research Campus in Norman was named the No.1 research campus in the nation by the Association of Research Parks.\u00a0 For more information, visit: http://soonerway.ou.edu/ and http://www.ou.edu/ Norman, which is part of the Oklahoma City metro area, is a culturally rich and vibrant community of almost 120,000 people that is a perennial contender on \u201cbest place to live\u201d rankings. Norman also has outstanding schools, amenities, and a low cost of living. To learn more about Norman, visit https://www.normanok.gov/residents-visitors/about-norman. Required Earned doctorate in Counselor Education from a CACREP-accredited program. Eligible for appropriate counselor certification and licensure in the State of Oklahoma. Evidence of an established research agenda, with scholarly contributions to the field through published research and presentations at national conferences, at a level appropriate for appointment at the rank of associate professor. Ability to teach courses in the CACREP core. Preferred A record of effective teaching, supervision, and professional activity. Demonstrated commitment to high-quality teaching and student advising through the use of traditional and web-based education technologies and/or experience or interest in teaching online courses. Ability and commitment to mentor and supervise graduate students. Demonstrated knowledge, commitment, and sensitivity to a variety of backgrounds, \u00a0collaboration, and innovation. To apply, all materials must be uploaded to the following website https://apply.interfolio.com/192538 . Please include the following: Comprehensive cover letter indicating interest in this position, and how the applicant\u2019s qualifications and experiences match the program\u2019s primary training goals, and required and preferred qualifications for the position. The cover letter may also address examples of the candidate\u2019s approach to teaching, research, service, and graduate student mentorship. Up to three representative reprints or preprints of scholarly publications (if available) Curriculum Vitae Current teaching evaluations, if applicable A list of the names, affiliations, and contact information of at least three professional references. Application Review Date and Contact Person The search committee will begin reviewing applications on October 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled.\u00a0 Inquiries should be directed to the search committee chair: Delini Fernando, Ph.D., Professional Counseling Program, University of Oklahoma; dfernando@ou.edu Additional Information For more information, go to: http://soonerway.ou.edu/ To obtain answers to specific questions, email Search Chair Dr. Delini Fernando at dfernando@ou.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192540": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Educational Psychology - Professional Counseling", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Jeannine Rainbolt College of Education: Department of Educational Psychology Location: Norman, Oklahoma The Position The Professional Counseling M.Ed. Program, in the Department of Educational Psychology, Jeannine Rainbolt College of Education, at the University of Oklahoma, seeks an Assistant Professor with a demonstrated record of strength in teaching, supervision, and research, along with a primary professional identity as a counselor educator. The successful candidate will serve a nine-month, tenure-track appointment beginning Fall 2027.\u00a0 Salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and background. Summer employment is contingent on student demand and funding. The Program The Professional Counseling Program at OU provides master\u2019s-level professional preparation for qualified individuals seeking to become clinical professional counselors, school counselors, and/or drug and alcohol counselors. The program offers (1) a 60-hour CACREP-accredited Clinical Professional Counseling track that meets the state of Oklahoma LPC requirements, (2) a 15-hour Drug and Alcohol counseling track, and (3) a\u00a015-hour\u00a0School Counseling Certificate.\u00a0 The Drug and Alcohol Counseling concentration includes five specialized courses that have been approved by the Oklahoma Board of Mental Health and Substance Abuse Services. Our primary training goals are to facilitate student integration of counseling theory, research, and practice; and to foster the development and application of holistic counseling skills. We seek to integrate varied multicultural perspectives into our students\u2019 professional training and development.\u00a0The program\u2019s foundational value is to foster understanding, critical thinking, and ethical decision-making. Information About the University of Oklahoma and the City of Norman OU is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement. OU serves the educational, cultural, economic, and health-care needs of the state, region, and nation from three campuses: Norman, Health Campus in Oklahoma City, and the Schusterman Center in Tulsa. OU enrolls over 30,000 students and has more than 2,700 full-time faculty members across 21 colleges. In 2014, OU became the first public institution ever to rank #1 nationally in the recruitment of National Merit Scholars. The 277-acre Research Campus in Norman was named the No.1 research campus in the nation by the Association of Research Parks.\u00a0 For more information, visit: http://soonerway.ou.edu/ and http://www.ou.edu/ Norman, which is part of the Oklahoma City metro area, is a culturally rich and vibrant community of almost 120,000 people that is a perennial contender on \u201cbest place to live\u201d rankings. Norman also has outstanding schools, amenities, and a low cost of living. To learn more about Norman, visit https://www.normanok.gov/residents-visitors/about-norman Required Earned doctorate in Counselor Education from a CACREP-accredited program by August 1, 2027 . Eligible for appropriate counselor certification and licensure in the State of Oklahoma. Demonstrated potential to establish a strong research agenda and scholarly contributions to the field through published research and presentations at national conferences. Ability to teach courses in the CACREP core. Preferred A record of effective teaching, supervision, and professional activity. Ability to contribute to one or more specialty areas such as school or addictions counseling. Demonstrated commitment to high-quality teaching and student advising through the use of traditional and web-based education technologies and/or experience or interest in teaching online courses. Ability and commitment to mentor and supervise graduate students. Demonstrated knowledge, commitment, and sensitivity to a variety of backgrounds, collaboration, and innovation. To apply, all materials must be uploaded to the following website https://apply.interfolio.com/192540 . Please include the following: Comprehensive cover letter indicating interest in this position, and how the applicant\u2019s qualifications and experiences match the program\u2019s primary training goals, and required and preferred qualifications for the position. The cover letter may also address examples of the candidate\u2019s approach to teaching, research, service, and graduate student mentorship. Up to three representative reprints or preprints of scholarly publications (if available) Curriculum Vitae Current teaching evaluations, if applicable A list of the names, affiliations, and contact information of at least three professional references. Application Review Date and Contact Person The search committee will begin reviewing applications on October 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Inquiries should be directed to the search committee chair: Delini Fernando, Ph.D., Professional Counseling Program, University of Oklahoma: dfernando@ou.edu Additional Information For more information, go to: http://soonerway.ou.edu/ To obtain answers to specific questions, email the Search Chair: Dr. Delini Fernando at dfernando@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195314": {"title": "Adjunct Lecturer of Engineering", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gallogly College of Engineering Location: Remote The Gallogly College of Engineering at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for part-time adjunct instructors in the Master of Science in Engineering Leadership & Management (ELM) program. ELM is a fully online graduate program for working engineers and technical professionals moving into leadership roles. Courses are delivered in accelerated 8-week terms. This is a continuing applicant pool. Applications are reviewed as teaching needs arise, and appointments are made by the course and by the term, contingent on enrollment and program need. Adjunct instructors may teach in one or more of the program\u2019s core areas: Decision analysis, data analytics, and problem solving for engineering management Leadership and team management Project, operations, and financial management in technical organizations Law, ethics, and professional conduct in engineering practice Technical and professional communication Capstone project advising Responsibilities include: Teaching an assigned graduate course online in the Canvas learning management system, using the program\u2019s established course design and learning outcomes Holding scheduled live online sessions, typically on weekday evenings (Central Time), and regular virtual office hours Giving timely, substantive feedback on student work and responding promptly to student questions Submitting grades and course records by university deadlines Contributing to program assessment by scoring designated assignments against program rubrics Coordinating with the program director on course updates and continuous improvement Required Ph.D. in engineering, engineering management, industrial and systems engineering, or a closely related field, conferred by the appointment start date Demonstrated expertise in one or more of the program\u2019s core areas listed above Ability to teach effectively online to working professionals Strong written and oral communication skills Preferred Prior graduate-level teaching experience, particularly in online or accelerated formats Professional experience in engineering leadership, management, or a related role in industry or government Experience with Canvas or a comparable learning management system Experience advising applied or team-based graduate projects Professional licensure or certification (e.g., P.E., PMP) Applications must be submitted online through Interfolio . A complete application includes: Cover letter (two pages maximum) stating which program areas you are prepared to teach and your experience teaching online or working with professional students Curriculum vitae Names and contact information for three professional references (references are contacted only for candidates under serious consideration) Unofficial graduate transcripts showing the conferred doctoral degree (official transcripts are required on appointment)"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/175446": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associates in Advanced Manufacturing", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gallogly College of Engineering: School of Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering Location: The University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK The Sooner Additive Manufacturing Laboratory (SAML) at the University of Oklahoma is seeking 1-3 highly motivated, innovative, and collaborative postdoctoral research associates to lead pioneering research in advanced manufacturing. Successful candidates will join a dynamic, interdisciplinary team and will have the opportunity to work on high-impact projects sponsored by federal agencies and industry partners. The positions are focused on three core research thrusts. Candidates should have demonstrated skills and a strong interest in one or more of the following areas: 1. In-Situ Monitoring for Advanced & Additive Manufacturing: This research focuses on developing and implementing novel sensing and data analytics techniques for real-time process monitoring and quality assurance. Responsibilities may include (i) integrating optical, thermal, acoustic and other sensors with manufacturing systems, (ii) developing data fusion and signal processing algorithms, and (iii) applying machine learning or AI models to detect and classify process signatures and defects. 2. Predictive Modeling for Manufacturing: This research aims to create high-fidelity, physics-based or data-driven models to simulate manufacturing processes. Responsibilities may include developing and validating finite element models to predict thermal history, microstructure evolution, residual stress, and final part performance. A strong background in computational mechanics is preferred. 3. Manufacturing Process Development & Optimization: This research involves hands-on development and optimization of novel manufacturing processes, with an emphasis on metal additive and subtractive manufacturing. Responsibilities include designing and conducting experiments, developing new material-process parameter sets, creating innovative post-processing techniques, material testing, and utilizing advanced materials characterization methods to link process variables to material properties. The postdoctoral research associates will be key members of SAML. The positions are for an initial one-year appointment, with the possibility of annual renewal for up to three years based on performance and availability of funding. Successful candidates will receive dedicated mentorship to foster their career development, including guidance on proposal writing, project management, publishing and presenting results, and mentoring graduate and undergraduate students. The positions include a competitive salary and a comprehensive benefits package. Review of applications will be done on a rolling basis and will continue until the positions are filled. The positions can start as soon as possible. About SAML SAML is a state-of-the-art facility dedicated to advancing the science and technology of manufacturing science at the University of Oklahoma. Our research covers the entire lifecycle of manufacturing, from fundamental process science to materials characterization and part qualification. The lab is equipped with a wide range of industrial additive manufacturing systems, advanced characterization tools, and high-performance computing resources. Required Qualifications: 1. A Ph.D. in mechanical engineering, aerospace engineering, materials science, industrial engineering, or a closely related discipline. 2. Demonstrated expertise in one of the three research areas described above. \u00b7 For the in-situ monitoring position: Proficiency with sensor integration, data acquisition systems (e.g., LabVIEW), and data analysis programming (e.g., Python, MATLAB). \u00b7\u00a0 For the modeling position: Significant experience with simulation software (e.g., Abaqus, COMSOL, ANSYS) and/or developing custom scientific code (e.g., C++, Fortran, Python). \u00b7 For the process development position: Hands-on experience with advanced manufacturing equipment (e.g., laser powder bed fusion, directed energy deposition) and materials characterization techniques. 3. A strong record of publishing research in peer-reviewed academic journals. 4. Excellent written, oral, and interpersonal communication skills. Preferred Qualifications: 1. A multidisciplinary skill set spanning more than one of the listed research areas. 2. Experience in mentoring students and contributing to research proposals. 3. Experience in proposal writing. 4. Demonstrated ability to work independently and direct research projects. 5. Experience in collaborating with partners in industry or at national laboratories. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter and curriculum vitae, and the names and telephone numbers of at least three references to Dr. Zahed Siddique ( zsiddique@ou.edu ) and Dr. Yingtao Liu ( yingtao@ou.edu ). Salary will be commensurate with experience, expertise and skills."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/183241": {"title": "Director, School of Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering (Professor)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gallogly College of Engineering: School of Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering Location: Norman, OK The School of Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering (AME) at the University of Oklahoma (OU) invites applications for a Director to start in the Fall of 2026. OU seeks an accomplished leader with a demonstrated record of collaborative and visionary leadership, excellence in research and external funding, and a strong commitment to advancing engineering education and industry partnerships. The Director will elevate AME\u2019s impact and innovation in research, creative activity, education, and external engagement. Housed within the Gallogly College of Engineering, AME offers ABET-accredited B.S. degrees in Aerospace Engineering and Mechanical Engineering, as well as M.S. and Ph.D. programs. The School supports robust undergraduate and graduate enrollments, a growing faculty, and a growing portfolio of research activity across aerospace systems, advanced materials and structures, robotics and autonomous systems, advanced manufacturing, fluid and thermal sciences, energy systems, computational engineering, and other interdisciplinary domains. This position offers a unique opportunity to shape the future trajectory of a dynamic and growing School within a major public research university. Additionally, The School of Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering benefits from partnerships among AME faculty with other colleges across the University of Oklahoma, as well as extensive engagement with regional and national aerospace, defense, energy, and advanced manufacturing industries. These collaborations create a dynamic environment for interdisciplinary research, innovation, workforce development, and technology translation. This is a full-time position that will be performed on-site in Norman, Oklahoma. This position will be at the rank of Professor and salary commensurate with experience with a generous benefits package. The ideal candidate will have a Ph.D. in Aerospace Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, or a closely related field. Additional required qualifications include: Collaborative and Visionary Leadership: Demonstrated ability to lead with a focus on collaboration, working closely with faculty to advance their initiatives and promote the collective success of the School. Proven commitment to fostering a supportive, high-performing academic environment grounded in shared governance. The successful candidate will articulate a compelling strategic vision and guide the School in achieving measurable outcomes in research, education, and external engagement. Research and Creative Activity: A distinguished record of peer-reviewed publications and significant external research funding, particularly from federal sources such as NASA, NSF, DoD, DOE, and related agencies. The candidate should demonstrate the ability to create and sustain a thriving research and creative environment, support interdisciplinary initiatives, and facilitate faculty collaborations that enhance collective research productivity and impact. Vision for Educational Excellence: Understanding of university-level teaching and ABET-accredited programs, with a demonstrated vision for advancing aerospace and mechanical engineering education. Commitment to student success, curricular innovation, experiential learning, graduate education, and workforce preparation aligned with evolving industry and societal needs. External Engagement and Industry Partnerships: Proven ability to engage internal and external stakeholders, including industry, federal laboratories, alumni, and community partners. Demonstrated success in building external industry partnerships and engagement that enhance research opportunities, student pathways, and the School\u2019s national visibility and impact. To view the position profile, including additional information on the positions and qualifications, please visit https://www.dsgco.com/search/22940-university-of-oklahoma-director-of-aerospace-and-mechanical-engineering/ . Review of candidates will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. For full consideration, please submit your curriculum vitae (CV) and cover letter electronically and in confidence, at the following link: https://talent-profile.dsgco.com/search/v2/22940 Confidential nominations, referrals, or questions should be directed to: OUDirectorAME@dsgco.com ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195402": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Metal Additive Manufacturing Modeling and Analysis", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gallogly College of Engineering: School of Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering Location: The University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK The Sooner Additive Manufacturing Laboratory (SAML) at the University of Oklahoma is seeking several highly motivated, innovative, and collaborative postdoctoral research associates to lead pioneering research in additive manufacturing. Successful candidates will join a dynamic, interdisciplinary team and will have the opportunity to work on high-impact projects sponsored by federal agencies and industry partners. Candidates must be a US person or U.S. citizen. The positions are focused on three broad research areas. Candidates should have demonstrated skills and a strong interest in one or more of the following areas: \u00b7 Non-destructive testing \u00b7 Finite element modeling (FEM) \u00b7 Metallurgical characterization and analysis The postdoctoral research associates will be key members of SAML. The positions are for an initial one-year appointment, with the possibility of annual renewal for up to three years based on performance and availability of funding. Successful candidates will receive dedicated mentorship to foster their career development, including guidance on proposal writing, project management, publishing and presenting results, and mentoring graduate and undergraduate students. The positions include a competitive salary and a comprehensive benefits package. Review of applications will be done on a rolling basis and will continue until the positions are filled. The positions can start as soon as possible. About SAML SAML is a state-of-the-art facility dedicated to advancing the science and technology of manufacturing science at the University of Oklahoma. Our research covers the entire lifecycle of additive manufacturing, from fundamental process science to materials characterization and part qualification. The lab is equipped with a wide range of industrial additive manufacturing systems, advanced characterization tools, and high-performance computing resources. Required Qualifications: 1. A Ph.D. in mechanical engineering, aerospace engineering, electrical engineering, materials science, industrial engineering, or a closely related discipline. 2. Demonstrated expertise in one of the three research areas described above. 3. Experience with additive manufacturing technology and materials characterization techniques. 4. A strong record of publishing research in peer-reviewed academic journals. 5. Excellent written, oral, and interpersonal communication skills. Preferred Qualifications: 1. A multidisciplinary skill set spanning one or more research areas listed above. 2. Experience in mentoring students and contributing to research proposals. 3. Demonstrated ability to work independently and direct research projects. 4. Experience in proposal writing. 5. Experience in collaborating with partners in industry or at national laboratories. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter,\u00a0 curriculum vitae, and the names and contact information for at least three references.\u00a0 Salary will be commensurate with experience, expertise, and skills."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195407": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Metal Additive Manufacturing Process Development", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gallogly College of Engineering: School of Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering Location: The University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK The Sooner Additive Manufacturing Laboratory (SAML) at the University of Oklahoma is seeking several highly motivated, innovative, and collaborative postdoctoral research associates to lead pioneering research in additive manufacturing. Successful candidates will join a dynamic, interdisciplinary team and will have the opportunity to work on high-impact projects sponsored by federal agencies and industry partners. Candidates must be a US person or U.S. citizen. The positions are focused on three broad research areas. Candidates should have demonstrated skills and a strong interest in one or more of the following areas: \u00b7 Hands\u2011on metal AM experimentation \u00b7 Process simulation \u00b7 AI/machine learning for process optimization The postdoctoral research associates will be key members of SAML. The positions are for an initial one-year appointment, with the possibility of annual renewal for up to three years based on performance and availability of funding. Successful candidates will receive dedicated mentorship to foster their career development, including guidance on proposal writing, project management, publishing and presenting results, and mentoring graduate and undergraduate students. The positions include a competitive salary and a comprehensive benefits package. Review of applications will be done on a rolling basis and will continue until the positions are filled. The positions can start as soon as possible. About SAML SAML is a state-of-the-art facility dedicated to advancing the science and technology of manufacturing science at the University of Oklahoma. Our research covers the entire lifecycle of additive manufacturing, from fundamental process science to materials characterization and part qualification. The lab is equipped with a wide range of industrial additive manufacturing systems, advanced characterization tools, and high-performance computing resources. Required Qualifications: 1. A Ph.D. in mechanical engineering, aerospace engineering, electrical engineering, materials science, industrial engineering, or a closely related discipline. 2. Demonstrated expertise in one of the three research areas described above. 3. Experience with additive manufacturing technology and materials characterization techniques. 4. A strong record of publishing research in peer-reviewed academic journals. 5. Excellent written, oral, and interpersonal communication skills. Preferred Qualifications: 1. A multidisciplinary skill set spanning one or more research areas listed above. 2. Experience in mentoring students and contributing to research proposals. 3. Demonstrated ability to work independently and direct research projects. 4. Experience in proposal writing. 5. Experience in collaborating with partners in industry or at national laboratories. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter,\u00a0 curriculum vitae, and the names and contact information for at least three references.\u00a0 Salary will be commensurate with experience, expertise, and skills."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195409": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Sensor Integration for Metal Additive Manufacturing", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gallogly College of Engineering: School of Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering Location: The University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK The Sooner Additive Manufacturing Laboratory (SAML) at the University of Oklahoma is seeking several highly motivated, innovative, and collaborative postdoctoral research associates to lead pioneering research in additive manufacturing. Successful candidates will join a dynamic, interdisciplinary team and will have the opportunity to work on high-impact projects sponsored by federal agencies and industry partners. Candidates must be a US person or U.S. citizen. The positions are focused on three broad research areas. Candidates should have demonstrated skills and a strong interest in one or more of the following areas: \u00b7 In\u2011situ monitoring for additive manufacturing \u00b7 Sensor integration & data acquisition \u00b7 AI/machine learning for large dataset analysis The postdoctoral research associates will be key members of SAML. The positions are for an initial one-year appointment, with the possibility of annual renewal for up to three years based on performance and availability of funding. Successful candidates will receive dedicated mentorship to foster their career development, including guidance on proposal writing, project management, publishing and presenting results, and mentoring graduate and undergraduate students. The positions include a competitive salary and a comprehensive benefits package. Review of applications will be done on a rolling basis and will continue until the positions are filled. The positions can start as soon as possible. About SAML SAML is a state-of-the-art facility dedicated to advancing the science and technology of manufacturing science at the University of Oklahoma. Our research covers the entire lifecycle of additive manufacturing, from fundamental process science to materials characterization and part qualification. The lab is equipped with a wide range of industrial additive manufacturing systems, advanced characterization tools, and high-performance computing resources. Required Qualifications: 1. A Ph.D. in mechanical engineering, aerospace engineering, electrical engineering, materials science, industrial engineering, or a closely related discipline. 2. Demonstrated expertise in one of the three research areas described above. 3. Experience with additive manufacturing technology and materials characterization techniques. 4. A strong record of publishing research in peer-reviewed academic journals. 5. Excellent written, oral, and interpersonal communication skills. Preferred Qualifications: 1. A multidisciplinary skill set spanning one or more research areas listed above. 2. Experience in mentoring students and contributing to research proposals. 3. Demonstrated ability to work independently and direct research projects. 4. Experience in proposal writing. 5. Experience in collaborating with partners in industry or at national laboratories. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter,\u00a0 curriculum vitae, and the names and contact information for at least three references.\u00a0 Salary will be commensurate with experience, expertise, and skills."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/102919": {"title": "Endowed Faculty Positions in Biomedical Engineering", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gallogly College of Engineering: Stephenson School of Biomedical Engineering Location: Norman, OK The Stephenson School of Biomedical Engineering (SBME) in the Gallogly College of Engineering (GCoE) at the University of Oklahoma (OU) seeks applicants for two senior faculty positions at the associate/full professor level with endowed Stephenson Chairs available. SBME encourages applications from leading researchers in Biomedical Engineering, with an emphasis in immunoengineering including, but not limited to, cancer, diabetes, vaccines, aging, and nanomedicine. SBME\u2019s mission is to educate the next generation of biomedical engineers and to create new technologies that advance human health. These endowed faculty positions are aligned with the SBME, GCoE, and OU strategic plans. Individuals with a strong track record in leadership and collaboration in team-based biomedical fields are encouraged to apply. SBME was founded in 2016, and we moved into our home in the brand-new Gallogly Hall building in 2019, with lab space ready for these new faculty hires. SBME is committed to undergraduate and graduate education, with B.S., M.S., and Ph.D. programs in Biomedical Engineering. SBME currently has 12 regular faculty, ~230 undergraduates, and ~37 graduate students. Our faculty and students enjoy extensive partnerships with scientists and clinicians at the OU Health Sciences Center ( OUHSC ) and the Oklahoma Medical Research Foundation ( OMRF ). Known as the City of Festivals, Norman is a culturally rich and vibrant town located in the Oklahoma City metro area. With outstanding schools, amenities, and a low cost of living, Norman is a perennial contender on the \u201cBest Places to Live\u201d rankings. Explore further at VisitNorman.com . OU is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university with three campuses: the main campus in Norman, the Health Sciences Center in Oklahoma City, and another campus in Tulsa. OU enrolls ~30,000 students. Successful candidates should have a PhD or MD/PhD degree in Biomedical Engineering or a closely related field and bring an established vibrant research program with a strong track record of external funding, with additional weight given to NIH sponsorship. We seek candidates who support inclusive excellence in all aspects of faculty responsibility, including within the classroom, the academic community, and within their professional commitments. Applications (a cover letter and curriculum vitae) should be submitted at http://apply.interfolio.com/102919 . Review of the applications will begin immediately, but the positions will remain open until filled. Salary and benefits are nationally competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience. Competitive start-up packages will be available. Nominations are welcomed and should be sent to Search Committee Chair Professor Stefan Wilhelm at stefan.wilhelm@ou.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195191": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gallogly College of Engineering: Stephenson School of Biomedical Engineering Location: Norman, OK We are seeking a highly motivated and collaborative postdoctoral researcher with expertise in neurophysiology and/or neuromechanics, applied in the context of sensorimotor control and neuroengineering. Responsibilities will include one or more of the following: \u2022 Leading research project(s) examining neural control of movement, using noninvasive and mildly invasive electrophysiology techniques alongside biomechanical methods (training provided for specialized procedures). \u2022 Designing, assembling, and/or programming devices for novel experiments (e.g., actuators, sensors), and/or supporting student projects in device engineering. \u2022 Interfacing with a diverse team of experts, including clinicians, specialized engineers, and neurosurgeons, to support collaborative and translational research efforts. About the program: The Neuroengineering Program aims to build fundamental understanding of nervous system physiology while developing assistive technologies that improve quality of life for individuals with neurological disorders. The program integrates engineering principles with neuroscience to design and implement novel tools for neural recording, stimulation, and modulation, ultimately advancing both basic knowledge of nervous system function and translational applications addressing unmet clinical needs. The postdoctoral researcher will receive multidisciplinary training and mentorship in professional and personal development, including preparing high-impact publications and conference presentations, developing translational neurotechnology, and mentoring junior trainees. The position includes a competitive salary and health insurance. Location: The Neuroengineering Lab is based at the University of Oklahoma (OU), with lab space located on the south side of the OU-Norman campus, a hub for research and engineering facilities. We are part of the Stephenson School of Biomedical Engineering (SBME), which was founded with a historic $30 million gift in 2016 and now includes 18 core faculty and 20+ total graduate faculty. Known as the City of Festivals, Norman is a culturally vibrant town in the Oklahoma City metro area, consistently ranked among the \u201cBest Places to Live\u201d for its schools, amenities, and low cost of living. Learn more at VisitNorman.com. Required A PhD in neuroscience, biomedical/electrical/mechanical engineering, kinesiology, or a related field is required. Wanted Demonstrated experience in neurophysiology and/or neuromechanics research, strong communication skills Ability to work collegially within a diverse team of rehabilitation scientists/clinicians, engineers, experimentalists, and importantly trainees at varying experience levels Genuine interest in high-impact fundamental and/or translational science, as well as in the development or implementation of neural interfaces and neuromodulation devices Please email your CV, a brief statement of your research interests and alignment with the lab, and contact information for two references to Dr. Robyn Mildren at robyn.mildren@ou.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/178106": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Sustainable  Chemical, Biological and Materials or Biomedical Engineering - Bioprocessing", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gallogly College of Engineering: School of Sustainable Chemical, Biological and Materials Engineering Location: Norman, OK The Gallogly College of Engineering (GCoE) at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for a tenure-track position in the area of Bioprocessing at the Assistant or Associate Professor level, with expected start date of August 2026.\u00a0 We are interested in applicants with both professional and academic experience in Bioprocessing. The prospective candidate is expected to complement OU's research portfolio in the field of biotechnology to produce pharmaceuticals using living cells and to design biosystems with such capabilities. The GCoE received a $7M investment for a multidisciplinary project in bioprocessing and biologics and established a state-of-the-art Bioprocessing Core Facility. The successful candidate is expected to advance research and workforce development through this facility ( https://www.ou.edu/coe/research/centers/bioprocessing-core-facility ). The position will be either with the School of Sustainable Chemical, Biological and Materials Engineering (SCBME) or with the Stephenson School of Biomedical Engineering (SBME). The successful candidate is expected to collaborate with ongoing research initiatives within the Gallogly College of Engineering ( https://www.ou.edu/coe/research/clusters-and-initiatives ) and should be able to engage in both disciplinary and interdisciplinary research. There is an expectation that the candidate will develop a funded research program related to the research areas outlined above.\u00a0 The successful candidate will be expected to teach courses relevant to bioprocessing at the undergraduate and graduate level and to demonstrate a commitment to excellent and innovative instruction. Expectations include collaboration with other faculty members and effective mentoring of students with an ethos of care and support. To be considered for this position applicants must have a Ph.D. in Chemical or Biomedical Engineering or a closely related discipline.\u00a0 Candidates should present evidence of the ability to engage in high-quality scholarship or relevant industrial experience through a leadership position as demonstrated through publications and/or patents. They should also demonstrate a commitment to developing cutting-edge externally funded research activities and to effectively teaching and mentoring graduate and undergraduate students.\u00a0 Highly qualified candidates may be considered at the rank of associate professor and/or for an endowed position. OU utilizes Interfolio's Faculty Search for applications. Applicants to this position receive a free dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. The following application materials must be submitted electronically via Interfolio, at https://apply.interfolio.com A cover letter. The cover letter may elaborate on the applicant\u2019s qualifications for the position in ways that go beyond the information contained in the other documents, such as providing examples of the candidate\u2019s approach to research, teaching, graduate student mentorship and service. Curriculum vitae. Contact information for at least three reference letter writers. Up to 3 samples of published research, such as articles (reprints/preprints) or links to software programs and applications. A statement of research experience and plans may be included but is optional. We encourage applicants to apply by March 31, 2026, for full consideration, although applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Application review will begin immediately, and the position will remain open until filled. Please send inquiries to the SCBME Director, Prof. Dimitrios Papavassiliou ( dvpapava@ou.edu ) or the Search Committee Chair, Prof. Vassilios Sikavitsas ( vis@ou.edu )."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188158": {"title": "Research Assistant Professor of Computer Science - Omics Data Science for Immuno-Engineering", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gallogly College of Engineering: School of Computer Science Location: 110 W Boyd St Norman OK 73019 The School of Computer Science at the University of Oklahoma invites the applications for two non-tenure track Research Assistant Professor positions in Omics Data Science and Bioinformatics for Immuno-Engineering. These research-focused faculty positions will support interdisciplinary multi-omics research through collaborative data analysis and methodology development for immuno-engineering applications. Responsibilities The successful candidates will be expected to: \u00b7 Collaborate with investigators on the design and analysis of multi-omics studies; \u00b7 Develop, validate, and apply computational workflows for large-scale omics data, including single-cell and spatial data; \u00b7 Support data integration, interpretation, manuscript preparation, and data sharing; \u00b7 Lead research and development of new bioinformatics, machine learning, and data science methods for immuno-engineering; \u00b7 Develop grant applications and contribute to proposals in immuno-engineering; \u00b7 Participate in workshops, consultations, and other collaborative research activities. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Computer Science, Bioinformatics, Computational Biology, Data Science, Biomedical Informatics, Statistics, or a closely related field. Candidates should have experience in computational analysis of biological or biomedical data, strong programming skills, experience with scalable computing environments, and the ability to work effectively in interdisciplinary teams. Applicants should submit a cover letter, CV, and contact information for three references to interfolio . Applications will be reviewed until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188172": {"title": "Research Assistant Professor of Computer Science - Agentic AI systems for Health Behavior Intervention", "description": "Posted: Sep 23, 2026 Deadline: Nov 30, 2026 Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gallogly College of Engineering: School of Computer Science Location: 110 W Boyd St Norman OK 73019 The School of Computer Science at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for a 12-month non-tenure track Research Assistant Professor position in Computer Science with a research focus on agentic AI systems for health behavior intervention. The successful candidate will contribute to the design, development, evaluation, and deployment of advanced AI systems that support behavior change, personalized intervention, and decision support in health-related settings. Responsibilities The successful candidate will be expected to: \u00b7 Conduct cutting-edge research on agentic AI systems for health behavior intervention. \u00b7 Develop intelligent, autonomous, and human-centered AI agents that support personalized health coaching, behavior change, intervention planning, and adaptive decision-making. \u00b7 Design, implement, and evaluate AI methods such as large language model agents, multi-agent systems, reinforcement learning, conversational AI, human-AI interaction, and trustworthy AI for health applications. \u00b7 Collaborate with interdisciplinary teams spanning computer science, public health, medicine, psychology, behavioral science, and related fields. \u00b7 Publish in leading journals and conferences in artificial intelligence, machine learning, digital health, health informatics, and related areas. \u00b7 Contribute to the preparation of grant proposals and help expand externally funded research programs. \u00b7 Mentor graduate and undergraduate student researchers. \u00b7 Participate in appropriate professional, academic, and service activities consistent with a research faculty appointment. A Ph.D. in Computer Science, Artificial Intelligence, Machine Learning, Data Science, Biomedical Informatics, Human-Computer Interaction, or a closely related field by the start date of the appointment. \u00b7 Demonstrated research expertise in one or more of the following areas: agentic AI, large language models, intelligent agents, machine learning, reinforcement learning, human-AI interaction, digital health, health informatics, or computational behavioral science. \u00b7 A record of scholarly productivity appropriate for appointment at the rank of Research Assistant Professor. \u00b7 Strong programming, system-building, and experimental evaluation skills. \u00b7 Excellent written and oral communication skills. Preferred Qualifications Preference will be given to candidates with: \u00b7 Experience developing AI systems for healthcare, public health, mental health, or behavior-change applications. \u00b7 Experience with real-world deployment, user studies, clinical collaborations, or community-based health intervention research. \u00b7 A strong publication record in top-tier venues. \u00b7 Experience contributing to or leading externally funded research projects. \u00b7 A demonstrated ability to work in interdisciplinary research environments. Applicants should submit a cover letter, CV, and contact information for three references to interfolio . Applications will be reviewed until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191967": {"title": "Research Assistant Professor of Computer Science - Medical Imaging Informatics and AI for Translational Cancer Research", "description": "Posted: Sep 23, 2026 Deadline: Nov 30, 2026 Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gallogly College of Engineering: School of Computer Science Location: 110 W Boyd St Norman OK 73019 The School of Computer Science at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for a 12-month non-tenure track Research Assistant Professor position in Computer Science with a focus on informatics and AI/ML for medical imaging in translational cancer research. This Research Assistant Professor will be part of the Clinical Imaging and Data Resources Core (CIDRC) of the NIH COBRE Oklahoma Center of Medical Imaging for Translational Cancer Research (OCMICR). The position will perform independent and collaborative research in medical imaging AI and informatics, including image segmentation, representation learning, multimodal data integration, quantitative imaging biomarkers, AI-assisted annotation, and reproducible computational analysis. Responsibilities The successful candidate will be expected to: Conduct methodological and collaborative research in areas such as medical image segmentation, classification, registration, multimodal learning, radiomics/pathomics, AI-assisted annotation, foundation models, and quantitative imaging biomarker discovery; Collaborate with investigators on the design and analysis of cancer imaging studies involving radiology, pathology, microscopy, optical imaging, ultrasound/photoacoustic imaging, and other biomedical imaging modalities; Support the development of imaging informatics and computational infrastructure for organizing, processing, analyzing, and sharing multimodal imaging datasets; Develop reproducible computational workflows for image preprocessing, feature extraction, model training, evaluation, visualization, and downstream statistical or machine learning analysis; Integrate imaging data with relevant clinical, biospecimen, biomarker, molecular, or other biomedical data to support translational interpretation; Contribute to manuscripts, grant applications, preliminary-data generation, software development, documentation, and training activities; Participate in interdisciplinary consultations, workshops, and collaborative research activities related to CIDRC and the broader medical imaging research community A Ph.D. in Computer Science, Data Science, Biomedical Informatics, Electrical Engineering, or a closely related field. Demonstrated research expertise in one or more of the following areas: machine learning, computer vision, medical image analysis, or biomedical imaging informatics. A record of scholarly productivity appropriate for appointment at the rank of Research Assistant Professor. Strong programming, system-building, and experimental evaluation skills. Ability to develop, validate, and apply computational methods for biomedical imaging data. Ability to work effectively in interdisciplinary research teams involving clinicians, biomedical engineers, biostatisticians, and cancer researchers. Excellent written and oral communication skills. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and contact information for three references to Interfolio . Applications will be reviewed until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/170589": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Electrical and Computer Engineering - High-Performance Computing", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gallogly College of Engineering: School of Electrical and Computer Engineering Location: Norman, OK, United States As part of its strategic plan, The University of Oklahoma (OU) continues to grow its Gallogly College of Engineering (GCoE).\u00a0 As such, one tenure-track faculty position at the rank of assistant professor is currently available within the University of Oklahoma\u2019s (OU) School of Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE).\u00a0 ECE welcomes applicants who have demonstrated experience and expertise in high-performance computing, with specific areas described below, and who are committed to being an integral part of the research and educational mission at OU. OU is continuing to expand its internationally known radar program in collaboration with its School of ECE. The Advanced Radar Research Center (ARRC, http://arrc.ou.edu ), which serves as the cornerstone of the radar program, is a 125+ person research center on campus that has developed advanced radar capabilities and all-digital phased arrays. Modern all-digital communication systems, multi-function radar, AI/ML algorithms rely on high performance digital signal processing and real-time computing to provide high-fidelity results in an actionable timeframe. The ARRC intends to grow these capabilities in high-performance computing. Position:\u00a0High-Performance Computing A High-Performance Computing (HPC) expert for developing solutions for digital signal processing applications is encouraged to apply.\u00a0 A successful candidate would develop a research and teaching agenda around: Co-Designing algorithms and hardware for processing massive amounts of high-dimensional streaming tensor data for latency sensitive and real-time applications. Low-SWaP, high-performance embedded computing (HPEC) High-speed, low-latency data interfaces and networking Real-time computing on heterogeneous processor types Optimized GPU computing and exploitation of GPU architectures for HPEC (tensors, multi-GPU instantiations, advances in GPU for AI/ML) Compute-focused optimization of System-on-Chip (SoC) technologies. The University of Oklahoma : OU is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement, serving the educational, cultural, economic, and healthcare needs of the state, region, and nation from three campuses: Norman, Health Sciences Center in Oklahoma City, and the Schusterman Center in Tulsa. OU enrolls over 30,000 students and has more than 2,700 full-time faculty members in 21 colleges. Norman is a vibrant university town of around 113,000 inhabitants with a growing entertainment and art scene. With outstanding schools, amenities, and a low cost of living, Norman is a perennial contender in \u201cbest place to live\u201d rankings. Visit http://www.ou.edu/flipbook and http://soonerway.ou.edu for more information. Within an easy commute, Oklahoma City features a dynamic economy and outstanding cultural venues adding to the region\u2019s growing appeal. The position requires an earned doctorate in electrical and/or computer engineering, or a closely related discipline at the time of initial appointment. Serious consideration will be given to exceptional candidates who also possess a record of professional/industrial achievement sufficient for an untenured position at the rank of Assistant Professor. The successful candidate will be expected to: (a) contribute to research efforts in heterogeneous, real-time computing for multi-channel radar systems (b) teach at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. APPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS Confidential review of applications will begin on March 1, 2025, and will continue until the position is filled. Candidates are invited to submit: 1) a letter of interest, 2) a detailed curriculum vitae, and 3) contact information for four references who will be contacted only upon approval from the applicant. https://apply.interfolio.com/170589 The letter of interest may contain a research and teaching statement summarizing the applicant\u2019s prior contributions to research and their goals for developing a research program at OU, their past instructional and mentorship experiences, and plans/goals for teaching at OU (including existing and proposed courses) and advising a varied cohort of undergraduate and graduate students. Inquiries about the position can be addressed to the search committee chair and co-chair: Prof. Tian-You Yu (Chair), School of Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of Oklahoma, 110 W. Boyd St., Room 150, Norman, OK 73019-1102; Email: tyu@ou.edu. Prof. Mark Yeary (Co-Chair), School of Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of Oklahoma, 110 W. Boyd St., Room 150, Norman, OK 73019-1102 Email: yeary@ou.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/184078": {"title": "Director of the School of Electrical and Computer Engineering (Professor)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gallogly College of Engineering: School of Electrical and Computer Engineering Location: Norman, OK, United States The University of Oklahoma (OU) and its Gallogly College of Engineering invite applications for the position of Director of the School of Electrical & Computer Engineering (ECE). OU seeks a visionary leader who will provide comprehensive oversight of ECE\u2019s academic, research, and public engagement enterprises and play a critical role in advancing ECE\u2019s mission, vision, and commitments. The School of Electrical and Computer Engineering is one of the largest programs in the Gallogly College of Engineering offering ABET-accredited B.S. degrees in Computer Engineering and Electrical Engineering, accelerated BS/MS degrees, as well as M.S. and Ph.D. programs. As of Fall 2025, ECE includes 35 faculty, 143 graduate students, and 570 undergraduate majors. ECE is a key contributor to the educational mission of the University of Oklahoma. The ECE student body is active in numerous competition teams, student organizations, and research. ECE faculty have earned prestigious funding which includes research awards from NSF, NIH, DARPA, DOE, NOAA, etc. In 2025, research expenditures in ECE totaled $19.9 million; thus, ranking it as one of the most productive units on campus. Both ECE and the Gallogly College of Engineering are experiencing strong growth in research, corporate and private donations, and student enrollment. Candidates will possess the following required qualifications: A Ph.D. in Electrical Engineering (EE), Electrical & Computer Engineering (ECE), or a closely related field including, but not limited to, engineering, physics, material science or computer science; a distinguished record of scholarly achievement consistent with appointment as a tenured Full Professor at an R1 institution; deep commitment to academic excellence through promoting student success, cutting-edge research, and international impact; and an ability to communicate effectively with faculty, staff, students, senior university administrators, alumni, philanthropic donors, federal and industry partners. Desired qualifications include the following: Demonstrates strong, proactive leadership and advocacy skills with the ability to represent ECE effectively at the college and university levels; articulates and executes a clear, forward-looking vision for growing the department, improving rankings, and strengthening faculty research; administrative experience with proven ability to manage budgets, facilities, personnel, and programs with transparency; ability to create a tactical roadmap of hiring goals, research goals, instructional goals, and service goals for ECE with a 5-year horizon, in consort with ECE faculty and OU\u2019s senior administration, especially in the context of OU\u2019s Lead-On Strategic plan; experience with or supervision of online educational content preparation; awareness and appreciation of innovative teaching methods and educational technology; a demonstrated ability to attract and retain strong faculty, students, and staff; a demonstrated commitment to shared governance and ability to build strong positive relationships with multiple constituencies; effective communication skills: demonstrated ability to communicate research and teaching concepts clearly and effectively to varied audiences; ability to understand and foster secure research; and experience building and sustaining industry partnerships, government collaborations, and other external relationships. To view the position profile, including additional information on the positions and qualifications, please visit: https://dsgco.com/search/22941-university-of-oklahoma-director-of-electrical-and-computer-engineering. Review of candidates will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. For full consideration, please submit your curriculum vitae (CV) and cover letter electronically and in confidence, at the following link: https://talent-profile.dsgco.com/search/v2/22941 Confidential nominations or referrals should be directed to: OUDirectorECE@dsgco.com. This is a full-time position that will be performed on-site in Oklahoma. The director will be hired at the rank of professor. Salary commensurate with experience with a generous benefits package."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/175309": {"title": "Adjunct Instructor/Lecturer of Journalism and Mass Communication", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gaylord College of Journalism and Mass Communication Location: Norman Are you someone working in or who have worked in the media and mass communication fields? Do you have a Master\u2019s degree? Are you looking to pass on your experience and know-how to future generations of media professionals? The Gaylord College of Journalism and Mass Communication is the No. 1 media program in the country according to the 2025 Broadcast Education Association school rankings. We are looking for adjuncts to help shape the education and future of our undergraduate students across our five majors: Advertising, Creative Media Production, Journalism, Public Relations and Professional Writing. Applications for these positions will be reviewed as they are submitted and as we have a need to fill gaps in our teaching schedules. Applicants should provide 1) a cover letter describing their qualifications and their vision and plans for this position, 2) a curriculum vitae/resume that details professional experience, 3) names, addresses, phone numbers, and email of at least three references. For specific questions about the positions and application process, please contact Associate Dean for Academic Affairs, Dr. Elanie Steyn at elanie@ou.edu . Applications should be submitted online via Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com/175309 ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187242": {"title": "Associate/Full Professor of Journalism and Mass Communication - Public Relations", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gaylord College of Journalism and Mass Communication Location: Norman The Gaylord College of Journalism and Mass Communication at the University of Oklahoma seeks an outstanding candidate for a tenured Associate/Full Professor position in Public Relations, beginning Fall 2027. The college prides itself on the outstanding professional preparation of students grounded in personal interactions with faculty and extraordinary opportunities to connect with industry professionals regionally, nationally, and internationally. Courses are designed to be experiential, providing students with hands-on experience in developing strategies and plans and creating communications assets for outside clients. The selected candidate will have a robust research agenda in public relations/strategic communication, aligned with the OU Research Strategic Framework (https://www.ou.edu/research-norman/strategic-framework) and will teach public relations undergraduate and graduate-level classes in the #1 media program in the nation, according to BEA 2025 and 2026 rankings. The candidate will be expected to help teach core classes in the public relations curriculum, including the following: Public Relations Writing, Public Relations Ethics, Public Relations Research, Public Relations Campaigns, and Contemporary Problems in Public Relations. The candidate should conduct research and teach elective courses in one or more of the following: nonprofit PR, fundraising and event planning; public affairs/government/political campaigns, technology/AI/digital media/AR/VR, travel/tourism/hospitality, cross-cultural strategic communication, environmental/ESG/CSA/CSR, PR management/leadership, entertainment/fashion/lifestyle PR, or other areas of expertise. The ability to work in cross-disciplinary settings and build connections with researchers across campus through OU trans-disciplinary Institutes and Centers to support the OU Strategic Plan and the ambitious goals of the OU Project 200, as well as with area professionals, businesses, and alumni, is expected. Proven ability to pursue external research funding is required. As a senior faculty member, the candidate will also be a leader and active member of the Gaylord College and the University of Oklahoma community and will chair graduate student committees and serve on various college and university committees. The rank of a successful candidate will be selected depending on qualifications and experience, and will be hired with a 40% teaching, 40% research, and 20% service load. Candidates should have a Ph.D. in Public Relations or a related discipline. Professional experience in public relations or a related field is highly desired. Candidates must demonstrate evidence of robust prior success and excellence in all three areas of research, teaching, and service, as well as a current, ongoing, cutting-edge public relations/strategic communication-focused research program with clear evidence of external funding and cross-disciplinary research collaboration. The candidate should also be able to demonstrate a comprehensive understanding of contemporary public relations/strategic communication theories, beyond one theoretical paradigm and school of thought. Applications for the search will be reviewed beginning September 2026, and will be accepted until the position is filled.\u00a0\u00a0Applicants should provide: 1) a cover letter describing their qualifications and their vision and plans for this position, 2) a curriculum vitae, 3) a one-page research statement, and 4) names, addresses, phone numbers, and emails of at least three references. For specific questions about the position and application process, please contact the search committee chair Jensen Moore, Ph.D. (jensenmoore@ou.edu). All submissions must go through Interfolio ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/175894": {"title": "Visiting Assistant Professor of Law", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: College of Law Location: University of Oklahoma College of Law The University of Oklahoma (OU) College of Law seeks outstanding applicants, entry-level, for a Visiting Assistant Professorship (VAP) starting in the 2026-27 academic year .\u00a0 OU Law welcomes outstanding applicants in all areas, with particular interest in curricular needs in Religion, Culture and Indian Law , Economic Development in Indian Country , Indian Child Welfare Act , Public Health in Indian Country, Criminal Law in Indian Country, Water law in Indian Country, Criminal Justice in Indian Country, or other specific areas of legal interest related to Indian Country.\u00a0 \u00a0Applicants must demonstrate potential for scholarly achievement and classroom teaching.\u00a0 Applicants must have a JD from an ABA-accredited law school, outstanding academic credentials, and significant law-related practice and/or clerkship experience. The VAP is appointed to a one-year term with the possibility of an additional one-year renewal.\u00a0 The program is designed for promising scholars whose area of research focuses on Native American law and Indigenous issues and who plan to pursue a career in law teaching. \u00a0This is a full-time position, and the VAP will be expected to be in residence at OU and participate broadly in the intellectual life of the law school, develop a scholarly agenda, publish at least one law-review article, and make substantial headway on one work in progress. The VAP will teach one course per semester and receive mentoring in their teaching and scholarly work from an advisory faculty team, the Associate Dean for Academic Affairs, and the Associate Dean for Faculty Development and Research.\u00a0 One course will focus on a substantive area of law related to Indian Country, and the other course will be either a first-year doctrinal course or upper level required course.\u00a0 The VAP will also receive financial support for their scholarship, including access to research assistants, coverage of professional travel, and access to faculty development activities. The position is designed to support the VAP in preparing to apply for full-time, tenure-track legal academic positions.\u00a0 The VAP will present a paper to a faculty group and receive feedback in preparation for the entry-level job market.\u00a0 The VAP will be eligible to apply for a full-time position at OU College of Law as part of a national search subject to the College of Law\u2019s needs.\u00a0 Salary will be commensurate with experience.\u00a0 The successful candidate will possess a JD degree from an ABA-accredited law school, possess a professional record of excellence, a commitment to excellence in teaching, and the potential for excellence in scholarly endeavors. Review of candidates will begin immediately .\u00a0 \u00a0Expressions of interest should be submitted as soon as possible to Phyllis Taite , Associate Dean for Academic Affairs , University of Oklahoma College of Law, c/o Rachael Davis at rdavis@ou.edu .\u00a0 Please include \"VAP Application\" in the subject line. \u00a0Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. Applicants should submit the following documents: Statement of interest; Ideal course package (listed in order of preference); A Curriculum vitae; Contact information for two academic references (including name, title, email address, and telephone number); Copies of or link to any scholarly legal articles published, unpublished, or in draft form you wish to be considered; and A research proposal or agenda of 2,000 words or less."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189024": {"title": "Associate Professor of Law", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: College of Law Location: University of Oklahoma College of Law The University of Oklahoma College of Law seeks to fill two tenure-track faculty positions beginning in the 2027-2028 academic year. The primary subject matter areas for these hires are Constitutional Law, Criminal Law, Professional Responsibility, Federal Indian Law, and Torts.\u00a0 The College also has curricular needs in Remedies, Property, Evidence, Wills and Trusts, Commercial Law, and related areas.\u00a0 We anticipate hiring across these areas based on candidate quality, curricular need, and alignment with available fund opportunities. OU Law's strong national reputation is buttressed by a commitment to attracting and supporting excellent faculty with summer research grants, publication placement bonuses, course reductions based on scholarly productivity, and an extraordinary number of endowed positions.\u00a0 OU Law is committed to excellence in educating legal professionals, advancing legal scholarship, and serving the public.\u00a0 OU Law delivers exceptional value through academic rigor, affordability, and outstanding student outcomes. OU Law sits on the university's main campus in Norman, a college town alive with entertainment, arts, food, and sports.\u00a0 A perennial \"best place to live,\" Norman has excellent public schools and low cost-of-living.\u00a0 Neighboring Oklahoma City features a dynamic economy, outstanding cultural venues, and a major airport.\u00a0 For additional information regarding the university,\u00a0 visit: http://www.ou.edu/facultyrecruitment Applicant Requirements 1.\u00a0 \u00a0 A J.D. or equivalent academic degree 2.\u00a0 \u00a0 Strong academic credentials 3.\u00a0 \u00a0 A commitment to excellence in teaching and demonstrably outstanding potential for scholarship All applicants must submit their application materials (CV and job-talk paper) via Interfolio, https://apply.interfolio.com/189024 .\u00a0 A cover letter is optional.\u00a0 If one is selected for an interview, teaching evaluations will be requested of those candidates with teaching experience.\u00a0 Review of applications will begin immediately, and the positions will remain open until filled.\u00a0 Inquires (but not application materials) may be sent directly to the chair of the Faculty Appointments Committee, Jon Lee:\u00a0 jon.lee@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189135": {"title": "Professor of Law", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: College of Law Location: University of Oklahoma, Norman Campus The College of Law at the University of Oklahoma seeks to fill three tenured faculty positions beginning in the 2027-2028 academic year. The primary subject matter areas for these hires are Constitutional Law, Criminal Law, Professional responsibility, Federal Indian Law, and Torts.\u00a0 The college also has curricular needs in Remedies, Property, Evidence, Wills and Trusts, Commercial Law, and related areas.\u00a0 We anticipate hiring across these areas based on candidate quality, curricular need, and alignment with available funding opportunities. As part of this hiring cycle, the College may make one or more endowed-chair appointments, depending on the candidate pool and fit.\u00a0 These opportunities include the Calvert Chair, for a scholar whose work focuses on law and liberty, and the Chickasaw Chair in Native American Law, for a scholar with expertise in Federal Indian Law, Tribal Law, Native American Law, or related fields.\u00a0 Candidates whose scholarly and teaching interests align with either endowed opportunity are encouraged to identify that alignment in their application materials. OU Law's strong national reputation is buttressed by a commitment to attracting and supporting excellent faculty with summer research grants, publication placement bonuses, course reductions based on scholarly productivity, and an extraordinary number of endowed positions. OU Law is committed to excellence in educating legal professionals, advancing legal scholarship, and serving the public.\u00a0 OU Law delivers exceptional value through academic rigor, affordability, and outstanding student outcomes. OU Law sits on the university's main campus in Norman, a college town alive with entertainment, arts, food, and sports.\u00a0 A perennial \"best place to live,\" Norman has excellent public schools and low cost-of living.\u00a0 Neighboring Oklahoma City features a dynamic economy, outstanding cultural venues, and a major airport.\u00a0 For additional information regarding the university, visit: https://www.ou.edu/facultyrecruitment Must have a J.D. or equivalent academic degree. Must have strong academic credentials. Must have a commitment to excellence in teaching and scholarship. To apply, please submit a CV to https://apply.interfolio.com/189135 .\u00a0\u00a0A cover letter is optional. If selected for an interview, teaching evaluations will be requested. Review of applications will begin immediately, and the positions will remain open until filled. Inquiries (but not application materials) may be sent directly to the chair of the Faculty Appointments Committee, Jon Lee: Jon.lee@ou.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189963": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Law - Clinical Legal Education - Criminal Defense Clinic", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: College of Law Location: College of Law The University of Oklahoma College of Law seeks a ranked, renewable-term faculty member to teach and supervise students in its Criminal Defense Clinic beginning in the 2027-2028 academic year.\u00a0 This is a non-tenure-track position with a nine-month academic year appointment. The successful candidate will provide intensive, practice-based instruction and supervision as students represent clients in criminal matters.\u00a0 Responsibilities include teaching the classroom component of the clinic; supervising students through all stages of client representation; ensuring compliance with applicable court rules and professional standards; and participating in the intellectual and service life of the College of Law. Ranked, renewable-term faculty members at OU Law serve an initial three-year probationary period under a series of one-year appointments, each covering a nine-month academic-year service period.\u00a0 Before the end of the third year, the faculty member may be considered for appointment to a five-year, presumptively renewable term. OU Law is committed to excellence in legal education, scholarship, public service, affordability, and strong student outcomes.\u00a0 The College values collaborative colleagues who are committed to students, clients, and the profession.\u00a0 The College also welcomes candidates who will contribute to curricular development, institutional initiatives, and partnerships that strengthen clinical education and service to Oklahoma communities across the state. Applicants must hold a J.D. or equivalent law degree and have substantial relevant legal practice experience.\u00a0 By the beginning of the appointment, the successful candidate must be an active member in good standing of the Oklahoma Bar and must remain authorized to practice law in Oklahoma throughout the appointment.\u00a0 The candidate must also satisfy all requirements necessary to supervise licensed legal interns and represent clinic clients in Oklahoma courts. Significant criminal defense experience is required.\u00a0 Applicants should demonstrate a commitment to excellent clinical teaching, close student supervision, ethical and client-centered representation, and service to the profession and community.\u00a0 Prior clinical teaching or experience supervising law students or junior attorneys is strongly preferred.\u00a0 Trial experience, including substantial responsibility for felony matters, is also strongly preferred. Applicants must submit a cover letter, CV, and a list of three professional references through Interfolio; https://apply.interfolio.com/189963 .\u00a0 Applicants may also submit teaching evaluations or other materials demonstrating teaching or supervisory experience. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries, but not application materials, may be directed to Jon Lee, chair of the Faculty Appointments Committee, at jon.lee@ou.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192320": {"title": "Open Rank Professor of Law - Clinical Legal Education", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: College of Law Location: College of Law The University of Oklahoma College of Law invites applications for a full-time, ranked-renewable term faculty member to teach and supervise students in the OU Legal Clinic beginning in the 2027-2028 academic year.\u00a0 This non-tenure-track position carries a nine-month academic year appointment.\u00a0 OU Law seeks to expand the scope of its clinical offerings and has identified several areas of interest, including rural access, transactional law (including small business or economic development), family law (including child advocacy), technology, juvenile justice, and immigration.\u00a0 OU Law also welcomes creative proposals for clinics in other fields and applications from exceptional candidates with demonstrated expertise in any area of legal practice. The successful candidate will provide intensive, practice-based instruction and supervision by teaching the clinic's classroom component, supervising students throughout client representation, ensuring compliance with court rules and professional standards, and contributing to the intellectual and service life of the College of Law. Ranked, renewable-term faculty members at OU Law serve an initial three-year probationary period under a series of one-year appointments, each covering a nine-month academic-year service period.\u00a0 Before the end of the third year, the faculty member may be considered for appointment to a five-year, presumptively renewable term. OU Law is committed to excellence in legal education, scholarship, public service, affordability, and strong student outcomes.\u00a0 The College values collaborative colleagues who are committed to students, clients, and the profession.\u00a0 The College also welcomes candidates who will contribute to curricular development, institutional initiatives, and partnerships that strengthen clinical education and service to communities throughout Oklahoma. Applicants must hold a J.D. or equivalent law degree and have substantial relevant legal practice experience.\u00a0 By the beginning of the appointment, the successful candidate must be an active member in good standing of the Oklahoma Bar and must remain authorized to practice law in Oklahoma throughout the appointment.\u00a0 The candidate must also satisfy all requirements necessary to supervise licensed legal interns and represent clinic clients in Oklahoma courts. Applicants should demonstrate a commitment to excellent clinical teaching,\u00a0 student supervision, ethical and client-centered representation, and service to the profession and community.\u00a0 Prior clinical teaching or experience supervising law students or junior attorneys is strongly preferred. Applicants must submit a cover letter, CV, and a list of three professional references through Interfolio; https://apply.interfolio.com/192320 .\u00a0 Applicants may also submit teaching evaluations or other materials demonstrating teaching or supervisory experience. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries, but not application materials, may be directed to Jon Lee, chair of the Faculty Appointments Committee, at jon.lee@ou.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185340": {"title": "Instructor/Lecturer in Public & Community Health Programs - Online", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Public and Community Health Program Location: Norman, OK The Public & Community Health Programs in the Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences invites applications for a renewable, 9-month Instructor/Lecturer position. This position is designed primarily to support the delivery of our fully online Bachelor of Arts in Community Health degree. There will also be the possibility of teaching in-person sections as enrollment and program needs evolve. The courses this position may be asked to teach (depending on education, experience, and expertise) include Introduction to Public Health, Introduction to Epidemiology, Health Policy, Law & Ethics, Health Data & Statistics, Health Communication, Public Healthcare Systems, Determinants of Health, and Community Health Capstone. Successful candidates will have a commitment to excellence in teaching and engagement with students. The selected applicant will be appointed as renewable term faculty. The term of the initial appointment is five years, pending annual performance review. Faculty who meet the appropriate qualification requirements are eligible to be considered for renewal of their appointment. The regular teaching load is 4 courses per semester (4:4), or equivalent as determined by programmatic need and enrollment trends. The distribution of effort for this position will be 80% teaching and 20% service. Anticipated start in January 2027. The University of Oklahoma is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement, serving the educational, cultural, economic, and healthcare needs of the state, region, and nation from three campuses: the main campus in Norman, the Health Campus in Oklahoma City, and the Schusterman Center in Tulsa. The University of Oklahoma enrolls over 30,000 students and has more than 2,700 full-time faculty members in 21 colleges. Required: Master\u2019s in Public Health (MPH) or closely related field from an accredited institution. Experience using a learning management system (e.g. Canvas) preferably in an instructional context. Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively, preferably with undergraduate student populations. Candidates should have a conferred masters or doctoral degree at the time of appointment. Preferred: Doctorate in public health or closely related field from an accredited institution. Demonstrated effectiveness in teaching online courses, particularly in asynchronous environments. Evidence of applying student engagement strategies tailored to online learners. Professional experience in public health practice, community health, or related field. Experience teaching undergraduate students. Applicants should submit: 1.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 A cover letter briefly describing your interest in the position, 2.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Current CV 3.\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Evidence of effective teaching (1-2 pages) describing your approach to teaching in different modalities, experience with online instruction, and strategies you use to support student learning and success. 4.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Evidence of quality instruction (e.g. course observations, student evaluations, letter of reference specifically addressing teaching). 5.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Names and contact information for three references (may be contacted later). Candidates may submit supplemental documents including syllabi of courses taught or summary of past teaching position or courses taught. All application materials should be submitted via https://apply.interfolio.com/185340 .\u00a0 Inquiries about the position can be addressed to Anne Pate at apate@ou.edu , Program Director for Public & Community Health Programs. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Starting date for the position will be in January 2027."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191914": {"title": "Research Scientist II of the Institute for Public Policy Research and Analysis (IPPRA)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Vice President for Research & Partnerships Location: Norman, OK This position will lead and contribute to a portfolio of externally funded, applied social science projects at the University of Oklahoma\u2019s Institute for Public Policy Research & Analysis (IPPRA). Research areas may include extreme weather and climate, water resources, energy systems, environmental policy, and other interdisciplinary challenges at the intersection of science, technology, and public policy.\u00a0 The Research Scientist will design and conduct interdisciplinary research, publish findings in peer-reviewed journals, develop competitive proposals for external funding, and collaborate with academic, government, industry, and operational partners. The successful candidate will lead research projects from conception through dissemination, producing high-quality scholarship as well as policy-relevant products that support evidence-based decision making.\u00a0 The position also involves regular engagement with local, state, and federal officials to ensure research addresses real-world challenges and informs public policy and practice in Oklahoma and across the nation. Through these activities, the Research Scientist will help translate research into actionable knowledge that strengthens communities and improves public decision making. The ideal candidate will thrive in a fast-paced, highly collaborative research environment and demonstrate the initiative, intellectual curiosity, and organizational skills needed to lead multiple research projects simultaneously. They will have a strong background in the social sciences, experience designing and conducting rigorous quantitative, qualitative, or mixed-methods research, and a demonstrated commitment to producing high-quality scholarship that informs public policy and practice. The successful candidate will have a record of publishing in peer-reviewed journals, developing competitive proposals for externally funded research, and collaborating effectively with interdisciplinary teams and external partners. Strong written and oral communication skills are essential, as is the ability to translate complex research findings into practical insights for policymakers, practitioners, and other stakeholders. Learn more about IPPRA at ou.edu/ippra. Supervision: May supervise research staff, postdoctoral researchers, and student researchers in support of sponsored research projects, as appropriate. Job Duties: Lead and manage interdisciplinary, externally funded research projects from project conception through dissemination, including study design, implementation, analysis, and reporting. Design, implement, and oversee quantitative and qualitative research activities, including surveys, interviews, focus groups, and other data collection and analysis efforts. Publish research findings in peer-reviewed journals and prepare policy reports, technical reports, briefing materials, and other products that translate research into practice Lead and contribute to the development of competitive proposals for external research funding. Build and maintain collaborative relationships with academic, government, industry, and community partners to ensure research addresses real-world policy and decision-making needs. Plan, organize, and facilitate meetings, workshops, and stakeholder engagement activities with local, state, and federal officials and other partners. Present research findings to academic, professional, and public audiences, including conferences, workshops, and stakeholder meetings. Mentor and provide research guidance to research staff, postdoctoral researchers, graduate students, and undergraduate students, as appropriate. Identify new research opportunities and contribute to the strategic growth of IPPRA\u2019s externally funded research portfolio. Develop decision-support tools, policy recommendations, and other research products that improve public policy and practice. Perform other duties as assigned to support project and Institute goals. Preferred Skills and Attributes: Experience developing competitive proposals for externally funded research. Experience working on applied research that informs public policy and decision making. Experience translating research findings into policy briefs, decision-support tools, or other products for policymakers and practitioners. Experience building and sustaining collaborative research partnerships with academic, government, industry, or nonprofit organizations. Minimum Required Education and Experience: Ph.D. in Public Policy, Political Science, Economics, Public Affairs, or a closely related social science field. Experience designing and conducting independent social science research using quantitative, qualitative, or mixed methods. Record of publishing research in peer-reviewed academic journals. Experience collaborating with interdisciplinary research teams and external partners, including government agencies, nonprofit organizations, industry, or community stakeholders. Ability to manage multiple research projects and deadlines in a collaborative research environment. Please submit (1) a cover letter describing your interest in the position and relevant qualifications, (2) a curriculum vitae, and (3) the names and contact information for three professional references via Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com/191914 and to Dr. Joseph Ripberger ( jtr@ou.edu ). The University of Oklahoma is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Protected veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194396": {"title": "Research Associate/Scientist \u2013 ART Radar Engineering", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Atmospheric and Geographic Sciences: CIWRO Location: Norman Oklahoma Position Description The Cooperative Institute for Severe and High-Impact Weather Research and Operations (CIWRO) at The University of Oklahoma is currently seeking a Research Associate or Research Scientist to advance weather radar technologies with the Advanced Radar Techniques (ART) team. The ART team is dedicated to improving meteorological products from weather radars by pioneering signal-processing and remote-sensing techniques, harnessing the potential of phased-array radar for weather observations, and transferring technology to operational systems. This collaborative work with NOAA's National Severe Storms Laboratory (NSSL) presents an exciting opportunity to shape the future of weather radar applications and contribute to cutting-edge research and development. Job Responsibilities Contribute to the design, development, and testing of signal processing algorithms that improve the quality of weather radar products Acquire the knowledge and skills necessary to support, evaluate, and update weather radar techniques Work with an interdisciplinary team of scientists and engineers to design, develop, and implement signal processing enhancements for weather radar systems Attend technical meetings, workshops, and professional conferences to present research results and interact with collaborators Provide support for regular summaries of work accomplishments through technical reports and/or peer-reviewed publications as needed (Research Scientist only) Lead research and development efforts in advancing the science and transition of next-generation weather radar technologies Qualifications We are looking for candidates who possess: Ph.D. (Research Scientist) or M.S. (Research Associate) in Electrical Engineering or a related field Expertise in signal processing theory and techniques Demonstrated proficiency in developing complex simulations using MATLAB Excellent oral and written communication skills with an ability to work both independently and cooperatively with others It is preferred that applicants demonstrate knowledge of weather radar, though specialized knowledge of remote sensing, radar systems, or atmospheric science is not required. Application Process To apply, please submit: A cover letter highlighting your interest in the position and describing how you meet the position qualifications, Your up-to-date resume/CV, and A list of three professional references. \u00b7 Send your application materials to: ciwro-careers@ou.edu . Please use the subject line: \" ATTN: ART Radar Engineering \" Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. The starting date is negotiable. The University of Oklahoma is an equal opportunity/Affirmative Action employer."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189883": {"title": "Open Rank Professor of OU Polytechnic Institute \u2013 Applied Artificial Intelligence (Ranked Renewable Term)", "description": "Posted: Jul 28, 2026 Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gallogly College of Engineering: Polytechnic Institute in Tulsa Location: OU Polytechnic Institute in Norman The University of Oklahoma (OU) has embarked on a transformational endeavor to grow the newly established OU Polytechnic Institute (OUPI) housed within the Gallogly College of Engineering. The aims are to further strengthen the educational landscape of the state, provide students with a unique educational experience that will enable them to be successful throughout their careers and respond to industry's need for a varied, well educated, highly skilled and adaptable workforce to meet current and future needs.\u00a0 This position will begin the newly announced extension of the OUPI from Tulsa at the OU-Tulsa Schusterman Center campus to include the Norman Campus . Enrollment for the inaugural class in applied artificial intelligence began this past November and classes will start in August 2025. The position is expected to be a 9-month appointment with the option for 3 additional months of support. OUPI seeks applicants for a full-time rank renewable term Assistant, Associate or Full Professor position with teaching emphasis in applied artificial intelligence, advanced computing, and related fields. Prospective applicants should be passionate about teaching and mentoring students and will be instrumental in designing and implementing new degree programs. The OUPI will offer bachelor\u2019s in Norman, bachelor\u2019s completion (junior and senior level classes) in Tulsa and graduate degree programs focused on innovation and advanced technology to meet growing workforce demands, both online and in-person in applied artificial intelligence. Threading through these areas are abilities in data science.\u00a0 The targeted start date is August 2026 or January 2027. The successful candidates will provide leadership in curriculum innovation, will be outstanding and effective communicators who enjoy and contribute to a shared camaraderie with faculty, staff, and students; and who show empathy in student interactions. Areas of creative activity emphasis may include but are not restricted to pedagogical methods, curriculum enhancement or design, student recruitment and retention, K-12 outreach, etc. Examples of creative activity output may include presentations at national or international conferences/symposia on computing and/or engineering education and/or publication in educational journals. In support of our ambitious plan for strategic research growth ( https://www.ou.edu/coe/about ), GCoE is investing in rapid growth of faculty numbers and we expect to see within the next three years total growth of nearly 70% in faculty size. GCoE currently has more than 5,000+ students and we are heavily invested in growing our graduate and undergraduate student bodies with a goal of reaching more than a 20% increase in the next six years. We seek candidates who are excited to contribute to the goals of our strategic growth and foster a welcoming environment in all aspects of faculty responsibility, including within the classroom and the academic community, and within their professional commitments. \u00a0Information about OUPI is available at https://www.ou.edu/polytechnic . OU is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement, serving the educational, cultural, economic and healthcare needs of the state, region, and nation from three campuses: the main campus in Norman, the Health Campus in Oklahoma City, and the Schusterman Center in Tulsa. OU enrolls over 30,000 students and has more than 2,700 full-time faculty members. Norman is a culturally rich and vibrant town located in the Oklahoma City metro area. With outstanding schools, amenities, and a low cost of living, Norman is a perennial contender on the \u201cBest Places to Live\u201d rankings. Visit https://www.ou.edu/facultyrecruitment for more information. OU invests in its faculty by providing support and resources through our Center for Faculty Excellence and Office of Vice President for Research and Partnerships. The GCoE supports faculty development through mentoring, new faculty orientation series, and access to research and educational support. Candidates who seek to work in a rapidly growing college, in collegial interdisciplinary and transdisciplinary groups, and in strong academic units are encouraged to apply. Strategic Plan Launched in July 2020, \u201cLead On, University\u201d defines a clear plan for the future of OU\u2019s Norman campus and comprehensive strategies to achieve it.\u00a0The plan is built upon five overarching pillars that stem from OU\u2019s core traditions of providing a world-class, affordable education; creating a welcoming place of belonging; and harnessing innovation and pathbreaking discoveries to advance society (see https://www.ou.edu/leadon.html for details). Applicants must have earned a doctoral degree in a computing-related field by the date the position starts and teaching experience at the college level in artificial intelligence, advanced computing, or a closely related field. Experience with computing student education through teaching and student mentoring is required. Evidence of scholarship in computing and/or engineering education is preferred. To hold the rank of associate or full professor, candidates must have the requisite record of professional experience, research, scholarship, teaching and mentoring\u00a0 in artificial intelligence or an advanced computing related field. Applicants are invited to submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and the contact information for at least four references. The cover letter may describe how the applicant\u2019s background and career goals will support the success of OUPI students. \u00a0Additional materials may be requested at a later date. Documents should be uploaded to https://apply.interfolio.com/189883 . Initial screening of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Inquiries may be sent to the search committee chair: Dr. John Hassell, University of Oklahoma at hassell@ou.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189884": {"title": "Open Rank Professor of OU Polytechnic Institute \u2013 Digital Manufacturing (Ranked Renewable Term)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gallogly College of Engineering: Polytechnic Institute in Tulsa Location: OU Polytechnic Institute in Norman The University of Oklahoma (OU) has embarked on a transformational endeavor to grow the newly established OU Polytechnic Institute (OUPI) housed within the Gallogly College of Engineering. The aims are to further strengthen the educational landscape of the state, provide students with a unique educational experience that will enable them to be successful throughout their careers and respond to industry's need for a varied, well educated, highly skilled and adaptable workforce to meet current and future needs.\u00a0 This position will begin the newly announced extension of the OUPI from Tulsa at the OU-Tulsa Schusterman Center campus to include the Norman Campus . Enrollment for the inaugural class in digital manufacturing will begin in August 2026 and classes will start in August 2027 in Norman. The position is expected to be a 9-month appointment with the option for 3 additional months of support. OUPI seeks applicants for a full-time rank renewable term Assistant, Associate or Full Professor position with teaching emphasis in digital manufacturing, advanced computing, and related fields. Prospective applicants should be passionate about teaching and mentoring students and will be instrumental in designing and implementing new degree programs. The OUPI will offer bachelor\u2019s in Norman, bachelor\u2019s completion (junior and senior level classes) in Tulsa and graduate degree programs focused on innovation and advanced technology to meet growing workforce demands in areas including cybersecurity, cybersecurity leadership, software development and integration, applied artificial intelligence, healthcare information systems, and digital manufacturing. Threading through these areas are abilities in data science.\u00a0 The targeted start date is August 2026 or January 2027. The successful candidates will provide leadership in curriculum innovation, will be outstanding and effective communicators who enjoy and contribute to a shared camaraderie with faculty, staff, and students; and who show empathy in student interactions. Areas of creative activity emphasis may include but are not restricted to pedagogical methods, curriculum enhancement or design, student recruitment and retention, K-12 outreach, etc. Examples of creative activity output may include presentations at national or international conferences/symposia on computing and/or engineering education and/or publication in educational journals. In support of our ambitious plan for strategic research growth ( https://www.ou.edu/coe/about ), GCoE is investing in rapid growth of faculty numbers and we expect to see within the next three years total growth of nearly 70% in faculty size. GCoE currently has more than 5,000+ students and we are heavily invested in growing our graduate and undergraduate student bodies with a goal of reaching more than a 20% increase in the next six years. We seek candidates who are excited to contribute to the goals of our strategic growth and foster a welcoming environment in all aspects of faculty responsibility, including within the classroom and the academic community, and within their professional commitments. \u00a0Information about OUPI is available at https://www.ou.edu/polytechnic . OU is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement, serving the educational, cultural, economic and healthcare needs of the state, region, and nation from three campuses: the main campus in Norman, the Health Campus in Oklahoma City, and the Schusterman Center in Tulsa. OU enrolls over 30,000 students and has more than 2,700 full-time faculty members. Norman is a culturally rich and vibrant town located in the Oklahoma City metro area. With outstanding schools, amenities, and a low cost of living, Norman is a perennial contender on the \u201cBest Places to Live\u201d rankings. Visit https://www.ou.edu/facultyrecruitment for more information. OU invests in its faculty by providing support and resources through our Center for Faculty Excellence and Office of Vice President for Research and Partnerships. The GCoE supports faculty development through mentoring, new faculty orientation series, and access to research and educational support. Candidates who seek to work in a rapidly growing college, in collegial interdisciplinary and transdisciplinary groups, and in strong academic units are encouraged to apply. Strategic Plan Launched in July 2020, \u201cLead On, University\u201d defines a clear plan for the future of OU\u2019s Norman campus and comprehensive strategies to achieve it.\u00a0The plan is built upon five overarching pillars that stem from OU\u2019s core traditions of providing a world-class, affordable education; creating a welcoming place of belonging; and harnessing innovation and pathbreaking discoveries to advance society (see https://www.ou.edu/leadon.html for details). Applicants must have earned a doctoral degree in a computing-related field by the date the position starts and teaching experience at the college level in digital manufacturing, advanced computing, or a closely related field. Experience with computing student education through teaching and student mentoring is required. Evidence of scholarship in computing and/or engineering education is preferred. To hold the rank of associate or full professor, candidates must have the requisite record of professional experience, research, scholarship, teaching and mentoring in artificial intelligence or an advanced computing related field. Applicants are invited to submit a cover letter, current C.V., a statement that describes how their background and career goals will support the success of OUPI students, and the contact information for at least four references. Additional materials may be requested at a later date. Documents should be uploaded to https://apply.interfolio.com/189884 . Initial screening of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Inquiries may be sent to the search committee chair: Dr. John Hassell, University of Oklahoma at hassell@ou.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185431": {"title": "Instructor or Lecturer - First Year Experience - Gateway to the Health Disciplines - RT", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: First-Year Experience Location: Norman, Oklahoma Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for a renewable term, 9 month faculty member for the general education course \u201cGateway to Health Disciplines\u201d. This course is part of a suite of courses that meet the General Education Core V requirement: First Year Experience. A first-year experience course is required for all incoming first-year and transfer students at the University of Oklahoma. Gateway to the Health Disciplines (CAS 1583) introduces students to the complex and collaborative world of health, helping them explore the wide variety of roles that contribute to patient and community health, from clinical professionals and healthcare administrators to public health experts. The course is organized around three interconnected modules: The Patient Journey, The Cost of Health, and The Promotion of Health and Prevention of Disease. This design provides students with a systems-level understanding of how the U.S. health ecosystem functions. Students will examine how a wide range of factors shape health outcomes and will engage with real-world challenges through case studies, data interpretation, and structured reflection activities. Throughout the semester, students will also develop essential professional skills, including critical thinking, evidence-based problem solving, and both oral and written communication, preparing them for the collaborative realities of a career in the health disciplines. Successful candidates for this position will have a commitment to excellence in teaching and engagement with students. The selected applicant will be appointed as renewable term faculty. The term of the initial appointment is five years, pending annual performance review. Faculty who meet the appropriate qualification requirements are eligible to be considered for renewal of their appointment. The regular teaching load is 4 courses per semester (4:4), or equivalent as determined by programmatic need and enrollment trends. The distribution of effort for this position will be 80% teaching and 20% service. Anticipated start in August 2026. The University of Oklahoma is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement, serving the educational, cultural, economic, and healthcare needs of the state, region, and nation from three campuses: the main campus in Norman, the Health Sciences Center in Oklahoma City, and the Schusterman Center in Tulsa. The University of Oklahoma enrolls over 30,000 students and has more than 2,700 full-time faculty members in 21 colleges. Required: Masters or Doctoral Degree in a health-related field (e.g. MPH, MHA, MA, MS, PhD, DrPH) or closely related field from an accredited institution. Experience using a learning management system (e.g. Canvas) preferably in an instructional context. Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively, preferably with undergraduate student populations. Candidates should have a conferred masters or doctoral degree at the time of appointment. Preferred: Demonstrated effectiveness in cultivating enriching discussions and spurring critical thinking among students Demonstrated effectiveness in engaging first-year and transfer students acclimating to collegiate standards. Documented effectiveness in teaching at the post-secondary level. Applicants should submit: a cover letter briefly describing your interest in the position, current CV, names and contact information for three confidential references. evidence of quality instruction (e.g., course observations, student evaluations, letter of reference regarding teaching) Candidates can submit supplemental documents including past syllabi of courses taught or a summary of past teaching positions and courses taught. Candidates should have a conferred masters or doctoral degree at the time of appointment. All application materials should be submitted via Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com/185431 . Inquiries about the position can be addressed to Anne Pate at apate@ou.edu , course coordinator of the Gateway to the Social Sciences course. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Starting date for the position will be in August 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/179516": {"title": "Research Scientist - School of Biological Sciences", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Biological Sciences Location: University of Oklahoma, Norman Campus The University of Oklahoma (OU), Norman campus, seeks to fill multiple full-time Research Scientist positions (3) in Artificial Intelligence, Quantitative Ecology, Computational Biology, or Microbial Ecology and Evolution to support research and educational programs in Environmental Microbiome/Genomics/Ecology, at the Institute for Environmental Genomics (IEG, https://www.ou.edu/ieg). The research areas of type A positions include one or more of the following topics: (i) development and application of AI-based algorithms/tools for microbial and ecosystem ecology research, particularly deep learning or large language models; (ii) development and application of novel mathematical/computational algorithms, biogeochemical models, or other mathematical frameworks enhanced by big microbiome data analysis; (iii) design and develop computational tools or pipelines for mathematical/statistical analysis of microbiome data (sequencing and gene chip data). Other responsibilities include: (i) managing different servers, pipelines, and cloud computing systems; (ii) coordinating the efforts for applying supercomputing to microbiome studies; and (iii) training postdocs and graduate students for AI and other advanced computational and statistical approaches. The research areas of type B positions include one or more of the following topics: (i) microbial and plant-microbe interactions and soil health; (ii) microbial evolution and its roles in community dynamics and ecosystem functioning; (iii) synthetic communities for theoretical and applied microbiome research. (iv) development and application of cutting-edge molecular technologies (e.g., long-read sequencing, CRISPR-CAS system, functional gene microarray, etc.) for microbial ecology research. Other responsibilities include: (i) managing field and laboratory experiment systems; (ii) coordinating the efforts for field studies; and (iii) training postdocs and graduate students. Opportunities exist for the scientists to participate in grant proposal development as a PI or Co-PI. The scientists can also hold adjunct faculty positions within relevant academic departments, such as the School of Biological Sciences or the School of Computer Science. Review of applications will begin on January 15, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. A near-term start date is desired. Education Competitive applicants should have a Ph.D. in Computer Science, Mathematics, Statistics, Physics, Bioinformatics, Biological or Environmental Engineering, Ecology, Microbiology, Soil Science, Biochemistry, or other relevant disciplines. Skill The candidate should have: \u00b7 Substantial knowledge/experience in Artificial Intelligence, Quantitative Ecology, Microbial Ecology and Evolution, Metagenomics, Applied Mathematics in Dynamical Systems, or related disciplines; \u00b7 For type A positions: o Strong research experience and skills in various aspects of computing, including but not limited to the following: algorithm development, predictive model design and implementation, data mining and machine learning, big data processing and management, parallel programming, computational tool/pipeline development, supercomputing, and/or cloud computing. o Experience in microbiome and system biology studies related to bioenergy, climate change, environment pollution, or human health would be desired; \u00b7 For type B positions: o Strong research experience and skills in molecular biology, ecosystem ecology, (meta)genomics, soil science, bioinformatics, or microbial ecology and evolution. o General programming or molecular experiment skills are required. \u00b7 For both types: o Excellent oral and written communication skills and a demonstrated ability to interact and collaborate with team members and students; o Experience in proposal development, although not required. Interested applicants should prepare the following documents: \u00b7 A letter of application \u00b7 A current curriculum vitae \u00b7 A description of research interests and accomplishments \u00b7 The names and contact information for at least three references ALL APPLICANTS MUST APPLY ONLINE. Inquiries should be directed to: Dr. Jizhong (Joe) Zhou (jzhou@ou.edu)\u00a0 or Daliang Ning (ningdaliang@ou.edu). Salary will be commensurate with experience, expertise and skills. Further information about the institute can be found on the IEG website: http://ieg.ou.edu/"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188995": {"title": "Associate / Full Professor of Biological Sciences - Microbiome Research - George Lynn Cross Endowed Chair", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Biological Sciences Location: University of Oklahoma-Norman Campus Description: The School of Biological Sciences (SBS) at the University of Oklahoma (OU) invites application for a full-time 9-month tenured or tenure-track position Associate or Full Professor with a start date of August 2027, and will maintain a 1:0 teaching load as a condition of being appointed to the endowed chair position. We seek an innovative, creative, and collaborative colleague with a record of leading scholarship in microbiomes who will bring a highly productive, cross-disciplinary, and externally funded program of scholarship to OU. The ideal candidate will have demonstrated success in using a combination of empirical and computational approaches to problems in metabolic processes. Areas of interest include regulation of inflammation, bacterial pathogenesis, and microbiome-immune system interactions. Further, the successful candidate will have: (1) a record of forming and leading productive interdisciplinary or transdisciplinary teams that work on advancing fundamental knowledge of the role of microbiomes in disease ; (2) successfully built collaborations within and outside their institution; and (3) mentored research trainees and taught courses related to the microbiome. The SBS has integrated research strengths in One Health, Behavior, Ecology, and Evolution and is growing in alignment with the Lead On Strategic Plan ; particularly in areas that complement strengths of the OU Health Campus. OU invests in its faculty by providing support and resources through the Center for Faculty Excellence and Vice President for Research and Partnerships Office . The Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences supports faculty development through mentoring, new faculty orientation series, and access to research and educational support. We encourage candidates to apply who are seeking to work in a rapidly growing college with collegial interdisciplinary groups and strong academic units. University of Oklahoma The University of Oklahoma (OU) is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement, serving the educational, cultural, economic, and healthcare needs of the state, region, and nation from three campuses: Norman, Health Campus in Oklahoma City and the Schusterman Center in Tulsa. OU enrolls over 32,000 students and has more than 2,700 full-time faculty members in 21 colleges. The Norman Community Norman is a vibrant university town of more than 130,000 inhabitants with a growing entertainment and art scene. With outstanding schools, amenities, and a low cost of living, Norman is a perennial contender in \u201cbest place to live\u201d rankings. Visit http://soonerway.ou.edu for more information. Within an easy commute, Oklahoma City features a dynamic economy and outstanding cultural venues adding to the region\u2019s growing appeal. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Microbiology or a related discipline. Established record of forming and leading research teams. Sustained record of high-quality research and publications on microbiomes. Demonstrated record of sustained nationally competitive extramural funding for research on microbiomes. Record of scholarship sufficient for appointment as an Associate or Full Professor. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated record of excellence in teaching. Familiarity with effective teaching practices and mentoring approaches that support students from a wide range of backgrounds. Evidence of ability to build partnerships within and across institutions. Demonstrated experience in convergent and interdisciplinary or transdisciplinary approaches to scholarship. Applicants should submit 1) a cover letter describing their interest in the position and their fit in the School of Biological Sciences; 2) a full curriculum vitae; 3) a research portfolio describing your current and future scholarship, consisting of up to a three-page narrative and up to three representative publications;\u00a0 4) a teaching portfolio detailing your experience and approach to classroom teaching and student mentorship, including your approach to classroom teaching and advising undergraduate and graduate students, with up to a two page narrative and optional syllabus for a course you are interested in teaching; 5) names and contact information for three confidential letters of recommendation.; 6)Additional materials may be requested at a later date. Applications must be submitted online via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/188995 The search committee will begin reviewing applications on September 15th, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Inquiries should be directed to: Dr. Jeff Kelly, Director, School of Biological Sciences University of Oklahoma jkelly@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190694": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Grassland Ecosystem Modeling and Carbon Cycle Forecasting", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Biological Sciences Location: Institute for Environmental Genomics, School of Biological Sciences, University of Oklahoma, Norman Campus The Institute for Environmental Genomics (IEG) and the School of Biological Sciences at the University of Oklahoma invite applications for a Postdoctoral Research Associate position in grassland ecosystem modeling, soil carbon dynamics, and ecological forecasting. The successful candidate will contribute to a funded project focused on improving prognostic ecosystem models and generating high-resolution data products of carbon fluxes and carbon stocks in grassland ecosystems. The position is closely aligned with the Center for Grassland Ecosystem Forecasting, which aims to integrate field observations, remote sensing, diagnotic and prognostic models, AI/ML approaches, digital twins, and decision-support tools to improve grassland management and conservation in Oklahoma and beyond. The postdoc will focus on using and improve the Microbial-Enzyme Decomposition (MEND) model, which explicitly represents mircrobial processes and predicts plant growth and soil carbon dynamics. MEND includes soil organic matter partitioning, microbial physiological states, enzymatic decomposition, coupled carbon-nitrogen processes, inorganic nitrogen dynamics, the use of metagenomic data, and canopy photosynthesis as a dynamic carbon input to soil. Major Responsibilties Develop, calibrate, and evaluate ecosystem models, especially MEND, for grassland carbon-cycle simulations. Use long-term experimental and observational data from Oklahoma grassland sites, including KAEFS and eddy covariance tower sites, to improve model parameterization and evaluate model performance. Assimilate data products from diagnostic models and remote sensing into prognostic model simulations. Generate geospatial data products of plant biomass, root biomass, and soil organic carbon stocks at high spatial and temporal resolution. Conduct scenario simulations to evaluate grassland carbon responses to climate variability, extreme events, altered precipitation intensity and frequency, and land-use change. Work collaboratively with an interdisciplinary team in ecosystem ecology, microbial ecology, remote sensing, AI/ML, biogeochemistry, and environmental modeling. Lead and contribute to peer-reviewed publications, presentations, data products, and proposal development. Required Qualifications A Ph.D. in ecosystem ecology, Earth system science, biogeochemistry, soil science, environmental science, applied mathematics, computer science, or a closely related field. Strong background in ecosystem modeling, terrestrial carbon-cycle modeling, soil carbon modeling, biogeochemical modeling, ecological forecasting, statistics, or machine learning. Demonstrated programming skills in Fortran, Python, R, Julia, MATLAB, or similar languages. Strong quantitative, writing, and presentation skills. A strong publication record or clear evidence of potential for peer-reviewed publications. Preferred Qualifications Preferred candidates will have in one or more of the following areas: Process-based ecosystem modeling, especially microbial-explicit soil carbon models. Model-data integration, data assimilation, parameter estimation, uncertainty analysis, or Bayesian/statistical modeling. Grassland ecology, soil carbon dynamics, plant-soil-microbe interactions, or carbon-nitrogen cycling. Remote sensing data products, geospatial analysis, Google Earth Engine, or GIS. High-performance computing, reproducible workflows, or development of open-source modeling pipelines. Experience working with eddy covariance data, long-term ecological experiments, or large environmental datasets. Upload CV/Resume Upload Cover Letter"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187022": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor in Aviation (Tenure-Track)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Atmospheric and Geographic Sciences: School of Aviation Location: School of Aviation The School of Aviation ( https://www.ou.edu/ags/aviation ) at the University of Oklahoma is inviting applications for a tenure-track Assistant/Associate Professor position. The school is housed within the College of Atmospheric and Geographic Sciences (AGS). In addition to the School of Aviation, the College comprises the Department of Geography and Environmental Sustainability and the School of Meteorology. \u00a0Together, these units form one of the most comprehensive and impactful colleges of their kind in the world. Each unit contributes distinct strengths, and collectively they deliver innovative, industry-aligned degree programs, cutting-edge research, and meaningful public service that address critical state, national, and global needs. Enrollment in AGS has grown by more than 70% over the past decade, reaching approximately 1,250 undergraduate and graduate students in fall 2025. We attract students from across the nation and around the world, 62% come from out of state, and 18% identify as first-generation college students. The School of Aviation is the main driver for this growth in enrollment. Students choose our programs for their academic excellence, hands-on learning environment, strong career outcomes, and mission-driven culture. We seek an innovative scholar committed to advancing education and research in aviation to support the OU School of Aviation's expansion, which is facilitated by major investments in a new fleet of aircraft and buildings. The ideal candidate will have expertise and demonstrated scholarly attainment in one or more of the areas listed in the qualifications. The teaching load will be 2 plus 2. The Committee Chair for this search is Eric Wydra, director of the School of Aviation. The applicant must show academic and/or employment experience in one or more areas. Unmanned Aerial Systems Human Factors Aviation/Aerospace Security Aviation/Aerospace Safety Aviation Psychology Business Program Management (eg. FAA, ICAO, DoD, NASA) Applied Educational Studies (Aviation) Airplane Pilot/Instructor Air Traffic Control Airport Management Prior experience as a pilot instructor, corporate pilot, or commercial aircrew is valued. Successful applicants will possess a terminal degree in a related field. Applicants who will soon complete a terminal degree will also be considered. Applicants should submit 1) a cover letter describing their interest in and highlighting their qualifications for the position, 2) a full curriculum vitae, 3) a 1-2 page research plan listing accomplishments and future plans, 4) a 1-2 page teaching plan listing teaching experience and future interests, 5) a statement about their prior and planned contributions to professional service (e.g. through mentoring roles or involvement in committees), and 6) contact information (email and phone numbers) for three people who will be asked to provide confidential letters of recommendation. Please combine all these materials into one file and submit via Interfolio ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195660": {"title": "Director of the School of Aviation", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Atmospheric and Geographic Sciences: School of Aviation Location: Norman Campus The School of Aviation at the University of Oklahoma (OU) invites applications for its next Director, with a tenured faculty appointment at the rank of associate professor or professor. This is a 12-month appointment in the College of Atmospheric and Geographic Sciences. The School seeks a leader who will advance aviation education and research, support faculty and staff, and prepare students for careers across the aviation industry. The School offers five Bachelor of Science concentrations: professional pilot\u2013airplane, professional pilot\u2013helicopter, aviation management\u2013flying, air traffic management, and aviation management\u2013non-flying. These programs combine academic study with applied learning to prepare students for professional practice and leadership. Airplane flight training is provided through the School\u2019s in-house FAA Part 141 flight school, which holds examining authority. Helicopter flight training is provided by a contracted third-party operator located at the airport. The aviation management\u2013flying concentration uses OU\u2019s fixed-wing training fleet. All aircraft in OU\u2019s training fleet are owned by the University and maintained by FAA-certified airframe and powerplant technicians who are full-time University employees. The professional pilot\u2013airplane, air traffic management, aviation management\u2013flying, and aviation management\u2013non-flying concentrations have been continuously accredited by the Aviation Accreditation Board International (AABI) since 2005. The School\u2019s air traffic management program is an FAA-approved Enhanced Air Traffic\u2013Collegiate Training Initiative (Enhanced AT-CTI) program, enabling qualified graduates to proceed directly to on-the-job training at an air traffic control facility. The School is also developing master\u2019s and PhD degree programs, with launches anticipated soon, creating new opportunities for graduate education and aviation research. As part of the College of Atmospheric and Geographic Sciences, the School offers opportunities to connect aviation education and research with the College\u2019s broader academic strengths. The Director will work with the Dean, faculty, staff, and students to develop and implement a shared vision for the School\u2019s future. Responsibilities include academic leadership, strategic planning, budget and personnel management, and support for teaching, scholarship, and student success. The Director will promote a strong culture of safety and professionalism, support the quality of academic, flight training, and air traffic control (ATC) simulation programs, and build relationships with alumni, industry, government, and other partners. Working closely with the Dean, the Director will help expand external support and identify opportunities for collaboration and program development. Candidates must hold a doctorate or a master\u2019s degree with documented leadership experience in academia, industry, or military organizations. Candidates must have an academic record that warrants appointment as a tenured associate professor or professor. Ideal candidates will have a strong record of teaching and scholarly achievement, demonstrated leadership and administrative skills, and the ability to communicate and collaborate effectively with faculty, staff, students, university administrators, alumni, and external partners. Preferred qualifications include an FAA pilot certificate or FAA controller credentials; significant experience in the aviation industry, aviation administration, military aviation, or aviation research; or a combination of these credentials and experiences. The anticipated start date is July 1, 2027. Applications should include a letter of interest describing the applicant\u2019s views on teaching, research, leadership, and vision for the School of Aviation; a complete curriculum vitae; and the names and contact information of at least three references. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026, and applications will be considered until the position is filled. Applications should be submitted through Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/195660 . Please direct questions to the search committee chair, Dean Robert Palmer ( rpalmer@ou.edu ), or his assistant, Jo Ann Mehl ( jmehl@ou.edu ). Additional information is available at www.ou.edu (University of Oklahoma), www.ou.edu/ags (College of Atmospheric and Geographic Sciences), and www.ou.edu/ags/aviation (School of Aviation)."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192490": {"title": "Open Rank Professor of International Area Studies\u2013 Endowed ConocoPhillips Professorship of Chinese and Asian Studies", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: David L. Boren School of International Studies Location: Norman, OK  The David L. Boren School of International Studies at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for a position holding the ConocoPhillips Endowed Professorship of\u00a0Chinese and Asian Studies, a full-time, tenure-track or tenured position at the rank of Assistant, Associate or Full Professor, beginning August 15, 2027. We seek a scholar whose research and teaching focus on Chinese politics/political economy, Chinese foreign policy, and/or China\u2019s role in international affairs. We are particularly interested in candidates whose work engages questions across Chinese international relations, including areas such as technology and national security, supply chains and strategic resources, state-market relations, and geopolitical competition in East Asia. Teaching load of 2:1 for the duration of the endowed appointment, with a department standard of 2:2. The position will strengthen the Boren School\u2019s longstanding commitment to the study of China and U.S.-China relations. The successful candidate will contribute to undergraduate and graduate teaching in the Boren School with courses that will include Chinese foreign policy, Chinese politics, Chinese political economy, U.S.-China relations, and the international relations of East Asia, as well as thematic courses dealing with technology, supply chains, strategic resources, and/or relations between states and markets. The successful candidate will also have opportunities to participate in the University of Oklahoma\u2019s broader China-related research community, including the Institute for U.S.-China Issues. Depending on expertise and interest, the faculty member may contribute to the Institute\u2019s programming, including leadership of a China International Relations/Policy/Politics Lecture Series, helping connect scholarship on contemporary China with students, faculty, policymakers, and the wider public. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Political Science, International Relations, or a closely related field by the time of appointment and demonstrate the potential for an excellent record of research, publication, and teaching. We welcome methodological and theoretical diversity and particularly encourage applicants whose research contributes to broader debates in international relations, comparative politics, or political economy while maintaining substantive expertise on China. The David L. Boren School of International Studies is housed within the University of Oklahoma\u2019s Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences and offers undergraduate and graduate programs spanning International Studies and Area Studies. The School provides an interdisciplinary environment for scholarship and teaching on global politics, security, development, political economy, languages, cultures, and world regions. Required: Ph.D. in Political Science or International Relations or expected by time of appointment. Demonstrated ability to conduct high-quality China related international relations \u00a0or comparative politics research. Demonstrated ability or potential to teach courses in Chinese Foreign Policy, \u00a0International Relations of East Asia and Chinese Politics. Preferred (Not Required): One or more publications in relevant peer-reviewed outlets and additional ongoing research in international relations or comparative politics. Research expertise in China\u2019s engagement with the world and/or Chinese grand strategy. Demonstrated ability or potential to generate external research funding. Demonstrated ability or potential to teach courses on international security and emerging technologies. Excellence in teaching and in supporting student success. Applicants are invited to submit a cover letter, current C.V., writing sample, and contact information for three confidential letters of recommendation. The cover letter may address the candidate\u2019s overarching research agenda as well as their approach to and excellence in teaching, service, and student mentorship. Additional materials may be requested at a later date. The search will remain open until filled. Submit applications to: https://apply.interfolio.com/192490 The search committee will begin reviewing applications on October 5, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Questions about the position may be directed to Dr. Mark Raymond, Wick Cary Associate Professor of International Relations and Chair of the Search Committee, at mraymond@ou.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193556": {"title": "Instructor or Lecturer of Business Communication (RT)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Michael F. Price College of Business: Division of Entrepreneurship  and Innovation: Business Communications Location: Norman, Oklahoma The Tom Love Division of Entrepreneurship and Innovation at the University of Oklahoma, Michael F. Price College of Business, is pleased to announce that we are accepting applications for a renewable-term instructor or lecturer in Strategic Communication for Business Professionals. Ideal candidates will have an outstanding teaching record, collaborate well with colleagues, and be invested in student success. Ideal candidates will also have a background in and/or experience with business writing, public speaking, and applying concepts to business contexts. Applicants should be committed to excellence in teaching, curriculum improvement, department service, collegiality, and fulfilling the mission of Price College ( https://www.ou.edu/price ). The Price College of Business at the University of Oklahoma (OU) continues to flourish in a period of growth and expansion. The Price College of Business is AACSB-accredited, with approximately 6,000 undergraduate, 700 master\u2019s, and 40 doctoral students OU is a Carnegie R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement, serving the educational, cultural, economic, and healthcare needs of the state, region, and nation from three campuses: Norman, the Health Sciences Center in Oklahoma City, and the Schusterman Center in Tulsa. OU enrolls over 30,000 students and has more than 2,600 full-time faculty members in 20 colleges.\u00a0 The primary campus is in Norman, a culturally rich, vibrant town just outside Oklahoma City and less than three hours from Dallas/Ft. Worth. With outstanding schools, extensive community activities, nearby urban amenities, and a low cost of living, Norman is a perennial contender in \u201cBest Places to Live\u201d rankings. \u00a0For more information about OU or life in Norman, see: https://ou.edu/facultyrecruitment . Earned master's degree, doctoral degree preferred, in Communication, English, Journalism, or related field Exceptional teaching experience and evidence of department and university service Broad experience with presentations, professional writing, and the ability to apply these skills in business contexts To apply, please upload a cover letter, current vita, names, contact information for three references, and a teaching portfolio to Interfolio .\u00a0 Your teaching portfolio could include (1) a teaching statement, (2) a list of courses taught, (3) evidence of teaching effectiveness (student evaluations, peer observations, teaching awards, etc.), (4) course materials, (5) professional development in teaching, etc. For additional information, please contact Kylie Harrison ( kharrison@ou.edu )."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194302": {"title": "Research Scientist or Associate - Adaptation Specialist", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Atmospheric and Geographic Sciences: South Central CASC Location: Norman, OK The South Central Climate Adaptation Science Center (CASC) at the University of Oklahoma seeks to hire a Research Associate or Research Scientist to join an interdisciplinary team focused on reducing risk to drought, flood, wildfire, and other climate extremes across the CASC\u2019s four-state region (LA, NM, OK, TX). The South Central CASC is a boundary organization, and this position will serve as one of our primary boundary spanners to connect with key partners in the region. This position will provide technical assistance for natural resource management for a variety of federal, state, and tribal agencies, nongovernmental organizations (NGOs), and other resource management partners. The Adaptation Specialist will be an employee of the University of Oklahoma based in Norman, OK. Moderate travel within the South Central CASC region is expected to meet with partners and continue building relationships with resource managers in the region. Job Responsibilities The selected candidate will play a leading role in synthesizing climate adaptation research and combining the information with climate-related datasets, tools, or other relevant products for resource managers. Specific duties are listed below: - Engage with partners in actionable science and provide science-based information as they adapt to, respond to, and plan for climate extremes in their local jurisdictions - Communicate relevant scientific/technical information to a variety of audiences, including scientists and non-scientists, in a variety of venues - Collaborate with USGS, federal/state/tribal agencies, NGOs, and others to identify research and information gaps, including science information, products, datasets, decision support tools, etc. to meet partners\u2019 needs for adaptation efforts - Synthesize and disseminate the research conducted by the South Central CASC using effective software and through reports, agency science highlights, and other media in formats accessible and usable for resource managers - Attend meetings and professional conferences to build connections with key partners - Lead novel research or climate-related data analysis on impacts and adaptation for ecological systems with other research partners (Research Scientist) Qualifications (required) - Ph.D. ( for Research Scientist position) in ecology, hydrology, physical geography, climate science (broadly interpreted), environmental science, or related field, OR - Masters Degree (for Research Associate position) with at least 5 years of experience in ecology, hydrology, physical geography, climate science (broadly interpreted), environmental science, or related field - Strong data analysis skills (quantitative and/or qualitative) - Ability to effectively communicate scientific/technical information to non-expert audiences - Proficiency with computer applications, including videoconferencing (e.g., Zoom), cloud services (e.g., Google Docs), word processing, presentation software, and spreadsheets Qualifications (preferred) - Experience with statistical analysis of environmental data, geographical information science, or intermediate programming using R, Python, or similar language to process or display environmental data - Familiarity with management of climate, hydrologic, ecological, or other spatial environmental datasets - Experience working independently and as part of an interdisciplinary team - Experience working with practitioners outside academia on actionable science - Interest in applied conservation, decision support, or co-developed science - Experience writing proposals, governmental reports, or scientific summaries for decision makers - Experience in vulnerability assessments, hazard mitigation plans, or climate adaptation planning To apply, please submit the following items to Emma Kuster ( emmakuster@ou.edu ) with the subject line \u201cAdaptation Specialist Position\u201d: - a cover letter highlighting your interest in this position and how you meet the listed qualifications - your up-to-date resume/CV - a one-page statement on how you have helped or will help make science useful and useable for decision makers - contact information for 4 professional references (references will not be contacted without your permission) - a writing sample \u2013 can be a chapter of a thesis or dissertation, a published or submitted manuscript in which you are lead author, a government report in which you are lead author, a book chapter in which you are lead author, or an essay in which you are the sole author"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194216": {"title": "Research Associate / Research Scientist  at the Advanced Radar Research Center", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Vice President for Research & Partnerships: Advanced Radar Research Center Location: Norman, OK  The University of Oklahoma has an opening for a Research Associate or Research Scientist in advanced radar signal processing, electromagnetic/radio-frequency interference (EMI/RFI) mitigation, and phased array radar (PAR). Appointment level will be commensurate with education and experience: candidates with an M.S. will be considered at the Research Associate level, while candidates with a Ph.D. will be considered at the Research Scientist level. The primary focus will be development, implementation, and evaluation of signal-processing algorithms to detect, characterize, and mitigate external interference in weather-radar data. The successful candidate will work with simulated and measured complex I/Q data; develop interference detection, excision, filtering, estimation, and mitigation methods; and quantify effects on radar data quality, including reflectivity, radial velocity, spectrum width, and dual-polarization variables. The work will emphasize rigorous, reproducible evaluation over a range of interference levels, radar operating modes, and weather/ground-clutter conditions. The candidate will also contribute to phased array radar analyses relevant to spectrum-efficient weather-radar operation. Depending on background and project needs, topics may include the following: array signal processing, digital beamforming, adaptive nulling, frequency/waveform agility, spectral-footprint characterization, and trade studies involving update rate, sensitivity, interference susceptibility, and spectrum coexistence. The position will involve close collaboration with OU/ARRC radar engineers and scientists and NOAA/NWS technical partners, with access to radar testbeds, high-performance computing resources, and extensive weather-radar datasets. The successful candidate will work primarily in the Radar Innovations Laboratory and National Weather Center in Norman, Oklahoma, in a highly collaborative environment involving OU faculty, engineers, students, NOAA scientists, and other federal and industry partners. Salary will be competitive based on experience and qualifications, and OU offers competitive benefits. The initial appointment is expected to be one year, renewable for a second year subject to satisfactory performance and continued funding. Requirements: \u00b7 M.S. or Ph.D. in Electrical Engineering, Electrical and Computer Engineering, or a closely related discipline by the start date. \u00b7 Strong background in digital and/or statistical signal processing, including areas such as spectral analysis, detection and estimation, adaptive filtering, or time-series analysis. \u00b7 Programming experience for scientific computing and data analysis, preferably in MATLAB and/or Python. \u00b7 Ability to work with large complex-valued datasets and communicate technical results clearly in reports, presentations, and collaborative discussions. To apply, please submit an electronic application via Interfolio. Include (1) a cover letter describing relevant experience and interests in signal processing, EMI/RFI mitigation, radar, and/or phased arrays; (2) a current CV or resume; and (3) names and contact information for three references via Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com/194216 Questions may be directed to Dr. David Schvartzman at dschvart@ou.edu . Applications will be reviewed as received until the position is filled; full-consideration date: October 16 th , 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195561": {"title": "Open Rank Professor of History - Western American History (Tenure-Track or Tenure)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: History Location: University of Oklahoma Posted: Oct 21, 2026 The Department of History at the University of Oklahoma (OU) invites applications for an open rank tenured or tenure-track position in Western American History. The department seeks an outstanding scholar of any time period related to North American Wests. We are especially interested in scholars who specialize in Latinx history, borderlands history, and/or African-American history. The successful candidate will be expected to make a substantial contribution to the department's graduate program in Western American history by actively mentoring graduate students and should be prepared to teach upper-division and graduate courses in Western history and the undergraduate US History survey. The regular teaching load is two courses per semester. The appointment is expected to begin August 16, 2027. The University of Oklahoma is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement. OU enrolls over 32,000 students and has more than 2,700 full-time faculty members in 20 colleges. The University is home to a 277-acre Research Campus in Norman, a culturally rich and vibrant town with outstanding schools, amenities, and a low cost of living. Norman is located just south of Oklahoma City, the capital and economic hub of the state of Oklahoma, which offers all the amenities of a major metropolis. See http://soonerway.ou.edu OU houses the Western History Collection, among the most important facilities in the world for Native American, Western American, and environmental history, with 65,000 books, 10,000 cubic feet of manuscripts, and nearly 2 million photographs. The city of Norman, Oklahoma, is a perennial contender on \"best place to live\" rankings with outstanding schools, amenities, a low cost of living, small town charm, a variety of recreational opportunities, four distinct seasons, and more than 225 days of sunshine each year. Norman is located 20 miles south of Oklahoma City, the capital and economic hub of the state of Oklahoma. The Oklahoma City Metroplex, including Norman and several other suburban cities, is home to about 1.5 million residents, offering all the amenities of a major modern metropolitan community, including rich cultural heritage, an abundance of the arts, superb dining and nightlife, major sporting and concert events, family entertainment, and recreational adventure activities. Candidates will be hired at the Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor rank and should demonstrate clear evidence of high-quality scholarship through published research. To be considered for this position candidates must have a PhD in History or a related field at the time of application as well as demonstrated excellence in teaching and mentoring. To apply, please submit a curriculum vitae, a cover letter, an article or chapter-length writing sample, and contact information for three (3) confidential letters of recommendation to https://apply.interfolio.com/195561 . Review of applications will begin December 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Direct inquiries to Dr. Julie Reed, Search Committee Chair ( Julie.Reed-1@ou.edu )."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195446": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Library and Information Studies - Information Retrieval and Human Computer Interaction (Tenure-Track or Tenured)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Library and Information Studies Location: Norman, OK Posted: Oct 22, 2026 The School of Library and Information Studies (SLIS) in the Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences at the University of Oklahoma (OU) invites applications for a full-time Assistant or Associate tenured/tenure track faculty position in Information Retrieval and Human Computer Interaction . This position will begin in fall 2027. The position is located in Norman, Oklahoma, and the successful candidate will be required to move to the area. We are seeking a dynamic candidate with a demonstrated research agenda, the ability to teach courses at the undergraduate, master\u2019s, and/or doctoral levels, and a commitment to service. Most of SLIS\u2019 courses are taught in an online, asynchronous format, with the exception of our doctoral courses. This is a 9-month position with a teaching load of 2 courses in the fall semester and 2 courses in the spring semester. Faculty distribution of effort is typically 40% teaching, 40% research, and 20% service, and faculty members are expected to contribute to all three categories. Candidates must specialize in one or more of the following with clear application within library and/or information science: information retrieval, data and text analytics, generative AI and large language models, natural language processing and computational linguistics, human-AI interaction, AI ethics and societal impacts, AI policy and law, and responsible AI, human computer interaction (HCI). Courses taught by this faculty member could include topics such as: fundamentals of information technology, information retrieval and text mining, information visualization, and social/community informatics.\u00a0The faculty member will collaborate with other faculty to expand SLIS\u2019 course offerings focused on artificial intelligence, human computer interaction, user-centered design, information retrieval theories (doctoral level), and\u00a0advances in information retrieval (doctoral level). Faculty responsibilities include: \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Instruction, including course development, mentoring graduate students, and supervising student independent courses, internships, and projects \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Research and publication at a level appropriate for a Carnegie R1 research university Service to the Department, College and University, and professional service at the regional, national, and international levels The School of Library and Information Studies\u2019 mission is to 1) educate socially responsible, innovative leaders for the information society; 2) advance interdisciplinary knowledge and design creative solutions to information problems; and 3) contribute to the public good by engaging communities through teaching, research, and service. SLIS has a doctoral program in information science, three master's degrees in Library and Information Studies, Museum Studies, and Applied Statistics, two undergraduate programs, and a variety of undergraduate and graduate certificates and minors. SLIS is proud of our interdisciplinary faculty who are engaging in a variety of research projects to enact change in our society! Our faculty come from a variety of backgrounds, including library and/or information science, computer science, business and management information systems, learning sciences, museum studies, psychology, and sociology. We are growing our programs and faculty, especially in the areas of AI, AI ethics, information retrieval, human computer interaction, and indigenous archival practices. To learn more about SLIS, visit our website at: https://www.ou.edu/cas/slis . Required Qualifications: \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Earned Ph.D. or equivalent in Information Science, Library and Information Science or a closely related discipline (e.g. Data Science, Information Systems, Computer Science). The candidate can be ABD at the time of application but is expected to complete their dissertation defense/thesis viva before starting the position in fall 2027. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Research specialization in information retrieval or human computer interaction with clear application within library and/or information science. Preferred Qualifications: \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Experience teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels in library and/or information science courses \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Experience teaching in an online, asynchronous format \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Demonstrated research experience through presentations and publications as commensurate to their rank \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Experience with developing grant proposals if applying for Associate Professor rank \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Engagement with departmental, college, university, or professional service if applying for Associate Professor rank Applicants are invited to submit a comprehensive cover letter addressing how their credentials make them a good fit for this faculty position. The cover letter may address the candidate\u2019s research agenda and approach to teaching and mentoring. In addition to the cover letter, applicants will submit a current C.V. and contact information for three confidential letters of recommendation. Materials should be submitted to https://apply.interfolio.com/195446 . The position will remain open until filled. Contact Person and Application Review Date: The search committee will begin reviewing applications on November 15, 2026. Inquiries should be directed to the Search Committee Chair: Dr. June Abbas, Director and Professor The University of Oklahoma. School of Library and Information Studies jmabbas@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195054": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Mathematics - Mathematics Education (Tenure-Track)", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Dodge Family College of Arts and Sciences: Mathematics Location: Norman, Oklahoma Posted: Oct 22, 2026 The David and Judi Proctor Department of Mathematics at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor, specializing in Research in Undergraduate Mathematics Education (RUME), beginning August 2027. We seek a scholar with a strong research program or demonstrated potential to develop one, focused on undergraduate mathematics education. We welcome candidates whose research advances understanding of undergraduate mathematics teaching and learning and is grounded in relevant educational theories and rigorous research methodologies. The successful candidate will be expected to establish and sustain a productive research program, disseminate research through peer-reviewed publications and professional presentations, and actively pursue appropriate opportunities for external funding. Teaching responsibilities will include two courses per semester, comprising undergraduate mathematics courses and graduate courses in mathematics education. Service to the Department, College, University, and profession is also expected at a level appropriate to the faculty member\u2019s rank and experience. The Department has a long-standing commitment to Research in Undergraduate Mathematics Education (RUME) and offers opportunities for doctoral study and research in this area. The successful candidate will be expected to mentor and direct doctoral students, contribute to the organization and leadership of the department\u2019s RUME seminars, and play an active role in the intellectual development of the RUME program. We are particularly interested in candidates whose scholarship complements or extends the research strengths of current faculty and brings new perspectives and directions to the department\u2019s RUME program. The successful candidate will have opportunities to collaborate with mathematics education researchers and mathematicians within the Department, scholars across the University of Oklahoma, and beyond. We seek a colleague who will contribute to the continued growth and development of the department\u2019s mathematics education program and enhance its national visibility. OU is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement. OU enrolls over 30,000 students and has more than 2,700 full-time faculty members across 21 colleges. Norman is a culturally rich and vibrant town located just outside Oklahoma City. With outstanding schools, amenities, and a low cost of living, Norman is a perennial contender on \u201cbest place to live\u201d lists. For more information, visit http://www.ou.edu/content/dam/provost/documents/facultyflipbook.pdf. Required: Applicants must hold an earned doctorate in Mathematics, Mathematics Education, or a closely related field by the time of appointment, with a research specialization in undergraduate mathematics education. A strong background in mathematics beyond the master\u2019s level is required. Candidates should demonstrate evidence of, or strong potential for, excellence in both research and teaching. For full consideration, applicants should send a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a description of current and planned research, a description of teaching experience and plan, and contact information for three confidential letters of recommendation addressing the candidate\u2019s research. At least one letter must address the applicant's teaching experience and proficiency. The position will remain open until filled. Applications must be submitted via Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com/195054 Mathematics Department Contact Information: Phone: 405-325-6711 E-mail: mathsearch1@ou.edu Website: https://math.ou.edu/jobs/2027/ Screening of applications will begin on November 16, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/196232": {"title": "Postdoctoral Fellow", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Atmospheric and Geographic Sciences: School of Meteorology Location: University of Oklahoma- Norman Campus Posted: Oct 23, 2026 The CHAOS (Convection and weather Hazards with Artificial intelligence, Observations, and Simulations) research group led by Dr. Aaron Hill in the School of Meteorology at the University of Oklahoma is hiring a Postdoctoral Research Fellow to develop extreme precipitation forecast models with machine learning. The postdoc will be responsible for aggregating input datasets, training machine learning models, and evaluating historical and real-time prediction performance for operational partners. The prediction system will extract prediction information from global AI weather models to produce 1\u20138 day forecasts across global areas of interest. The successful candidate will join a new research center at the University of Oklahoma focused on extreme weather resiliency. The researcher will be supervised by Dr. Aaron Hill and will collaborate with other researchers in the School of Meteorology as well as with personnel across the center. The successful candidate will work in the National Weather Center where the School of Meteorology and NOAA\u2019s National Severe Storms Laboratory reside. The National Weather Center fosters a uniquely collaborative work environment with federal, state, and academic units, providing valuable networking opportunities for career development. Salary will be competitive based on rates from a recent survey of postdoctoral fellowships in the United States and adjusted to the cost of living in Oklahoma. Full consideration will be given to applications received by November 25 th 2026, but applications may be submitted until the position is closed. Candidates in the final stages of completing their Ph.D. are also encouraged to apply. The term of appointment will be one year, renewable for a second year subject to satisfactory performance. Why You Belong at the University of Oklahoma The University of Oklahoma values our community's unique talents, perspectives, and experiences. At OU, we aspire to harness our innovation, creativity, and collaboration for the advancement of people everywhere. You Belong Here! Requirements: \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Ph.D. in Atmospheric Science, Meteorology, Computer Science,\u00a0Physics, Mathematics, or related disciplines within the last 3 years \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Experience with programming languages for AI/ML algorithm development and data analysis Desired Skills and Qualifications: \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Excellent written and oral communication skills, evidenced by peer-reviewed publications \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Strong background in at least one of the following areas: extreme weather hazards, weather forecasting, mesoscale meteorology and dynamics, operational forecast product development, transitioning products and models into operations \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Past demonstrated experience and/or strong interest in using supercomputing or cloud computing resources To apply for this position, please submit the electronic application via Interfolio:\u00a0<insert link here> and to Prof. Aaron Hill (ahill@ou.edu) and include the following: a letter of research interest and experience, CV, and three names of references (including their contact information)."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/196325": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Atmospheric and Geographic Sciences: School of Meteorology Location: University of Oklahoma- Norman Campus Posted: Oct 26, 2026 The Applied Climate Dynamics research group led by Dr. Jason Furtado in the School of Meteorology at the University of Oklahoma is looking to hire a Postdoctoral Research Fellow to develop a subseasonal prediction tool for extreme cold air outbreaks (CAOs) across Eastern Europe. The successful candidate will be responsible for: \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Defining and creating a database of past (since 1979) CAOs across Eastern Europe. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Identifying important and skillful precursor patterns in the atmosphere, ocean, and land-surface leading up to these extreme CAO events. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Building and training a subseasonal prediction tool to be used for probabilistic predictions of these events from Weeks 1-5 for operational partners. \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Evaluating the historical and real-time prediction performance of this prediction tool against existing operational subseasonal forecast models. Interested candidates should have experience with working with large datasets and numerical model output, synoptic and subseasonal forecasting, and large-scale patterns of variability. The successful candidate will join a new research center at the University of Oklahoma focused on extreme weather resiliency. The postdoctoral research fellow will be supervised by Dr. Jason Furtado and will collaborate with others in that center and at the University of Oklahoma on tasks and related research projects. The successful candidate will work in the National Weather Center in Norman, OK where the School of Meteorology and several state and federal weather agencies reside. The National Weather Center fosters a uniquely collaborative work environment with federal, state, and academic units, providing valuable networking opportunities for career development. Salary will be competitive based on rates from a recent survey of postdoctoral fellowships in the United States and adjusted to the cost of living in Oklahoma. Full consideration will be given to applications received by 23 November 2026, but applications may be submitted until the position is closed. Candidates in the final stages of completing their Ph.D. are also encouraged to apply. The term of appointment will be for one year, renewable for a second year subject to satisfactory performance. Required Qualifications \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Ph.D. in Meteorology, Atmospheric Science, Computer Science,\u00a0Physics, or related disciplines \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Experience with working with large datasets, data analysis, and machine learning \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Demonstrated proficiency in Python Desired Skills and Qualifications \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Excellent written and oral communication skills \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Knowledge of subseasonal-to-seasonal prediction and operational subseasonal models \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Experience working with operational end users in a research environment To apply for this position, please submit the following items via Interfolio: (1) a cover letter, (2) CV, (3) a statement of research interests and experience, and (4) the names of three references (including contact information). Please contact Dr. Jason Furtado (jfurtado@ou.edu) with any application questions."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195812": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Educational Psychology - Learning Experience Design and Technology", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Jeannine Rainbolt College of Education: Department of Educational Psychology Location: Norman, Oklahoma Posted: Oct 22, 2026 The Department of Educational Psychology in the Jeannine Rainbolt College of Education at the University of Oklahoma (OU) invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Learning Experience Design and Technology. The University of Oklahoma is a comprehensive flagship R1 research institution with a student body of more than 35,000. OU is committed to excellence in teaching, research, and professional service. The Department of Educational Psychology offers degree programs at the master's and doctoral levels in Learning Experience Design and Technology (LXDT); Science of Psychology, Data, and Research in Education (SPDRE); Special Education (SPED); and Professional Counseling (PC); and at the undergraduate level in Special Education with teacher certification. We seek candidates with expertise in learning / instructional design and development, with particular emphasis on human centered learning environments. The successful candidate will teach required and elective courses related to the design, development, and evaluation of learning environments for engagement via in-person, online, or blended modes; advise and mentor graduate students; direct doctoral dissertations and serve on doctoral committees; develop or continue an impactful research agenda; and provide service to the department, college, university, profession, and community. Required Qualifications Earned doctorate or completion of doctorate degree by August 1, 2027, in Learning Design and Technology (LDT) / Instructional Design and Technology (IDT), or a closely related field. Evidence of scholarly activity, such as publications in professional journals and presentations at national professional conferences. Evidence of willingness and ability to provide quality learning experiences and mentorship for graduate students. Preferred Qualifications Experience teaching core learning / instructional design and development courses. Experience teaching elective / specialty courses related to learning / instructional design and development. Experience in designing and teaching in face-to-face, online, and hybrid modes. Strong grounding in theory, including learning and instructional theories and critical theories. Knowledge of models, strategies, tools, accessibility, and socio-cultural lenses in relation to instructional / learning design and technology. Expertise in any combination of a variety of current and emerging specialization areas including, but not limited to: Learning Experience Design and Development; Instructional Multimedia Design and Development; User Experience Design and Testing; Instructional Message Design / Visuals for Learning and Instruction; Immersive / Virtual / Augmented Reality; Human-centered Artificial Intelligence in Learning and Education; Design Based Research; LDT Project Management; \u00a0\u00a0etc. Experience working with varied student populations. Demonstrated commitment to the design and development of learning / instructional environments and materials that are accessible and equitable. Strong program of scholarly inquiry related to learning ecologies, systems thinking, and community centered learning design. Evidence (or strong potential) of maintaining a rigorous research agenda. Demonstrated ability or potential to obtain external funding. Application review will begin November 23, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. The position is a 9-month, tenure-track position, expected to begin August 16, 2027. Complete Applications include: Cover Letter \u2013 which may address examples of the candidate\u2019s approach to teaching, accessibility, graduate mentoring, research, and service. Curriculum Vitae Up to 3 writing samples Unofficial graduate transcripts (official transcripts required if invited to interview) Names and contact information for three professional references. Link to digital portfolio, if available.\\ All application materials should be submitted electronically via Interfolio using the following link: https://apply.interfolio.com/195812 Questions may be directed to: Dr. Mohammad Shams Ud Duha, Search Committee Chair, Email: duha@ou.edu Kalliopi Koutsaki, Senior Administrative Assistant, Educational Psychology Department, Email: kalliopi@ou.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195324": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Biomedical Engineering", "description": "Department: University of Oklahoma Norman Campus: Gallogly College of Engineering: Stephenson School of Biomedical Engineering Location: Norman, OK Posted: Oct 22, 2026 The Stephenson School of Biomedical Engineering (SBME) in the Gallogly College of Engineering at the University of Oklahoma invites applications for a part-time (0.5FTE) ranked-renewable-term Assistant Professor position starting Spring 2026. We seek applicants with experience in teaching both undergraduate and graduate courses with experience in lab based instruction. Competitive candidates will be effective communicators who are committed to excellence in teaching, value student mentoring, and contribute positively to our collaborative community. The successful candidate will be expected to teach core courses, develop new courses in biomedical engineering, and participate in mentorship and advising of undergraduate and graduate students. As a part-time position, the position will include teaching load of three credit hours each semester, mentoring undergraduate and MS SBME students, and working to support the School in areas of needed service. SBME\u2019s mission is to educate the next generation of biomedical engineers and to create new technologies that advance human health. Founded in 2016, SBME is committed to undergraduate and graduate education, offering B.S., M.S., and Ph.D. programs in Biomedical Engineering. We moved into the new Gallogly Hall in 2019, providing state-of-the-art lab spaces for faculty and students. Our faculty of 22 works with approximately 280 undergraduates and 70 graduate students. The University of Oklahoma (OU) is a Carnegie-R1 comprehensive public research university known for excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement. Serving educational, cultural, economic, and healthcare needs from campuses in Norman, Oklahoma City, and Tulsa, OU enrolls over 30,000 students and employs more than 2,700 full-time faculty members across 20 colleges. SBME faculty and students enjoy extensive partnerships with scientists and clinicians at the OU Health Campus ( OUHC / OU Health ), the OU Institute for Biomedical Engineering, Science and Technology ( IBEST ), the Oklahoma Medical Research Foundation ( OMRF ), the Oklahoma Center for Geroscience and Healthy Brain Aging , the Laureate Institute for Brain Research ( LIBR ) in Tulsa, and the strategic 10-year Value Partnership between OU Health and Siemens Healthineers. These collaborations provide ample opportunities for interdisciplinary research and access to cutting-edge facilities and resources. Required: Doctoral Degree: A Ph.D. in Biomedical Engineering, or a closely related field. Commitment to Education and Teaching: Experience in teaching at the undergraduate level as instructor/faculty of record. Experience in lab courses is desirable. Desirable Qualifications: Effective Communication Skills: Demonstrated ability to communicate research and teaching concepts clearly and effectively to varied audiences Community Engagement: Interest in participating in community outreach or educational programs that enhance the visibility and impact of SBME. Engagement with Students: Interest in working closely with undergraduate students as mentor and career advisor. Upload a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and contact information of four references to be used with your permission at https://apply.interfolio.com/195324 . The cover letter may address examples of the candidate\u2019s approach to research, teaching, and service; it may also include plans for future contributions to research, as well as plans/goals for teaching at the University of Oklahoma. A review of the applications will begin , 2026 and will continue until final selections are made. Salary and benefits are nationally competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience. Competitive start-up packages will be available. Nominations or inquiries may be sent to Prof. Wei Chen, Director & Professor of Biomedical Engineering, wei-r-chen@ou.edu."}}, "rows": {"https://apply.interfolio.com/143792": {"school_id": 1096, "posting_url": "https://apply.interfolio.com/143792", "job_title_in_post": "Faculty Fellow", "position_type": "Research_Fellow", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "Department of Housing and Residence Life", "area_key_words": "", "deadline_of_application": "", "posted_date": "", "position_start_date": "